window.SMLE_QUESTIONS = [{"q_num": 1, "stem": "A patient was planned for total knee replacement and the surgeon was about to make the incision on the wrong leg, a nurse knew but kept quite, one of the staff checked the patient’s file before the incision and discovered it’s the wrong leg, how to prevent such case?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nurse shouldn’t say anything and assume that surgeon knows better"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Review the patient’s chart"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Proper marking prior to the surgery"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "(Patient safety & surgical error prevention). This case describes a near-miss wrong-site surgery, which is a serious and preventable medical error. According to WHO Surgical Safety Checklist, Joint Commission, and standard surgical safety protocols, the most effective preventive step is: ✅C- Proper site marking before surgery\nThe correct surgical site must be marked clearly.\nMarking is usually done by the operating surgeon.\nIt is performed:\nBefore the patient enters the operating room.\nWith the patient awake when possible.\nUsing an indelible marker.\nThis is a part of the Universal Protocol to prevent:\n- Wrong patient\n- Wrong procedure\n- Wrong site surgery", "id": 1, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2, "stem": "A screening mammogram is used as the reference standard for breast cancer detection in a population of 1,000 women. The mammogram identifies 200 women as having breast cancer. A new screening test is evaluated against the mammogram: It correctly identifies 180 of the women who were positive on mammography. It misses 20 women who were positive on mammography. Among the 800 women the new test identifies 50 women as false positive. What is the sensitivity of the new screening test?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "70%"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "80%"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "90%"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "100%"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 2, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 3, "stem": "A 9-week primigravida vegetarian since 4 years presented to the clinic with concerns about her fetus. Which vitamin is most important to assess?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Vitamin B12"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vitamin K"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Vitamin C"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Vitamin E"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "According to UpToDate and ACOG: A pregnant woman who has been vegetarian for several years is at risk of vitamin B12 deficiency, especially if she follows a strict vegetarian or vegan diet.\nVitamin B12 is important in pregnancy because Vitamin B12 is essential for: DNA synthesis & Red blood cell formation & Fetal neural development.\nDeficiency can lead to:\n- Megaloblastic anemia in the mother or Neural tube defects in infant or Developmental delay in the infant.\nVegetarians are at risk,,,,, Vitamin B12 is found mainly in: Meat, Fish, Eggs, Dairy products ,,,e.g\nLong-term vegetarians (especially vegans) may have low body stores.\nVitamin K: Deficiency is rare in pregnancy. Usually related to newborn hemorrhagic disease, not maternal diet.\nVitamin C: Found in fruits and vegetables. Vegetarians usually have adequate or high intake.\nVitamin E: Deficiency is very rare and not a typical concern in vegetarian pregnancy.\nIn pregnant vegetarians or vegans, always think of: ✅Vitamin B12 deficiency", "id": 3, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 11, "stem": "Old patient with DM and CKD and intermittent claudication (no symptoms of heart failure or chest pain) Echo normal All normal values Presented complaining of atrial fibrillation How to control ventricular rate", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Digoxin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lostran"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Metoprolol"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Diltiatzem"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "For atrial fibrillation (AF) with no heart failure and normal echocardiogram, the first-line drugs for ventricular rate control are:\n- Beta-blockers (e.g., metoprolol)\n- or Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (e.g., diltiazem, verapamil)\nWhy PAD Changes the Answer?\nIn patients with:\nPeripheral vascular disease\nRaynaud\nSevere claudication\nAvoid beta-blockers (especially non-selective ones). Even though metoprolol is β1 selective, exams prefer diltiazem in PAD.\nPeripheral arterial disease (PAD) = atherosclerotic narrowing of peripheral arteries → ↓ blood flow to legs → intermittent claudication\nThe problem in PAD is already: Poor peripheral circulation\nWhat Do Beta-Blockers Do?\nBeta receptors:\n•β1 → Heart (↓ HR, ↓ contractility)\n•β2 → Peripheral blood vessels (vasodilation)\nWhen you block β2:\n- You reduce vasodilation.\n- You allow unopposed α-mediated vasoconstriction.\n- Peripheral circulation may worsen.\nThis can increase claudication pain & worsen cold extremities\nWhat About Metoprolol?\nMetoprolol is β1-selective , So in real life:\n•It usually does NOT significantly worsen PAD\n•It is often safe\nBUT in board-style questions: AF + PAD\n→ Prefer non-dihydropyridine CCB (diltiazem)\n→ Avoid beta-blockers if another good option exists\nBecause:\n•CCBs do NOT reduce peripheral perfusion\n•They may even mildly vasodilate\nSo when both are acceptable for AF: Choose the drug that does NOT potentially worsen PAD\nThat is diltiazem.\nWhen two drugs can do the job: Avoid the one that worsens a comorbidity.\nHere:\n•Metoprolol → possible PAD worsening\n•Diltiazem → neutral for PAD\nSo answer = Diltiazem", "id": 4, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 12, "stem": "55 year old woman with DM HTN CKD with eGFR 50/ml/min. What is the goal BP to prevent cardiovascular disease?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "<155/100"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "<145/90"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "<135/80"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "<125/70"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 5, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 20, "stem": "Women with epilepsy is taking lamotrigine. She didn't have seizure attacks since 6 months. She has no personal history of a baby with NTD (neural tubal defect), but her cousin had a baby with spina bifida. What is the recommended folic acid supplementation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "5 mg folic acid during pregnancy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "5 mg folic acid 3-6 months before pregnancy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give aspirin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Folic acid 400 mg with multivitamins"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "Confirmed 100%D✅:", "id": 6, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 49, "stem": "An elderly patient receiving IV heparin develops deep vein thrombosis 6 days post-operatively. Platelet count is 75 ×109/L. What is the best next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Start warfarin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Start enoxaparin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Transfuse platelets"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Start argatroban"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT type II)\nThrombocytopenia + thrombosis 5–10 days after heparin = Stop heparin + Start argatroban✅\nHIT is an immune-mediated reaction :\n- Antibodies form against heparin–PF4 complex\n- Causes platelet activation\n- Leads to thrombosis (paradoxically) despite low platelets.\nThis is a prothrombotic state, not a bleeding problem.\nImmediately stop all heparin and Start a non-heparin anticoagulant D. Start argatroban\nArgatroban is:\n- A direct thrombin inhibitor\n- First-line treatment for HIT\nUpToDate\nAmerican Society of Hematology (ASH) 2018 HIT Guidelines\nRecommend:\n- Immediate discontinuation of all heparin\n- Start a non-heparin anticoagulant\nRecommended agents:\n- Argatroban✅\n- Bivalirudin\n- Fondaparinux\n- DOACs (in selected cases)\nDo NOT start warfarin until platelets recover (>150 ×10⁹/L)", "id": 7, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 53, "stem": "Child was riding a bicycle, and then he sustained a handlebar injury, he presented to the ER with abdominal pain and tenderness And Nausea & Vomiting FAST Was Done And It Was Inconclusive. What’s Most Likely Injured Abdominal Organ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Spleen."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Liver."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Pancreas. Soft tissue"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Urinary bladder"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Handlebar injury (direct epigastric blow) causes pancreatic injury, especially in children.\nFAST is often inconclusive because pancreatic injuries are retroperitoneal and may not produce significant free intraperitoneal fluid early.\nUpToDate: Blunt abdominal trauma in children: Bicycle handlebar injuries are classically associated with pancreatic trauma, often with minimal early findings and inconclusive FAST because the pancreas is retroperitoneal.\nAmerican College of Surgeons: ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support): Pancreatic injuries may not be detected on FAST and require CT abdomen with contrast if suspected.\nNelson Textbook of Pediatrics: Notes that epigastric handlebar impact in children commonly results in pancreatic injury.", "id": 8, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 79, "stem": "Baby after delivery went into respiratory distress, abnormal breathing pattern, tachypnea, saturation 78% , there were some meconium stains around the baby. What's next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oxygen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Observation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intubation and mechanical ventilation if needed"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "ABGs"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 9, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 94, "stem": "A 1-year-old infant presents to Vaccina9on Clinic with normal exam and growth What are the recommended vaccines according according to Saudi national program?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "OPV. MMR, HAV, PCV"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Varicella, PCV,HIB"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "OPV,MMR,MCV4, PCV"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "OPV,MMR, varicella, DTP"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 10, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 96, "stem": "Girl was playing in the garden and fall down then she LOC and start do vomit then become stable and present to ER What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Neuro consultation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Observation for 24h"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Discharge"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "97-Year Old Child Presents With 2 Months History Of Epigastric Pain That Wakes Him Up At Night, Mother Mentioned A History Of PUD, What Is The Confirmatory Test?\nA. CBC\nB. Abdominal US\nC. Upper GI Endoscopy With Biopsy\nD. H. Pylori Stool Antigen Only", "id": 11, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 121, "stem": "13-year-old obese boy presents with persistent hypertension documented on multiple visits. Examination reveals acanthosis nigricans over the neck and axillae. Patient also has [headache sweating and palpitations] What is the next step to establish diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Catecholamine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cortisone level and ACTH"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renal US"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ambulatory BP Monitoring"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "✔️Most likely diagnosis Pheochromocytoma:\nA catecholamine-secreting tumor causing episodic or persistent hypertension.\nObese with acanthosis nigricans (insulin resistance).\nThe triad is:\nHeadache + sweating + palpitations = pheochromocytoma until proven otherwise\nNext step to establish diagnosis\n✅ Measure catecholamines or metanephrines:\nPlasma free metanephrines (best screening test).\nor 24-hour urinary catecholamines/metanephrines.\nIf hypertension + episodic adrenergic symptoms appear in a child/adolescent:\nCheck catecholamines first = rule out pheochromocytoma.", "id": 12, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 126, "stem": "A pregnant woman did not receive the rubella vaccine prior to pregnancy. When is the appropriate time to administer the rubella vaccine?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Now"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "During the second trimester"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Postpartum"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 13, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 132, "stem": "A 78-year-old man known to have type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease is complaining of fatigue and nausea. He denies any change in consciousness, seizures, abdominal pain, vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath. He is on insulin, lisinopril, amlodipine, erythropoietin, and vitamin D. Examination reveals that he is drowsy but not in distress . Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? Hidden GB Labs Are: K: 6.2 (Or 6.3) , Na: 141", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "NaHCO3"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "D5 water"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Diuretics"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Calcium gluconate"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "If K ≥ 6 or symptoms/ECG concern = ✅Calcium gluconate FIRST, always.\nChronic kidney disease (CKD) ✅\nHyperkalemia → K⁺ = 6.2–6.3 mmol/L\nSymptoms: fatigue + drowsiness → possible potassium effect on cardiac conduction\nHyperkalemia ≥ 6.0 mmol/L or symptomatic = medical emergency.\nStabilize the cardiac membrane to prevent fatal arrhythmias.This is done with:✅Calcium gluconate⬇️\nActs within minutes.\nProtects myocardium.\nDoes NOT lower potassium , but prevents arrhythmia.\nHyperkalemia management order (VERY HIGH-YIELD)\n1- Protect heart : IV calcium gluconate ✅\n2- Shift K⁺ into cells : insulin + glucose, β-agonist, ± bicarbonate\n3- Remove potassium : diuretics, binders, dialysis\n📚 References:\nKidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes — Immediate cardiac stabilization with IV calcium in severe hyperkalemia.\nHarrison’s Principles of Internal Medicine — First step in significant hyperkalemia is membrane stabilization with calcium.", "id": 14, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 140, "stem": "A 35y old man complaining of sore throat, fatigue, body aches, and cough with white sputum. After it's associated with streaks of blood. Examination of cardio and chest normal. Chest X-Ray : normal. What's the diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bronchitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pulmonary edema"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Goodpasture syndrome"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Q A 36-year-old man, previously healthy, presented to primary healthcare complaining of cough, fatigue, sore throat, and body\naches. The cough is productive with whitish sputum; on a few occasions, there were some blood streaks. Chest examination was\nwithin normal limits . Chest X-ray: Normal, without evidence of consolidation.\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\nA. Bronchitis\nB. Pulmonary edema\nC. Goodpasture syndrome\nD. Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia", "id": 15, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 165, "stem": "Diabetic patients present with an inflamed, tender right leg, On examination: skin discoloration, Fluctuance. What is the most appropriate immediate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CBC"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Surgical exploration"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "MRI of the site"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CT abdomen"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Diabetic patient\nInflamed, tender leg\nSkin discoloration + fluctuance = suggests abscess or necrotizing infection\n- Rapid progression is possible in diabetics (especially necrotizing fasciitis).\nAccording to Infectious Diseases Society of America and UpToDate:\nFluctuance + skin changes in diabetic limb = surgical emergency\n- Immediate incision & drainage or surgical exploration is indicated.\n- Delay increases risk of sepsis, limb loss, or death.\nDiabetic + tender, discolored, fluctuant limb = think abscess/necrotizing infection = immediate surgical exploration.\n- Imaging/labs are adjuncts, not first-line in acute obvious cases.\n✔️Necrotizing Fasciitis is a rapidly progressive, life-threatening infection of the fascia and subcutaneous tissue, often sparing the muscle initially, that can lead to sepsis, tissue necrosis, and death if not treated promptly.\nRapid onset and progression.\nSevere pain out of proportion to skin findings.\nSkin changes: erythema, edema, discoloration, bullae.\nSystemic signs: fever, tachycardia, hypotension.\nFluctuance may appear later (abscess formation).\nOften occurs in immunocompromised patients (e.g., diabetics).\nCommon Causes:\nPolymicrobial: Type I (Gram-positive + Gram-negative + anaerobes).\nMonomicrobial: Type II (usually Group A Streptococcus).\nEntry point: trauma, surgical wounds, insect bites.\nDiagnosis:\nMostly clinical (do not delay for imaging if suspected).\nImaging (MRI/CT): helps if diagnosis uncertain.\nLabs: may show leukocytosis, elevated CRP, creatinine, or lactic acidosis.\nManagement (high-yield exam points)\n1. Immediate surgical debridement/exploration (life-saving)\n2. Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (cover Gram-positive, Gram-negative, anaerobes)\n3. Supportive care: flfluids, ICU if septic", "id": 16, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 166, "stem": "Female comes for screening of breast cancer asking what type of nipple discharge should worry her?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Spontaneous and bilateral red"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Spontaneous bilateral milky"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Induced unilateral green"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Spontaneous unilateral single duct and clear"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 17, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 169, "stem": "Patient present with lower limb weakness, back pain and urine incontinence T10 sensory level. What is the most appropriate test to diagnose?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "MRI spine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CT spine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Urine culture"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 18, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 170, "stem": "Hairdresser came to the emergency department with a swollen bulb of the thumb finger after getting a stick from the tools she’s using, Nail bed is intact?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Felon"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Paronychia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Onychomycosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cellulitis"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "A deep infection of the distal pulp (fingertip pad).\nCauses tense, swollen, very painful bulb of the finger.\nUsually follows a penetrating injury (like a stick from tools — common in hairdressers, barbers, etc.).\nNail bed is intact (important clue).\nParonychia = Infection around the nail fold\nOnychomycosis = Fungal infection of the nail.\nCellulitis = Diffuse skin infection, not localized tense pulp swelling.\nFelon is dangerous because the fingertip pulp is divided into multiple septal compartments, so pressure builds up = risk of ischemia and osteomyelitis.\nManagement:\nEarly: antibiotics.\nLate/abscess: incision and drainage.\nA felon is a deep bacterial infection of the distal pulp (fingertip pad) of a finger or thumb.\nThe fingertip pulp is divided into multiple tight fibrous septal compartments.\nPus builds up inside these closed spaces.\nPressure increases.\nThis can lead to ischemia, tissue necrosis, or even osteomyelitis of the distal phalanx.\nUsually follows a penetrating injury (needle stick, splinter, tool injury).\nMost common organism: Staphylococcus aureus.\nSevere throbbing pain.\nTense swelling of the fingertip pad (bulb).\nRedness.\nNail bed usually intact.\nMay have fever (late).\nTissue necrosis.\nOsteomyelitis.\nFlexor tenosynovitis (if spreads).\nEarly stage = oral antibiotics\nAbscess present = incision and drainage (I&D)", "id": 19, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 172, "stem": "When to give dose of antibiotic preoperatively in a pregnant woman with pprom. she was going to OR for CS?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "30 minutes before inscision"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "60 mint before incision"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "90 min"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "120 mint"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 20, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 174, "stem": "A 36-year-old woman with a BMI of 48 kg/m² presents to the infertility clinic. Both she and her husband have normal fertility investigations. They been married for one year. She has been counseled on weight loss before. What is the best health promotion and illness prevention advice for her?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bariatric surgery"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ozempic (semaglutide)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lifestyle modification and calorie restriction"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Observation"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 21, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 180, "stem": "A 67-year-old man a known case of HCV cirrhosis admitted to Emergency Room with massive hematemesis. Variceal bleeding is suspected. On examination on, he is drowsy with tense ascites . Blood pressure 110/70 mmHg Heart rate 86 /min Oxygen saturation on 95 % Test Result Normal Values Hb 8 130-170 g/L (Male) 120-160 g/L (Female) INR 1.3 0.8-1.2 Which of the following management option has a great benefit in reducing this patient mortality?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Octreotide"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "IV ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Blood transfusion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pantoprazole infusion"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 22, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 201, "stem": "A 22-year-old patent presented to the Emergency Department with history of expulsion of products of conception at home. On examination, cervix were closed with minimal vaginal bleeding. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Complete abortion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Inevitable abortion"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Threatened abortion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Incomplete abortion"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 23, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 211, "stem": "Newly Married Woman Has Been On Her Honeymoon For 1 Week And Is Now Complaining Of Dysuria And Frequency. How Will You Manage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ampicillin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Nitrofurantoin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ciprofloxacin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 24, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 215, "stem": "A 38-year-old man presents to the clinic complaining of severe epigastric pain that is worse after eating. He describes the pain as burning in nature with mild improvement with antacid therapy. Physical examination is unremarkable . Test Result Normal Values Hb 120 130-170 g/L (Male) 120-160 g/L (Female) Platelets count 450 150-400 x 109/L WBC 10 4.5-10.5 x 109/L Urea 8.5 2.75-7.4 mmol/L Creatinine 65 44-115 mol/L What is the most appropriate test to establish the diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT scan of the abdomen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Upper endoscopy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Barium swallow"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "H. pylori antibody"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 25, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 223, "stem": "A newborn presents with poor feeding, tongue fasciculations, and decreased deep tendon reflexes. What would you tell the parents?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The condition is mild and will resolve on its own."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A muscle biopsy is needed to choose the proper treatment."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "With good nutrition, life expectancy can increase."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "There is a new therapy that is more effective if started early."}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "Case of Spinal Muscular Atrophy (SMA) , especially Type 1 (Werdnig-Hoffmann disease).\nNewborn\nPoor feeding\nTongue fasciculations\nDecreased deep tendon reflexes\nHypotonia\nThese findings strongly suggest Spinal muscular atrophy, caused by SMN1 gene mutation.\nImportant counseling point (modern management):\nThere are now disease-modifying therapies, and outcomes are significantly better when started early (even pre-symptomatic).\nExamples include:\nNusinersen\nOnasemnogene abeparvovec\nRisdiplam\nEarly treatment dramatically improves survival and motor outcomes.\n✅ Correct answer: D. There is a new therapy that is more effective if started early.\nIf you see tongue fasciculations in a floppy baby = think SMA immediately.", "id": 26, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 225, "stem": "A female patient presents with features of Cushing's syndrome for 4 months. Laboratory tests show High cortisol 8am =840, 4pm= 500 High ACTH = 15 (high) TSH =4 (normal) What is the most likely diagnosis ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pituitary adenoma"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adrenal adenoma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Exogenous steroid use"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ectopic ACTH secretion"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 27, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 234, "stem": "Graivda 39 and obese and baby good weight what to do to avoid shoulder dystocia? ✅A. Suprapubic maneuver", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Gaskin maneuver"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Rubin maneuver"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A. Rubin maneuver"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "B. Suprapubic pressure"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "pregnant woman with diabetes mellitus and obesity is being evaluated for obstetric risk Which of the following is the most appropriate maneuver to help prevent shoulder dystocia during delivery?\nC. Gaskin maneuver\nD. Zavanelli maneuver\nAnswer B✅", "id": 28, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 237, "stem": "Gravida 41weeks child deliver via NVD and had respiratory distress but not saturated what next?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "30-40 %oxygen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "NPO"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Surfactant"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Give magnesium"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "A. 30–40% oxygen ✅First-line management is supplemental oxygen for hypoxia.\nC. Surfactant: Used mainly in preterm RDS, not a 41-week baby.\nD. Give magnesium: Magnesium is not used for neonatal respiratory distress.", "id": 29, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 244, "stem": "Child contact with his maid she had TB, the child +ve igra +ve x ray but no Clinical findings. What should treatment for child? (Picture of lung TB)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Rifampicin and isoniazid for 3 months"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Isoniazid for 6 months"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "RIPE"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "IGRA = Interferon-Gamma Release Assay\nactive TB disease So treatment should be full anti-TB therapy.\nAccording to World Health Organization and pediatric TB guidelines:✅Correct answer: C. RIPE\nStandard initial therapy for active pulmonary TB:\nRifampicin\nIsoniazid\nPyrazinamide\nEthambutol\n(2 months intensive phase followed by continuation phase with RI)\nIGRA +, CXR normal → Latent TB → INH 6 months (or 3HR).\nIGRA +, CXR abnormal → Active TB → RIPE.", "id": 30, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 256, "stem": "Pregnant woman in her 38th week presented with 4 cm dilation and breech presentation. 2 hours later there was no change. CTG is reactive. (everything generally fine)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Wait 2 more hours"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Emergency CS"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "External cephalic version"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Induction of labor"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 31, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 259, "stem": "A 17-year-old girl presents with a 6-month history of irritability, poor concentration, tension, and anxiety. She is highly active on social media, posting daily. What is the most appropriate initial management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Decrease time on the phone to 6 hours"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Encourage her to avoid using social media"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 32, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 260, "stem": "30-year-old woman with a history of infertility for 6 years complains of severe dysmenorrhea unrelieved by naproxen. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Endometritis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Leiomyomata"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Endometriosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pelvic congestion"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 33, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 263, "stem": "Most common subtype HPV in cervical cancer?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "11 /12"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "16 /18"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "32/ 36"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "44"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 34, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 268, "stem": "A pregnant woman is admitted for labor. After continuous pushing, she is now exhausted. The baby is engaged at +2 station and shows minimal variability with late deceleration The mother exhausted and can't push What is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cesarean section"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Operative vaginal delivery"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reassessing after 1 hour"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Oxytocin"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 35, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 269, "stem": "A mother is worried about her son who has asthma. What is the most appropriate counseling?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "He will develop chronic lung disorder"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Childhood asthma will disappear by adolescence"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "He will have asthma for the rest of his life"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "No correlation between childhood and adulthood asthma"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 36, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 270, "stem": "Female with 6 weeks amenorrhea, bhcg was 700, they gave a table with range of bhcg for weeks 0-4, 0-6 and 0-7. Her bhcg was in normal range according to the table they gave, but TVUS showed no intrauterine pregnancy, what will you do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give methotrexate"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Repeat bhcg in 48 hours"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Repeat US in 48 hours"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 37, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 272, "stem": "Middle aged man came with metal objects lodged in his eye. What to do next?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Referral to ophthalmology"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Removal of object and irrigation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antibiotic and Out-Patient follow up"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Apply patch"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 38, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 274, "stem": "Newborn 2 days old came with lethargy, hypotension and pallor. History of Ventose used during delivery. Had head swelling that cross the suture lines. Hb 7.5. Normal platelets normal PTT PT?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Observe and serial Hb measurement"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Blood transfusion and fluid resuscitation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Vit K"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Hb 7.5 g/dL → severe anemia for a newborn\nVacuum-assisted delivery → risk of birth trauma\nHead swelling crossing suture lines → caput succedaneum (benign, resolves spontaneously)\nLethargy, hypotension, pallor, Hb 7.5 → significant blood loss / anemia\nNormal coagulation → not Vitamin K deficiency bleeding\nManagement:\nSeverely anemic, symptomatic neonate = requires urgent blood transfusion and fluid resuscitation.\nObservation alone → unsafe (Hb <8, hypotensive, lethargic)\nVitamin K → unnecessary here (PT/PTT normal, no bleeding).\nNelson Textbook of Pediatrics\nHemodynamically unstable newborns with Hb <8 → blood transfusion indicated.\nVacuum deliveries → more likely caput or cephalohematoma. Severe anemia in first 1–2 days usually indicates internal bleeding or significant birth trauma.", "id": 39, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 275, "stem": "30-year-old male presented with polydipsia and polyuria, has positive family history of type1 diabetes mellitus; labs show HbA1c 8.5%, C-peptide 0.09 (low), high parathyroid hormone (PTH), high calcium. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Type 1 diabetes mellitus"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Central diabetes insipidus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Maturity onset diabetes of the young MOD"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Polydipsia and polyuria → suggests diabetes.\nHbA1c = 8.5% → confirms diabetes mellitus.\nC-peptide = 0.09 (very low) → indicates little or no endogenous insulin production.\nFamily history of type 1 DM.\nLow C-peptide = Type 1 DM.\nNormal/high C-peptide = Type 2 DM أو MODY.\nType 1 diabetes mellitus , Most likely (Correct)\nIn Type 1 DM, autoimmune destruction of pancreatic β-cells occurs.\nThis leads to very low or absent C-peptide because C-peptide reflects endogenous insulin secretion.\nAlthough it commonly presents in younger patients, it can occur in adults (sometimes called LADA – latent autoimmune diabetes in adults).\nNote about high calcium and high PTH: This suggests primary hyperparathyroidism, which may be a separate condition and does not change the diabetes diagnosis in this question.", "id": 40, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 276, "stem": "A 9-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with pain between her legs after falling from a bicycle. She was screaming from pain in between her legs. She is accompanied by her mother. On examination, with the presence of her mother a bluish hematoma is noted in the left labia. What is the most appropriate management? ( no mention of LARGE)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Topical Ice placement"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Prophylactic antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Examination under anesthesia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Surgical evacuation of hematoma"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 41, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 277, "stem": "25 year old female presented with 2 day history of blurry vision, weakness and numbness. Denied hx of drug abuse, fever, or similar symptoms in the family. She admits seeking medical help for it before and the findings were. MRI: periventicular white matter CSF: Oligocolonal bands What is the most appropriate management to do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Rituximab"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Interferon beta"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prednisone 50 mg OD"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "IV Methylprednisolone 1000"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 42, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 278, "stem": "50-year-old man presents for a routine follow-up.He has no history of cardiovascular disease, diabetes, or hypertension. On *high dose of atrovstatins* His physical examination is unremarkable. Laboratory investigations reveal: cholesterol: within normal range Triglycerides: significantly elevated (2.8) normal (2.2) What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Increase Statin dose"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Omega-3 fatty acids"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Niacin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Evolocumab"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 43, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 279, "stem": "Elderly women with previous laparotomy for sigmoidectomy after complicated diverticulitis, she had an mild incisional hernia (There was mention of cough impulse and that it doesn't bother her but not exactly) It became painful with exercise and expanded. But no signs of strangulation (this was stated in the scenario) What is the best action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reaassure"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Open repair without mesh"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Laproscopic repair"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Laproscopic repair with mesh reinforcement"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "Asymptomatic hernia → observation possible\nSymptomatic or enlarging hernia → surgical repair\nIncisional hernia → mesh repair (often laparoscopic).\n280 281 282 283 284 285 286 287 288 289 290", "id": 44, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 350, "stem": "A 40-year-old male, 5-pack-year smoker presents to the Emergency Room with a sudden onset of retrosternal chest pain radiating to the neck. His blood pressure is 150/92 ECG: ST-segment depression in II, III, and aVF. Which of the following is the most considerable risk factor?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Smoking"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Male gender"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Patient's age"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hypertension"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 45, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 352, "stem": "Pediatric boy presents with low-set ears, hypertelorism, webbed neck. On examination: pulmonary stenosis and came with history of failure to thrive. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refer to cardiology and genetic testing"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Refer to endocrinology and measure growth hormone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "C- Refer to gastroenterology"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 46, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 355, "stem": "A 36-year-old male presents to the emergency room with right abdominal pain. On examination, he is febrile, toxic, and has tenderness in the right upper quadrant and lower intercostal margins. He reports anorexia and weight loss. Laboratory findings: Elevated temperature (~37.9°C) High WBC count. Elevated bilirubin Ultrasound: cystic lesion with thick wall in the liver What is the most appropriate step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Metronidazole"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Surgical drainage"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Percutaneous drainage"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 47, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 256, "stem": "Caes of child with Leukocoria with well-demarcated retinal lesions and calcifications Ask about diagnosis:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Toxocariasis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Retinoblastoma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Coats disease"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Congenital rubella"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 48, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 357, "stem": "Years old girl came complaining of irregular cycle and hirsutism. On examination only there is hirsutism. Lab tests of TSH, dehydroepiandrosterone, testosterone only done and all are normal. What is the management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Spironolactone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Colmiphene citrate"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Progesterone pill only"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Oral contraceptive pills"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 49, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 358, "stem": "A patient with a history of Alzheimer’s disease has been on donepezil for 6 months, and no improvement was noticed. His MMSE was 19 (six months ago) and is now 12. MRI show hippocampus atrophy What is the next best step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add sertraline"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add Memantine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add Rivastigmine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Switch Donepezil to memantine"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 50, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 359, "stem": "Patient had chemotherapy, after 1 day developed fever, labs showed low WBC and low neutrophils What to do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IV antibiotics"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Close observation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antibiotic and antiviral"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Oral antibiotic and antifungal"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 51, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 361, "stem": "Description of epidemiology case + a new screening test was developed to even include the missed cases. Which one is high?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Specificity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Sensitivity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Positive predictive value"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Negative predictive value"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 52, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 363, "stem": "A mother of a 2-month-old infant complains that her baby has frequent episodes of excessive crying, 3 hours raise legs and pass gas. She asks how to treat?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Simethicone drops"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Soothing like motion, white noise."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antibiotics"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "US abdomen"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 53, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 364, "stem": "A group of researchers are doing a study by gathering people with Alzheimer’s disease and giving them fish oil. What is the primary approach that is used by the IRB to assess the ethical acceptability of the research study? a. Utilitarianism", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Deontology"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ethical skepticism"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Comparativeism"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 54, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 367, "stem": "A 30 yo patient with persistent HTN despite being on 2 antihypertensive medications. Laboratory investigations show Hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis (elevated DH) What is the most appropriate next step to identify the underlying cause of hypertension?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "24-hour urine cortisol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Renin:aldosterone ratio"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Plasma metanephrines"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Renal artery Doppler ultrasound"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 55, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 368, "stem": "Regarding febrile convulsions in children, which of the following statements is correct?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "There is a 2% chance to develop epilepsy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "If it is a complex febrile seizure, it is more likely to progress to epilepsy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "If parents had febrile seizures, it will increase the chance to progress to epilepsy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Using antipyretics will decrease the chance of febrile Convulsions"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 56, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 369, "stem": "A 55 YO male underwent PTC (percutaneous transhepatic cholangiography) for obstructive jaundice. After 24 hours of the procedure he developed hematemesis. His vitals are stable. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT abdomen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Upper GI endoscopy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "US"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Angiography"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "Best diagnostic/management step or active bleeding Often Angiography, which can diagnose and treat the bleeding via embolization.\n⬆️Explanation: The recent procedure: Percutaneous Transhepatic Cholangiography (PTC) followed by hematemesis after 24 hours. It’s the most likely complication of PTC.\nPTC can cause hemobilia (bleeding into the biliary tree) due to injury of a hepatic vessel. Blood passes through the bile duct to duodenum and presents as hematemesis or melena.\nClassic triad of hemobilia (Quincke triad):\n1- RUQ pain\n2- Jaundice\n3- GI bleeding\nAfter hepatobiliary procedures (PTC, liver biopsy, ERCP), hemobilia is a known complication.\nNext step:\nPt is hemodynamically stable, the best initial diagnostic step is ✅Upper GI endoscopy\n- It is the first-line investigation for hematemesis.\n- It can show blood coming from the ampulla of Vater, suggesting hemobilia.\n- It also excludes other common causes of upper GI bleeding (ulcer, varices).\nCT abdomen is not first-line for acute hematemesis.\nUltrasound is low sensitivity for hemobilia.\nAngiography is used after diagnosis or if bleeding persists, and allows embolization.\nReference:\n- Schwartz’s Principles of Surgery\n- Sabiston Textbook of Surgery\n- American College of Gastroenterology guidelines for upper GI bleeding.\n✔️Post-hepatobiliary procedure + hematemesis = think hemobilia, first test = Upper GI endoscopy\n✔️Definitive diagnosis & therapy = Angiography with embolization.", "id": 57, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 370, "stem": "A 73-year-old woman underwent percutaneous trans-hepatic cholangiography for treatment of obstructive jaundice. 24 hours after the procedure, she started to have upper gastrointestinal bleeding . Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the management plan? Blood pressure 105/62 mmHg Heart rate 89 /min Respiratory rate 22 /min Oxygen saturation 94 % Test Result Normal Values Hb 103 130-170 g/L (Male) 120-160 g/L (Female) WBC 7.8 4.5-10.5 x 109/L", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT scan"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Endoscopy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ultrasound"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "AngiographyMG"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 58, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 371, "stem": "DM patient came with dental infection and subsequently develops jaundice and chills. Ultrasound reveals a 6 cm hypoechoic lesion in the liver. What is the most appropriate initial management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oral antibiotics"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Percutaneous drainage"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Anti-fungal and follow up"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 59, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 374, "stem": "A newborn presents with persistent bleeding after circumcision that does not stop with compression. There is a positive family history of a bleeding disorder affecting both his brother and mother. (no labs). What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hemophilia A"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Von willebrand disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Platelet disorder"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 60, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 388, "stem": "5 y old with persistent otitis media with effusion and conductive hearing loss is for surgical consideration?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Masteidectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adenoidectomy alone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Myringotomy alone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Insertion of tympanostomy tubes (grommet)"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 61, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 389, "stem": "A child presents with reduced tympanic membrane movement, and the school report notes decreased hearing. What is the most appropriate initial management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nasal decongestants and antihistamines"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Amoxicillin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refer for audiology assessment"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Insert tympanostomy tubes"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "A macrosomic baby (large baby, often due to maternal diabetes) who has a weak or absent Moro reflex suggests birth trauma, most commonly Brachial Plexus Injury such as Erb’s Palsy.This usually happens after shoulder dystocia during delivery of a large baby.\nNext step Initial management is conservative:\nImmobilization of the affected arm.\nSupportive management.\nFollowed later by physiotherapy.\nSurgery is only considered later (3–6 months) if there is no recovery.✅Correct answer: 1. Immobilization and supportive management", "id": 62, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 396, "stem": "A 64-year-old man presented to Emergency Room with symptoms of dyspnoea, SOB, and palpitation. He is a known case of diabetic mellitus, hypertension and hyperlipidaemia. He is smoker with history of stroke. On examination, found to have irregular pulse. Cardiac and chest examination showed high JVP, S3 and bi-basal crackles. Atrial fibrillation on ECG. Blood pressure 150/90 mmH Heart rate 144 /min Oxygen saturation 89 % What is the CHADS2 score of this patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "3"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "4"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "5"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "CHF: Yes → S3, crackles, high JVP → 1 point\nHypertension: Yes → 1 point\nAge ≥75: No (64 y) → 0 points\nDiabetes mellitus: Yes → 1 point\nStroke history: Yes → 2 points", "id": 63, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 402, "stem": "A child presents with vomiting and diarrhea about seven times a day, has very dry skin and mucous membranes, sunken eyes, delayed capillary refill of 4 seconds, lethargy, and hypotension, What type of dehydration does this child have?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Mild dehydration"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Moderate dehydration"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hypovolemic shock"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "No dehydration"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 64, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 417, "stem": "A 4-year-old girl was bitten by a newly adopted cat. The cat is febrile and lethargic. There is no redness at the bite site. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassure"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give prophylactic antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Irrigate wound [ONLY]"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Give rabies immunoglobulin and vaccine"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "A patient was bitten by a bat , what is the most likely infection?\nA. Rabies\nB. Tetanus", "id": 65, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 420, "stem": "A patient presented to the ED after he was stabbed in his chest. Upon examination he had hypotension, tachycardia, muffled heart sounds, and distended neck veins. Lungs were clear bilaterally. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Myocardial contusion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hemothorax"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tension pneumothorax"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cardiac tamponade"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 66, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 429, "stem": "Known case of DM, HTN with 6 months history of impairment of extraocular muscles, nystagmus, ataxia, dysarthria What is the affected area?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Basilar artery"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Anterior Cerebral"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Middle Cerebral"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Posterior Cerebral"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 67, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 430, "stem": "A 5-month-old infant presents with a 10-day history of cough and episodes of cyanosis, accompanied by post-tussive vomiting. Laboratory results show a × white blood cell count of 27 L with 80% lymphocytes. Which of the following is the most appropriate management", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Azithromycin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Nebulized salbutamol"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Corticosteroid"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 68, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 431, "stem": "A 24-week pregnant woman presents with exertional dyspnea. She is otherwise healthy, and systemic examination is unremarkable. Ultrasound shows monochorionic twin pregnancy with evidence of twin- to twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS). What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Termination of pregnancy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Expectant management with observation only"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Laser photocoagulation of placental vascular anastomoses"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cesarean section"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 69, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 433, "stem": "26 y.o man with history of fatigue for one month. He was diagnosed with AML 5Ma, and one day before starting chemotherapy, he had high grade fever. Labs show WBC 3 (low), neutrophils 0.2% (low), Hgb 6 (low), platelet 100 (low) What is the best medication to start with?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Floroquinolone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Granulocyte stimulating factor"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Extended spectrum penicillin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Third generation cephalosporin"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 70, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 434, "stem": "Patient in labor, everything normal GA 33 , head felt in vagina", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Forceps"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ventouse"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CS"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Fetal sampling"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 71, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 436, "stem": "A 22 years old woman with a history of multiple sexual transmitted diseases presented at the 37th gestational week for her first antenatal visit. The obstetrician is concerned about this maternal infection which may cause conjunctivitis and blindness In the newborn. Which of the following is the most suitable for health promotion and illness prevention?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassurance"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Azithromycin administration"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Newborn screening after delivery"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Referral to infectious disease clinic"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 72, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 438, "stem": "A Junior doctor in a private hospital notices senior doctor manipulating documentation and doing unnecessary procedures for money. Junior reports him, issue subsides. Now senior gives junior an alarming letter — What to do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Go silent, avoid confrontation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Go to senior directly"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Document the misconduct to preserve your legal rights"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 73, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 440, "stem": "A 23 year old woman with 4 month diarrhea, passing mucus with anemia and having family history of colon cancer her father at 60 year old came with abdominal pain relieving by defecation. ESR normal. What is the diagnosis ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Irritable bowel syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Colon cancer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ulcerative colitis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Gastroenteritis"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 74, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 441, "stem": "45 yr old Female patient with PCOS as king about what is her high risk for developing endometrial cancer:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Age"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "PCO"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Multiparty"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 75, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 442, "stem": "A 18 months old presenting with progressive pallor, SOB, fatigue and dactylitis. Labs showed Hb 70, high reticulocyte. What is the confirmatory diagnostic test?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Direct coomb test"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Osmotic fragility test"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "LDH"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 76, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 443, "stem": "62-Year-Old Woman With Atrial Fibrillation On Warfarin Develops A Subdural Hematoma. Her INR = 3.9, And She Has Already Received Vitamin K. The Hematoma Requires Urgent Surgical Evacuation. What Is The Most Appropriate Next Step In Management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Restart Warfarin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Activated Factor VII (rFVIa)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "4-Factor Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC)"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 77, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 444, "stem": "A newborn presents with microcephaly and a distended abdomen. Physical examination reveals hepatosplenomegaly. Natal history is significant for maternal diabetes. Laboratory investigations show pancytopenia and elevated AST/ALT levels. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Chromosomal analysis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Congenital infection screening"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Abdominal ultrasound"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CT abdomen"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 78, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 445, "stem": "Pt diabetic and hypertensive ‹ came to the diabetic clinic for follow up, he is DM and HTN is well controlled, HA1c: 7.2 ( not 100% sure ) but he admitted that he missed multiple appointments last year, his doctor is worried What medical value the doctor trying to achieve ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Efficiency"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Timeliness"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Patient centredness"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 79, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 446, "stem": "Ptn with non-hodgkin lymphoma and he passed away before going through chemotherapy by 1 week will he died before his chemotherapy session and he is known case of uncontrolled DM2 for 20 years and HTN. Labs: high uric acid, high potassium What's the cause of his death? A-Drug induced nephropathy", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tumor lysis syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Undiscovered renal failure"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renal tubular acidosis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 80, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 448, "stem": "3 years old boy came to the clinic with poor weight gain . Over the past 4 months has loose diarrhea and intermittent abdominal pain. Physical examination and vital signs is normal. His weight on 3rd percentile and his height on 15th percentile. In the past year his weight was 36th percentile. His Abdomen is distended, non tender and normal bowel sounds Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Blood gas"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CBC"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tissue Transglutaminase IgA"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Genetic test"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 81, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 449, "stem": "Pregnant presented with severe abdominal pain and moderate vaginal bleeding. 33 gestational week, she was hypotensive, ctg is reassurance, her bleeding did not stopped since admission. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Blood transfusion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Induction of labor"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give dexa and observe"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Immediate CS after one dose of dexa"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 82, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 450, "stem": "Post menopause with a family history of ovarian cancer comes for screening for ovarian cancer What do you do for her?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pelvic US"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CA125"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Counseling and ask about ovarian cancer symptoms"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 83, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 451, "stem": "A pregnant lady at 26weeks, she cannot feel fetal movement anymore. US showed : IUFD at age of 24 weeks Best management:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IV oxytocin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "C section"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Misoprostol"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Dilation and curettage"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 84, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 452, "stem": "An elderly patient presents with exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, and signs of heart failure. Echocardiography shows concentric left ventricular hypertrophy with a normal ejection fraction (60%). What is the most likely diagnosis? A- Restrictive cardiomyopathy", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Constrictive pericarditis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Dilated cardiomyopathy"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 85, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 473, "stem": "A 19-year-old male with known hepatitis B presents with bilateral knee pain for 2 days and has been extensively taking paracetamol. Serology: HBsAb negative, HBsAg positive, HBeAg negative, HBc IgG positive Labs: AST >2000, ALT >1000, Bilirubin 27 (normal 5.1–17.0), INR 2.something What is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IV corticosteroid"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "N-Acetylcysteine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Entecavir to treat acute hepatitis B"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Plasmapheresis with Penicillamine"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 86, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 488, "stem": "Probably Confirmed Recall (We Didn’t Find Her Mistake Yet): child was diagnosed with acute glomerulonephritis, and the parents are asking, When does the C3 level return to normal?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1 to 2 weeks"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "3 to 4 weeks"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "6 to 8 WEEKS"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 87, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 520, "stem": "Woman presented with menorrhagia. Pelvic us confirms the presence of uterine fibroid. What is the most likely location of this fibroid?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cervical"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Subserosal"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intramural"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Submucosal"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 88, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 550, "stem": "29-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with slurred speech. She has a known history of depression and alcohol use disorder, and she reports occasional cravings for mariuana. On exam, she is drowsy, slurring her words, and smells of alcohol. Vitals are stable. Best next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urine drug screen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Liver function tests"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IV thiamine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Head CT"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 89, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 558, "stem": "-the appropriate dose of folic acid is planning to get pregnant?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "5"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "15"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "20"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 90, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 578, "stem": "45-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a 3-day history of fever and a productive cough of yellowish sputum. Medical history is significant for type 2 diabetes mellitus. He is a smoker. His chest examination reveals decreased breath sounds throughout and crackles at the right lung base. Other systemic examination is unremarkable . Chest radiograph: Right lower lobe infiltrate. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Start ceftriaxone and azithromycin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Start vancomycin and azithromycin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antiviral and azithromycin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Meropenem and azithromycin"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "D✅Gameboy", "id": 91, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 619, "stem": "A 45-year-old man presented to the Emergency Room with palpitations. Initially, he was conscious and oriented. His vital signs were as shown. After some time, he became less responsive, and his blood pressure dropped to 80/45 mmHg . ECG: Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pacemaker"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cardioversion"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Propranolol"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Radiofrequency ablation"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "An 8-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department. He is alert, with a blood pressure of 75/50 mmHg and a heart rate of 130 beats per minute. His ECG is shown below.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\nA. Adenosine\nB. Amiodarone\nC. Vagal stimulation\nD. Synchronized electrical cardioversion\nUnstable px with SVT", "id": 92, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 650, "stem": "63-year-old man presents with multiple psychiatric symptoms, including prominent visual hallucinations. He started on donepezil, which improved his cognition but visual hallucinations persist. what to add?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sertraline"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Quetiapine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Amitriptyline"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Stop donepezil"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 93, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 779, "stem": "A 7-year-old boy presents with a pharyngitis, fever, and a diffuse, erythematous, sandpaper-like rash that began on his trunk and has now spread to his extremities. His tongue appears bright red (“strawberry tongue”). What is the most likely causative organism?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Parvovirus B19"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Staphylococcus aureus"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Streptococcus pyogenes"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Herpes simplex"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Diagnosis is: Scarlet Fever\nTreatment is Augmentin/ penicillin", "id": 94, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 798, "stem": "Patient 11 y with every symptom of active SLE malar rash and active arthritis but no cns on renal problems 100% sure the labs all are the classic labs ex c3 was low and creatinine normal but the patient is not on any drug and they did not say he was diagnosed before What is the management now?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Azathioprine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hydroxychloroquine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cyclophosphamide"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mycophenolate mofetil"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 95, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 824, "stem": "A 2 year old boy his father brought him to the clinic for elective circumcision. What is the best next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Do full investigation including coagulation profile."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Wait til he became able to make the decision by himself then."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refer to pediatric surgery."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Performs it on the clinic."}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 96, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 846, "stem": "A 34-Year-Old Man Previously Healthy Brought A Laboratory Results For Hepatitis B Virus From A Private Clinic (SeeLab Results). Test Result HBAg Negative HBeAg Negative Anti-HBsAg Positive Anti-HBc IgG Negative What Is The Clinical State The Laboratory Results Indicate?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Previous Hepatitis B Infection"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Acute Hepatitis B Infection"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Chronic Hepatitis B Infection"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Previous Hepatitis B Immunization"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 97, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 852, "stem": "Baby born by cessarian section He is preterm 27 weeker * Now he has respiratory distress", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hyaline membrane disease"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Meconium aspiration"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Transient tachypnea of newborn"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Congenital heart disease"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 98, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 871, "stem": "Young female w cardiac disease on warfarin, contraception?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Estrogen and progesterone patch"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "COC"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tubal ligation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Progesterone only pills"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 99, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 875, "stem": "25 YO complain of 3 months abdominal pain, bloody DIARRHEA, weight loss.Mother and brother have same complain Guaiac stool test: positive", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ulcerative colitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Familial adenomatous polyposis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Diverticulosis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 100, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 876, "stem": "A 55-year-old man is seen in the Emergency Department complaining of lethargy for the last 2 days. On physical examination, vital signs are stable with unremarkable physical examination. ECG shows no acute changes . Test Result Normal Values Sodium 139 134-146 mmol/L Potassium 6.6 3.5-5.1 mmol/L Bicarbonate 15 21-28 mmol/L Blood urea nitrogen 9 2.8 to 8.9 mmol/L Creatinine 118 44-115 mol/L Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Haemodialysis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Intravenous bicarbonate"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Insulin dextrose infusion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Intravenous normal saline"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 101, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 890, "stem": "A Young Female Presents With Joint Pain Located In Her Distal Interphalangeal Joints And Knees. On Examination, You Notice That Her Fingers Are Sausage shaped. Which Of The Following Is Expected To Be Seen In This Patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Dermatologic Problem"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pulmonary Edema"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "SLE"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "RA"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 102, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 892, "stem": "A 1-month-old baby is brought to the clinic with signs of constipation, abdominal distention, and bilious vomiting. The parents explain that constipation has been a problem since the baby's birth. Which of the following tests has the highest diagnostic value?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Plain X-ray"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Rectal biopsy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Anal manometry"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "US abdomen"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 103, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 897, "stem": "Patient K/C DM HTN and PAD. Presents to the ER complaining of sudden onset leg pain and paresthesia. On examination: diminished pulse. What is the most appropriate initial investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT Angiography"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "ABI"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Doppler"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 104, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 924, "stem": "What is the Folic acid quantity for a healthy lady wants to conceive and with no prior diseases or disorders?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "5"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "10"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "15"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 105, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 947, "stem": "A child living in a village presented with a history of drinking unpasteurized milk.", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "HSV 1"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "HSV 2"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Brucellosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Dangue"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 106, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 970, "stem": "9 year old girl suspected of appendicitis (they stated that), complain of right lower quadrant pain, no fever no anorexia and no rebound tenderness - WBC 9 What is the best thing to do to avoid missing the diagnosis", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Admit and do serial examination and repeat lap in 6-12 hrs"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CT"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Discharge patient with given instructions when to come back"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Open Appendectomy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 107, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 979, "stem": "Pt presents with fever stuffed nose headaches for which he was diagnosed with bacterial sinusitis but has shown no improvement after 72hrs of using amoxicillin What to do now", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refer to ENT"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CT Sinuses"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Change medication to Amoxicillin clavulanate for 10 days"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Change diagnosis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 108, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 998, "stem": "A 25-year-old patient sustained a blunt trauma to the right chest wall; there was paradoxical movement of the right side of the chest . Heart rate 96 /min Respiratory rate 35 /min Oxygen saturation 84 % Chest X-ray: Fracture 2nd through 4th ribs at more than 1 site. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management? A- Fluid resuscitation", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Assisted ventilation and analgesia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Needle decompression"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Thoracostomy chest tube"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "25 year old patient sustained a blunt trauma to the right chest wall. There was paradoxical movement of the right side of the chest. Heart rate 96/min. Respiratory rate 35 breath per minute, Oxygen saturation 84% chest x-ray fracture 2nd to 4th ribs at more than one site. Which of the following is the most important initial management?"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "A.Fluid resuscitation\nB.Assistant ventilation\nC.Needle decompression\nD.Thoracostomy chest tube.", "id": 109, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1020, "stem": "A 4 months old baby presents with monophasic expiratory wheeze. He is at the 50th percentile for weight and normal height. There is no respiratory distress. These features suggest tracheomalacia. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nebulized short-acting beta-agonist (SABA)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Systemic steroids"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reassure"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Tracheostomy"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 110, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1044, "stem": "A 23-year-old woman presented at 10 weeks of gestation with a history of vaginal bleeding, cramp-like pain, and passage of some placental tissue through the cervix. Which of the following is the most likely type of abortion?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Complete"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Inevitable"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Incomplete"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Threatened"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 111, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 413, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman G2 P1 L1, whose last menstrual period was 7 weeks ago, presented to the Emergency Room with lower abdominal pain and moderate vaginal bleeding, passing tissue. Examination revealed an open cervix with tissue seen at the cervix. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Missed abortion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ectopic pregnancy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Healthy pregnancy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Incomplete abortion"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 112, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 316, "stem": "A 25-year-old pregnant woman presented with a history of vaginal bleeding and passed tissue at home. Examination revealed an open cervix. Ultrasound: Intrauterine tissue. Which of the following is the most likely type of abortion she had?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Complete"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Inevitable"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Incomplete"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Threatened"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 113, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1056, "stem": "What is the most important vaccine in first antenatal visit", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tdap"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Influnza"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Meningioccocal"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hbv"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 114, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1099, "stem": "A 50-year-old woman presents with menorrhagia for the last year. The physical examination is unremarkable. A trial of medical treatment failed to control the bleeding for the last few months. Ultrasound: Presence of a fibroid, 7 x 7 cm in size, which is distorting the endometrial lining. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hysterectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Endometrial ablation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Uterine artery embolization"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hormonal intrauterine contraceptive device"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 115, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1116, "stem": "Post chemo therapy , 7 days of fever and neutropenia, Choice of treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IV ampho.. ( anti-fungal)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vancomycin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Azithromycin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ciprofloxacin"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 116, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 1190, "stem": "Down syndrome delivered 2 days ago, what is the recommended screening for this patient:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Serial abdominal x ray for another atresia “not duodenal”"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Serial US for pyloric stenosis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Observe meconium passage 24-48 hours"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Fecal calprotectin for necrotizing fasciitis"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 117, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1196, "stem": "Preterm rom was given a medication for prevention of respiratory distress. What is the medication?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Steroids"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ibuprofen"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antibiotics"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Paracetamol"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 118, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 1219, "stem": "4 year old boy came with barking cough and on auscultation there was monophonic wheeze and he was cyanotic with no inspiratory stridor?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bronchiolitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tracheomalacia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Laryngiomalacia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Asthma"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 119, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1223, "stem": "A 21-year-old primigravida at 40 weeks of gestation with breech presentation is planned for a cesarean section. During surgery, a right ovarian cyst measuring 5 cm is observed. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cystectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Oophorectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "No intervention"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Squeeze and rupture"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 120, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1224, "stem": "Child came to the clinic regarding poor nutrition, he is always eating biscuits and milk and not eating well. On examination there is mild conjunctival pallor, Hb is 6.2 mg/dl with low MCV. What is the most appropriate management for this child?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Immediate blood transfusion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give oral iron and advise diet modification"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bone marrow aspiration"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Folate with multivitamins"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 121, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1238, "stem": "Folic acid dose for pregnant lady with no risk factors?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0.2"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "0.8"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "1.4"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "2.4"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 122, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1242, "stem": "A 7-year-old child presents with pneumonia. What is the most common causative organism?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Escherichia coli"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Group B Streptococcus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Escherichia coli"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 123, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1400, "stem": "Pregnant lady in the last trimester Dx with peripartum cardiomyopathy what to give", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Losartan"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lisinopril"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hydralazine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Methyldopa"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Peripartum cardiomyopathy is a form of systolic heart failure occurring in the last month of pregnancy or within 5 months postpartum.\nManagement during pregnancy follows heart failure treatment that is safe in pregnancy.\nDrugs safe in pregnancy:\n- Diuretics (e.g., furosemide) for pulmonary congestion\n- Beta-blockers\n- Vasodilators like hydralazine\n- Nitrates\nDrugs contraindicated in pregnancy:\nACE inhibitors and ARBs are teratogenic.\nA. Losartan ARB – contraindicated\nB. Lisinopril ACE inhibitor – contraindicated\nHydralazine :\n- Safe vasodilator in prgnancy\n- Reduces afterload, improving cardiac output in heart failure\nMethyldopa :\n- Used for gestational hypertension\n- Not used for heart failure management\nIn heart failure (including peripartum cardiomyopathy):\n- Avoid ACEI / ARB\n- Use hydralazine + nitrates", "id": 124, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1415, "stem": "Research 2 pediatric groups there are many variables the Researcher want to see the relation between these variables and outcome of of the waight in the last 5 years across the all age and gender", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "T test"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Linear regression"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Logistic regression"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Multinomial"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 125, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 1456, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman with stable systemic lupus erythematosus presents to the clinic with active polyarthritis for 1 week. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Prednisolone and methotrexate"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Prednisolone and sulfasalazine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prednisolone and cyclophosphamide"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Prednisolone and mycophenolate mofetil"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 126, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1465, "stem": "60-year-old man from India presented to the Emergency Department with headache and low- grade fever for 3-weeks. The headache became worse over the last 2-days and was associated with voming. In the emergency room, he developed generalized seizures , Test Result Normal Values WBC 19 4.5-10.5 x 109/L Pressure 280 200-250 mmH20 (Male) 300-400 mmH20 (Female) Cells 380 0-3 /ML Total protein (Men) 3 0.22-0.33 g/L Glucose 1.2 2.50-3.89 mmol/L CSF differenal WBC count: 73% lymphocytes and 13 % neutrophils. CSF culture: Pending. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Neurocyscercosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tuberculous meningis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bacterial meningis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Viral meningis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 127, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 1479, "stem": "A 42-year-old P4+2 presented with severe, progressive secondary dysmenorrhea and menorrhagia. Pelvic examination demonstrated a tender, diffusely enlarged uterus . Endometrial biopsy: Normal endometrium. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Endometritis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Endometriosis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Adenomyosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Uterine sarcoma"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 128, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1484, "stem": "14-year-old male, diarrhea for 3 months, associated with abdominal bloating and loss of subcutaneous fat. What is the best treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hydrolyzed diet"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Gluten free diet"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "High caloric diet"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Fat free diet"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 129, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1496, "stem": "A 25-year-old woman presented with abnormal uterine bleeding, underwent an endometrial biopsy . Endometrial biopsy: Atypical complex hyperplasia. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tamoxifen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hysterectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Oral progesterone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Endometrial ablation"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 130, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1511, "stem": "A 62-year-old man known diabetes and hypertension presents with 4-hour history of sudden left leg pain, he had a history of left leg pain after walking 200 meters that make him stop and is relieved by rest. He is on oral hypoglycemic and statin but not compliant to medication and has been smoking for the last 40 years. Pulse examination on the left reveals present femoral absent popliteal and distal, his right leg revealed intact femoral and popliteal but diminished distal pulses . Blood pressure 110/70 mmH Respiratory rate 18 /min Temperature 36.6 °C Oxygen saturation 95% Test Result Normal Values Random Glucose 10 3.9-5.5 mmol/L HA1C 8 4.7-5.6% ECG: Normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT angiogram"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vascular ultrasound"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Conventional angiogram"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Magnetic resonant angiogram"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 131, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1512, "stem": "A 40-year old healthy woman presents to Emergency Room with a 4-hour history of sudden onset right lower limb pain. Her examination reveals intact femoral pulse but absent distal pulses, diminished sensory exam with an altered motor exam. Her labs are normal . Blood pressure 110/70 mmHg Heart rate 95 /min Respiratory rate 18 /min Temperature 36.6 °C Oxygen saturation 98% ECG: Shows atrial fibrillation. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Femoral embolectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Above knee amputation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Catheter-directed thrombolysis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Heparinization and observation"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 132, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1514, "stem": "A 62-year-old man known case of DM HT presents with 4-hour history of sudden left leg pain, the patient had a history of left leg pain after walking 200 meters that make him stop and is relieved by rest. He is on an oral hypoglycemic and a statin but he is not compliant to his medication. He has been smoking for the last 40 years. His pulse exam on the left reveals present femoral absent popliteal and distal, his right leg revealed intact femoral and popliteal but diminished distal pulses His labs are normal apart from an elevated BSL . ECG: Normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Catheter-directed thrombolysis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Femoral thrombectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Above knee amputation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Heparinization and observation"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 133, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1526, "stem": "A 58-year-old man presents with a 2-month history of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. Ultrasound shows a diffusely calcified gallbladder without stones. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cholecystectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Percutaneous biopsy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cholecystostomy cube"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 134, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1541, "stem": "A 50-year-old woman interphalangeal joints presented to the clinic with polyarthralgia affecting distal and proximal bilaterally for 3-years. She does not have significant early morning stiffness. Clinical examination revealed no active arthritis but there was non-tender hard nodules over some distal interphalangeal . Test Result Normal Values Hb 140 130-170 g/L (Male) 120-160 g/L (Female) Platelets count 190 150-400 × 109/L WBC 11 4.5-10.5 × 109/L ESR 15 2-1 mm/h (Male) 3-15 mm/h (Female) C-reactive peptide 5 <8.2 mg/L Rheumatoid factor 30 <58 kIU/L Urea 4 2.75-7.4 mmol/L Creatinine 75 44-115 mol/L Which of the following is the most likely rheumatological disease?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hand osteoarthritis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Polyarticular gout"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Reactive arthritis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 135, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1556, "stem": "A 40-year-old man is admitted with palpitations of 4-hour duration. He had similar attack a month ago but did not seek medical advice. His initial electrocardiogram showed atrial fibrillation with controlled ventricular rate. He subsequently converted to sinus rhythm. Which of the following drugs is the most appropriate to maintain sinus rhythm?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Digoxin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Bisoprolol"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Verapamil"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Amiodarone"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "1557.8 years old boy presented with focal pain on his left leg that is increasing in intensity Associated with local warmth and tenderness and subjective fever. He has a history of fall with wound What is the radiological investigation for the leg?\nA) X ray\nB) MRI\nC) Ct scan\nD) Nuclear scan\n1558.ministry of health in x country is detecting flu cases in the winter season, they gather data from hospitals, health centers, labs. What is the type of surveillance\nA) sentinel surveillance from selected reporting sites\nB) active surveillance\nC) passive surveillance\nD) syndromic surveillance", "id": 136, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 1559, "stem": "6 y old boy present with convulsions, pallor hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy Hb and plt low WBCs 25 What is the line of management for his CNS should be involved ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Systemic chemotherapy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Intrathecal chemotherapy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Anticonvulsant"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ventripleural shunt"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Explaination is here:", "id": 137, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1621, "stem": "A patient presents 3 weeks after vaginal delivery with sudden vaginal bleeding of 700 cc. Which of the following is this type of bleeding?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Early post-partum"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Late postpartum"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intra-partum"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ante-partum"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 138, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1635, "stem": "Couples trying to convince for 6 years the female had PCOS semen analysis for the male is normal they asked for sex selection because they want a male what is the proper action by the doctor?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refuse and dismiss them as patients"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Procceed with sex selection as requested?"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tell them that sex selection is not eithically justified"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Refer them to another clinic that do sex selection"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Answer: C (if A was refuse their request, without the word dismiss, go for it)", "id": 139, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1691, "stem": "Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment for a liver amoebic abscess?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Metronidazole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Open drainage"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Percutaneous drainage"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Percutaneous aspiration"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 140, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 1703, "stem": "A 29-year-old woman at 29 weeks of gestation presented with preterm labor. Which of the following tocolytics can cause patent ductus arteriosus?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Atosiban"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ritodrine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Nifedipine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Indomethacin"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 141, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1725, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman in her first pregnancy had an ultrasound confirming Monochorionic Monoamniotic twins. What was the time interval between ovulation and cleavage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0-72 hours"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "4-8 days"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "9-12 days"}, {"letter": "D", "text": ">12 days"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 142, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1745, "stem": "A 67-year-old man presented with a 2-month history of yellowish discoloration of his sclera and skin with dark urine and pale stool. On examination, he was jaundiced with a palpable distended gallbladder (see report and lab results). Test Result Normal Values Alkaline phosphatase 421 39-117 IU/L Direct bilirubin 122.3 1.5-6.5 mol/L Total bilirubin 134.5 3.5-16.5 umol/L Ultrasound: Dilated intra and extrahepatic bile ducts with hugely distended gallbladder. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Klatskin tumour"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pancreatic cancer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mirizzi's syndrome"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Common bile duct stone"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 143, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1775, "stem": "A 33-year-old pregnant woman G3P2 at 24 weeks' gestation presents to the clinic complaining of sudden, watery vaginal discharge. A sterile speculum examination is performed to evaluate for preterm prelabor rupture of membranes (PPROM). Which of the following proteins in the vaginal secretions can help establish the diagnosis of membrane rupture?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Placental Protein 14 (PP14)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Placental alpha-1 microglobulin 1 (PAMG-1)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Placental Vasopressinase"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Placental growth factor."}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 144, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1813, "stem": "A diabetic pt has ulcer in lateral foot with discharge and granulation but intact pulse", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Venous"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ischemic"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Malignant"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Neuropathic"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Diabetic patient with pus discharge from ulcer in the Lateral foot ulcer “not leg” with granulation tissue and pus, pulses\nare present. What's the underlying etiology?\nA- venous\nB- arterial\nC- neuropathic\nD- malignant", "id": 145, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1877, "stem": "68-year-old Male patient was complaining of yellowish discoloration ,epigastric pain ,dark urine , pale stool and weight loss. On examination the patient was jaundiced and on palpation tenderness in the epigastric region and right upper quadrant. The gallbladder was palpable and tender. Labs showed • Elevated liver enzymes high total and direct bilirubin high ALP • GGT 213 CA19-9 : 502 Ultrasound showed hugely dilated common bile duct and gallbladder. Which of the following is the most likely cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Mirizzi's syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Liver metastasis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Distal common bile duct obstruction"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cancer of the head of the pancreas"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 146, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1878, "stem": "A 34-year-old woman presented with a 2-month history of jaundice and upper abdominal discomfort. She is a known case of SLE on hydroxychloroquine. She also gave a history of pruritus and fatigue. Clinical examination revealed obesity and hepatomegaly. US Abdomen: Showed enlarged liver with bright coarse echogenicity. Viral Hepatitis: Negative. ANA: 1:640 (High). Liver Biopsy: Interphase hepatitis with plasma cells. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Prednisolone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Methotrexate"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Vitamin E with weight loss"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ursodeoxycholic acid"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "A 34-year-old woman presented with 2-months history of jaundice and upper abdominal discomfort. She is known case of SLE on hydroxycholorquine. She also gave history of pruritis and fatigue. Clinical examination revealed obesity and hepatomegaly Test Result Normal Values\nDirect bilirubin 32 1.5-6.5 mol/L\nTotal bilirubin 43 3.5-16.5 mol/L\nAspartate aminotransferase 654 12-40 IU/L Alanine aminotransferase 734 5-40 IU/L Alkaline phosphatase 129 39-117 IU/L Gamma glutamyltransferase 60 6 to 37 IU/L\nRandom Glucose 7 3.9-5.5 mmol/L\nGlobulin 43 21-36 g/L\nUS Abdomen: Showed enlarged liver with bright coarse echogenicity. Viral Hepatitis Negative. ANA: 1:640\nLiver Biopsy: Inter phase hepatitis with plasma cells.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\nA. Prednisolone\nB. Methotrexate\nC. Vitamin e with weight loss\nD. Ursodeoxycholic acid", "id": 147, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1886, "stem": "A 30-year-old PG at 30 weeks of gestation with severe preeclampsia complaining of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. Which of the following is the most likely cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Esophagitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pancreatitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Gastric ulcer"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Tension of the liver capsule"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 148, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1887, "stem": "A 28-year-old G4 P3 AO at 12 weeks of gestation with a prior history of preeclampsia and fetal growth restriction presented to the Antenatal Clinic. Which of the following will most likely reduce the risk of growth restriction and preeclampsia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aspirin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Progesterone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Multivitamins"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ferrous sulfate"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 149, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1888, "stem": "A 23-year-old primigravida presents to the Emergency Room at 35 weeks of gestation complaining of decreased fetal movement for the last few hours. Examination revealed the cervix is 1 cm dilated and the head is at -2 station. Cardiotocography shows that the baseline is 160 beats per minute with good accelerations, good variability, and early decelerations. The uterine contractions are mild and regular. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Discharge home"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cesarean section"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Augmentation of labor"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Observation and continuous CTG monitoring"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 150, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1889, "stem": "A 30-year-old GS P3013 woman presented in labor at term. She had a vaginal delivery complicated by abruption placenta. She was then noted to have significant uterine bleeding. The team in charge noted she had bleeding from multiple venipuncture sites. Which of the following is the best next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Coagulopathy correction"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Internal iliac ligation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Packing the uterus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "B-Lynch suture"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 151, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1891, "stem": "A 30-year-old, 12 weeks pregnant woman presented to the Emergency Room with vaginal bleeding. Examination revealed her fundal height corresponded with 19 weeks of gestation. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Partial mole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Complete mole"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Threatened abortion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Abdominal pregnancy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 152, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1897, "stem": "Which of the following is the best oxygen delivery system in a patient with Pulmonary Disease?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Simple face mask"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Rebreathing bag"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Nasal cannula"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Venturi mask"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 153, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1903, "stem": "A 35-year-old woman presents with unusual vaginal bleeding and a fleshy vaginal mass. She underwent a suction evacuation for hydatidiform mole. Her beta-hCG level was 870,000 mIU/mL prior to the procedure. She was followed with weekly beta-hCG levels for 5 weeks, with the following results: 1st week: 576 mIU/mL 2nd week: 350 mIU/mL 3rd week: 180 mIU/mL 4th week: 70 mIU/mL 5th week: 300 mIU/mL Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Continue weekly surveillance"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Administer methotrexate immediately"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Perform a biopsy to confirm the diagnosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Perform a metastatic workup without biopsy"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 154, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1904, "stem": "A child came for a routine check up. He can walk steadily independently, and has an immature pincer grasp. Says 4-6 words, knows 2 body parts. What is the developmental age in months?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "24 months"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "18 months"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "15 months"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "12 months"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Child walking unsteady with raising hands when walking.\nHow old is he in months:\nA. 15\nB. 18\nC. 12\nD. 24", "id": 155, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 1973, "stem": "An 18-year-old man involved in burn accident, he has 2nd degree burn to his both lower limbs. Weight 70 kg Using Parkland formula, which of the following is most appropriate fluid resuscitation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lactate ringer's 200 ml/hr for 24 hrs"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Normal saline 1L bolus then 120ml/hr"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lactate ringer's 5 L over 1st 6 hrs then 5 L over next 16 hrs"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lactate ringer's 2.5 L over 1st 6 hrs then 2.5 L over the next 16 hrs"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 156, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1977, "stem": "A 35-year-old Indian man presents to the Emergency Department with lethargy, weight loss, and headache for 3 months. He has a history of contact with a tuberculosis patient several years ago. His neurological and ophthalmologic examination are unremarkable. He has a positive polymerase chain reaction for Mycobacterium tuberculosis . Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol and diuretics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol and acyclovir"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol and dexamethasone"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 157, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 1983, "stem": "A 32-year-old healthy woman had neck ultrasound to evaluate lipoma; incidentally, there was right thyroid lobe 2.3 cm solid hypoechoic nodule . Test Result Normal Values Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone 2.3 0.4-5.0 pU/mL. Thyroxine (T4 free serum) 11.3 8.5 - 15.2 pmol/L. Fine Needle Aspiration: Follicular neoplasm, Bethesda 4. Which of the following is most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nodule excision"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hemithyroidectory"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Repeat US in 3 months"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Radiofrequency ablation"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 158, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 1992, "stem": "A 44-year-old woman (P5005) presents to the clinic with an uncomfortable sensation in the vagina for the last year. She reports pelvic pressure associated with urinary frequency but no urinary leaks or irregular menstruation. Obstetric history includes 5 term spontaneous vaginal deliveries. She works in loading and unloading heavy boxes. Examination reveals a large bulging of the anterior vaginal wall. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Kegel exercises"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vaginal pessary"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bladder suspension"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Anterior colporrhaphy"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 159, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1993, "stem": "A 42-year-old P3003 healthy woman presents with heavy prolonged vaginal bleeding over the past 7 months. Her periods have become longer and progressively heavier, with the last one occurring 15 days ago. Pelvic examination reveals a small amount of blood in the vaginal vault, normal-sized uterus, and mobile ovaries. (See reports.) Pelvic ultrasound: Endometrium 13 mm and a 2.2 cm x 1 cm lesion in the uterine cavity. Endometrial biopsy: Normal histology. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sonohysterogram"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Examination under anesthesia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Magnetic resonance imaging of the uterus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Evaluate hemoglobin level and coagulopathy screening"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 160, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1994, "stem": "A 38-year-old woman is scheduled to undergo a myomectomy due to the presence of a large uterine fibroid. The surgeon is planning to administer medication that will help reduce the size of the uterine fibroid prior to performing the procedure. Which of the following is the most appropriate medication to use for this purpose?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "GnRH injection"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Combined contraceptive pills"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intrauterine contraceptive device"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Oral continuous progesterone therapy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 161, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1995, "stem": "A 20-year-old woman presented with a history of primary infertility for 1 year. She had a history of scanty irregular cycles with prolonged intervals . Which of the following is the important workup?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "MRI pelvis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "TSH serum level"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prolactin serum level"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Chromosomal analysis"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 162, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1996, "stem": "A 35-year-old woman is complaining of severe abdominal pain. She is a known case of primary infertility and underwent embryo transfer 3 weeks ago . Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ovarian torsion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ectopic pregnancy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Acute appendicitis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Intestinal obstruction"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 163, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 1997, "stem": "A 36-year-old presented to the Antenatal Clinic for an Ultrasound scan. Ultrasound: Single viable fetus with significant increased levels of amniotic fluid (polyhydramnios). Which of the following is the most likely cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Anencephaly"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Post-term pregnancy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Posterior urethral valve"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Maternal ingestion of NSAID"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 164, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2025, "stem": "A 74-year-old man presented with a 2-month history of vague upper abdominal pain associated with progressive yellowish discoloration of his skin and sclera and dark urine. On examination, he was jaundiced . Test Result Normal Values Alkaline phosphatase 312 39-117 |U/L Direct bilirubin 87 1.5-6.5 mol/L Total bilirubin 97 3.5-16.5 mol/L Ultrasound: Dilated intrahepatic biliary tree with collapsed gallbladder. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Klatskin tumour"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mirizzi's syndrome"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Gallbladder cancer"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Periampullary tumour"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 165, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2031, "stem": "IUGR neonate placed under warmer, he is still hypothermic despite warm environment, how to manage", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IV dextrose"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Sepsis workup + empirical antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reassure + change environment ( increase warmer temperature i guess)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Skin-to-skin contact"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 166, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2032, "stem": "Child 7 years old had febrile seizure which was transferred by the EMD (emergency medical dispatch). The mother wants to inform the school regarding the child’s condition. Which of the following is the role of EMD regarding the mother’s concern ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Advice taking consent before sharing the medical information"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Advice the parents to share the medical information"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Do not inform them"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 167, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2033, "stem": "10 years old sudden loss of consciousness then regained while running. all physical examination normal except ejection systolic murmur. What Is the most appropriate test?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "ECG"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Echo"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Chest X-Ray"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Reassure"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 168, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2036, "stem": "A child presented with low grade fever for 2 days then developed of acute onset of stridor What is the most common cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Acute epiglottitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Acute tonsilitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Viral laryngotracheitis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 169, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2045, "stem": "9 month boy on breast feeding , his mother recently started to introduce solid food , but he is mainly on breast feeding , what you recommend regarding his feeding:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Iron supplements."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Folate supplements ."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Vit D supplements ."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Speed up the rate of introduction of the solid food."}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 170, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2047, "stem": "Year-old G4P2 woman with previous postpartum hemorrhage is currently pregnant and worried about recurrence. Strongest risk factor for postpartum hemorrhage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Grand multiparity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Multiple gestation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Macrosomia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Previous postpartum hemorrhage"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "A pregnant woman G7P6 gave birth after labor of 7 hours had post-partum hemorrhage,\nwhat was her most likely risk factor?\nA. prolonged labor\nB. precipitous labor\nC. grand multiparity", "id": 171, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2049, "stem": "I had a question today about a 2-year-old kid who came with his mother. The chief complaint was that the kid's grandpa, who lives with them in the same house, was diagnosed with open tuberculosis. She's asking what we should do for the baby. The baby's medical history was almost positive for BCG vaccine. The options were:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassure because he already had the vaccine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Do chest X-ray and PPD and then wait for results."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Start them on isoniazid and ethambutol and typical TB treatment."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CXR + PPD + Start Latent TB Treatment NOW"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 172, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2050, "stem": "Child presented with fever, diarrhea, sunken eyes, poor feeding, hypotension, and capillary refill of 4 seconds. Which single finding most strongly indicates immediate admission?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Inability to eat solid food"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fever"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Capillary refill 4 seconds"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 173, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2051, "stem": "Adult patient presented with meningitis. No Na mentioned. Most likely causative organism?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Listeria monocytogenes"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Staph"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "E. coli"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 174, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2052, "stem": "Patient with bowel obstruction. Most appropriate investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Abdominal X-ray"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ultrasound"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Double contrast CT abdomen"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Colonoscopy"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2053.Patient with bowel obstruction. Best initial investigation?\nA. Abdominal X-ray\nB. CT abdomen\nC. Ultrasound", "id": 175, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2054, "stem": "Female in her 50s or 70s with localized LLQ tenderness and pain. Most appropriate investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Colonoscopy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CT abdomen"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Plain X-ray"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ultrasound"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 176, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2057, "stem": "Female with long history of autoimmune thyroiditis developed thyroid malignancy. Most likely type?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Papillary carcinoma"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Medullary carcinoma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Anaplastic carcinoma"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Thyroid lymphoma"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 177, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2059, "stem": "A 52-year-old woman presented with a recent rapid enlargement of a neck swelling associated with weight loss. She was diagnosed 25 years ago with Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hemorrhagic goiter"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thyroid lymphoma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Papillary carcinoma"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Sub-acute thyroiditis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 178, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2062, "stem": "A 55-year-old woman is referred to the clinic because of a 1 cm solitary nodule with sharp borders in the right lung. The nodule was detected on an abdominal CT performed to evaluate abdominal pain. She does not have any respiratory complaints. She smoked 20 cigarettes per day for 10 years but managed to stop smoking 20 years ago. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Follow-up CT in 3-6 months"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "No follow-up needed"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Transbronchial biopsy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Surgical resection"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 179, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2063, "stem": "28-week pregnant female with increased vaginal discharge for 6 hours. Sterile speculum showed pooling of fluid in posterior fornix. No contractions. Diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bacterial vaginosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Candidiasis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Normal vaginal secretions"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Rupture of membranes"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "2064.60y Postmenopausal woman with stage IA endometrial cancer. Best treatment?\nA. Chemotherapy\nB. Radiotherapy\nC. Progesterone therapy\nD. Hysterectomy", "id": 180, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2069, "stem": "Unvaccinated infant with meningitis. Gram-negative pleomorphic coccobacillus identified. Organism?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Neisseria meningitidis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Haemophiles influenzae type b"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2070.female 7y old with facial rash and multiple small joint tenderness suggestive of SLE.\nMarker of disease activity?\nA. ANA\nB. Rheumatoid factor\nC. Anti-dsDNA", "id": 181, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2071, "stem": "Alcoholic patient with recurrent epigastric pain and multiple admissions. Mildly elevated amylase. Most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Biliary colic"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Chronic pancreatitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Acute cholecystitis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 182, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2074, "stem": "A 58-year-old woman presents to the follow-up clinic. She is known to have idiopathic deep venous thrombosis and is on warfarin, which was initiated 6 weeks ago. The examination is unremarkable . Which of the following is the most appropriate management in addition to withholding warfarin?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Administer vitamin K"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Re-measure INR in 2 days"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Administer fresh frozen plasma"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Administer prothrombin complex concentrate"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 183, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2075, "stem": "Patient with severe hypertension and anterior epistaxis while on aspirin. Imaging normal. Most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Facial CT"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Refer to cardiology"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Nasal endoscopy"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 184, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2076, "stem": "Patient with ruptured appendix and localized abscess. Most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Immediate appendectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Interval appendectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Conservative management and observation"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 185, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2077, "stem": "Pregnant woman wants to quit smoking but failed previous attempts and is worried about continuing smoking during pregnancy. Most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nicotine replacement patches"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Non-nicotine gum"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bupropion"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 186, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2079, "stem": "45-year-old woman with endometriosis failed medical therapy and does not desire future fertility. Most definitive management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hysterectomy with oophorectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Laparoscopic ablation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Endometrial ablation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Combined oral contraceptive pills"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 187, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2081, "stem": "Which of the following findings is most characteristic of nephrotic syndrome?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hematuria"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hypertension"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Low serum albumin"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 188, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2082, "stem": "A patient with diabetic and hypertension is found to have persistent proteinuria. Which antihypertensive medication is preferred?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Calcium channel blocker"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "ACE inhibitor"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Thiazide diuretic"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Beta blocker"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2083.A 29-year-old pregnant woman at 34? Weeks Was admitted 10 days ago for observation due to headache, seizures and\ndrowsiness? presents with headache and BP 140/92 mmHg. She has no seizures. Which of the following is the\nmost appropriate treatment?\nA. Ramipril\nB. Labetalol\nC. Magnesium sulfate\n2084.Patient involved in RTA with air in the chest and neck/ after trauma. Most likely diagnosis?\nA. Aortic injury\nB. Pneumothorax\nC. Pulmonary embolism?\nD. Cardiac contusion", "id": 189, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2085, "stem": "Following a head-on motor vehicle collision, a 21-year-old unrestrained passenger presents to ER with dyspnea and respiratory distress. She is intubated and physical examination reveals subcutaneous emphysema and decreased breath sounds. Chest x-ray reveals cervical emphysema, pneumomediastinum, and a right-sided pneumothorax. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tension pneumothorax"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Open pneumothoraх"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tracheobronchial injury"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Esophageal injury"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Pulmonary contusion"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 190, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2086, "stem": "Preschool child with hypermetropia. What you will do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Concave glasses"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Convex glasses"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Covered one eye"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 191, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2087, "stem": "A patient came to the ER with multiple fractures and was given crystalloids but continued bleeding profusely. Labs showed Hb < 7 g/dL and severe tachycardia. The source of bleeding was not mentioned. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "ABG"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Diagnostic laparoscopy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Surgical laparotomy"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2088.A 4-year-old child presented with a history of non-bloody diarrhea for around 1 month (or several weeks).\nThe diarrhea occurred only during daytime and not at night.\nThe child appeared well with no failure to thrive.\nA. Toddler’s diarrhea\nB. Rotavirus\n2089.A 2-year-old child initially had upper respiratory tract infection / viral symptoms.\nAfter around 12 hours, the child presented to the ER with cyanosis, drooling, tripod position, and oxygen saturation of\nWhat is the diagnosis?\nA. Croup\nB. Epiglottitis\nC. Bacterial tracheitis", "id": 192, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2090, "stem": "A pediatric patient presented with ear discharge, high-grade fever, and other symptoms. What is the most likely cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Viral"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fungal"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bacterial"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Parasitic"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2091.A pediatric patient came to the well clinic with only runny nose and cough.\nTemperature was 36.5°C.\nWhat should be done regarding vaccination?\nA. Give vaccination\nB. Postpone vaccination\nC. Postpone live-attenuated vaccine\n2092.Which vaccine is contraindicated during pregnancy?\nA. OPV\nB. DTaP\nC. Influenza vaccine\n2093.Patient came complaining of bleeding with and after defecation with mild itching, what’s the cause:\n-anal fissure\n-perianal abscess\n-hemorrhoids\n-fistula", "id": 193, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2097, "stem": "A patient has a thyroid mass and laboratory results show a markedly elevated serum calcitonin level 48. This finding is most consistent with which type of thyroid cancer?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Papillary carcinoma"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Follicular carcinoma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Medullary carcinoma"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Anaplastic carcinoma"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 194, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2098, "stem": "7 year-old child unvaccinated presents with mild fever and cropped vesicles on the trunk. What is the most likely diagnosis? Vitals weren’t given", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Varicella"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Herpes simplex virus"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Smallpox"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Measles"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 195, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2099, "stem": "Child present with meconium aspiration and shortness of breath, was managed by mechanical intubation. how to diagnose complications?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bronchoscopy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Echocardiogram"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Pulmonary ultrasound"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "ECG"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 196, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2100, "stem": "A 30 year old male presented with foul smelling of perianal discharge. No change in bowel habits, no bleeding no diarrhea . On examination, 3 perianal fistulas were found on 7 and 3 o’clock and maybe 11 o’clock What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pelvic MRI"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fistulogram"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Rectal US"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Colonoscopy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 197, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2103, "stem": "A 7-year-old boy presents to the ER with abnormal movements during sleep involving his face and limbs. He has a history of seizures but is otherwise neurologically normal. EEG shows frontotemporal spikes. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Infantile spasms"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Rolandic seizure"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 198, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2105, "stem": "A 5-day-old girl brought to Emergency Room with lethargy and poor feeding. On examination, she was irritable, deeply jaundiced and dehydrated. The mother gave history of previous babies' required admission and exchange transfusion . Which of the following is important to be obtained in the history? Blood pressure 90/55 mmHg, Heart rate 95 /min, Respiratory rate 18 /min Temperature 36.6 °C Oxygen saturation 94 % Weight 3 kg Test Result Normal Values Hb 95 165-195 g/L (Newborn) 112-165 g/L (Child) Reticulocyte 3 0.2-1.2 % Total bilirubin 360 <274 mol/L (Preterm (3-5) days) <205 mol/L (Term (3-5) days) <21 mol/L (Older infant pre-term) <20.5 mol/L (Older infant term)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Apgar score"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Birth weight"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Type of delivery"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Maternal blood group"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "2106.An 80-year-old man presents with lower abdominal pain. He is afebrile and\nhemodynamically stable. Abdominal ultrasound reveals a 1 × 1 cm fluid collection in the\nlower abdomen. What is the most appropriate next step in management?\nA. Immediate surgical drainage\nB. Start empiric IV antibiotics and observe\nC. CT-guided drainage\nD. Exploratory laparotomy\n2107.- An 80-year-old man presents with lower abdominal pain. He is febrile and\nhemodynamically stable. Abdominal ultrasound reveals a 1 × 1 cm fluid collection in the\nlower abdomen. What is the most appropriate next step in management?\nA. Immediate surgical drainage\nB. IV antibiotics\nC. CT-guided drainage\nD. Conservative", "id": 199, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2109, "stem": "A 60 yr old man went to family med clinic for regular checkups. He’s asking if there’s any vaccine he can take:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "MMR"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pneumococcal"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Meningococcal"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Dtap"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 200, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2110, "stem": "Adult asthmatic patient has recurrent cough and wheeze when exposed to dust and perfumes. On physical examination, she has mild respiratory wheeze that failed to respond to albuterol. Spirometry shows a reversible obstructive pattern. What is the next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Inhaled salbutamol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Inhaled budesonide"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Inhaled ipratropium"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Montelukast"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 201, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2111, "stem": "An 18-month-old child with normal growth and weight 50th percentile is brought by his parents. They are concerned because his older sibling was recently diagnosed with celiac disease. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassure the parents"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Order tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA) test"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Start a gluten-free diet"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 202, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2112, "stem": "An 4-Year-Old child with normal growth and weight is brought by his parents. They are concerned because his older sibling has celiac disease. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassure the parents"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Order tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA) test"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Start a gluten-free diet"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 203, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2118, "stem": "6days new born with g6pd def. Type of anemia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hemolytic"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Inflammatory"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Of chronic dis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Physiological"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 204, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2124, "stem": "3 years old, looks acutely ill, sit on his mother's lap with his neck extended. What is the management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give him racemic epinephrine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Dexamethasone,"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intubate in the OR"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 205, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2125, "stem": "A 30 years old male came with nausea and vomiting. Anti-HAV IgM: Negative HBsAg: positive HBeAg: positive Anti-HCV: negative. What the type of hepatitis causing this ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hepatitis A"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hepatitis B"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hepatitis C"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hepatitis D"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 206, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2126, "stem": "20-Year-Old Female With Transient Vision Loss During Sport, No Other Symptoms, MRI Brain: Multiple Demyelinating Hyperintense Lesion In Periventricular And White Matter Area, What Is The Most Likely Diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Multiple Sclerosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Brain Lymphoma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Brain Vasculitis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Neuropsychiatric SLE"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 207, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2128, "stem": "23-year-old newly married female with irregular menstrual cycles is seeking contraception. She prefers to avoid methods that require daily use. Which of the following is the most appropriate contraceptive option?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Combined oral contraceptive pills"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Condom"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Diaphragm"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Estrogen transdermal patch"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 208, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2133, "stem": "A 70-year-old woman presents with fatigue and exertional dyspnea for several months. Medical history includes longstanding hypertension and chronic kidney disease, and she is not adherent to her medications. Examination reveals pale mucous membranes and nail beds . Kidney ultrasonography: Showed small kidneys bilaterally with echographic features suggesting chronic kidney disease. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's anemia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Erythropoietin deficiency"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Myelodysplastic syndrome"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Iron deficiency"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Inflammation"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 209, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2134, "stem": "17y, pregnant primigravida 9 weeks, complain mild vaginal bleeding, US: crown rump length 9 weeks and heartbeat +. Next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pelvic examination"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "IV Progesterone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "US for placenta position"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Reassurance and expectant management"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 210, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2135, "stem": "A 9-year-old child with gastroenteritis caused by Shigella species. Which of the following antibiotics should be prescribed?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Amoxicillin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Azithromycin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Metronidazole"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 211, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2140, "stem": "Case of enlarged parotid gland bilaterally (it was long) what's the causative organism?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Mumps"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "EBV"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CMV"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 212, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2141, "stem": "A 50-year-old man presented to the Emergency Department following a fail from 20 feet, on arrival he has a Glasgow Coma Scale of 8 with no external sign of injury. Mean arterial pressure 65 mmHg Intracranial pressure 15 mmHg. Which of the following is the most likely cerebral perfusion pressure in mmHg?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "40"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "50"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "60"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "70"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 213, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2142, "stem": "A 72-Year-Old Man Presented With Acute Severe Chest Pain With An ECG Revealing ST Segment Elevation In II, III, AVF Leads And Was Treated With Thrombolysis. Two Days Later He Became Acutely Unwell With Severe Dyspnea. On Examination: • Loud Systolic Murmur At The Apex Radiating To The Axilla • Bilateral Crackles At The Base Of The Lungs • Blood Pressure: 90/60 mmHg • Heart Rate: 100 /min • Respiratory Rate: 25 /min • Oxygen Saturation: 88 % Which Of The Following Is The Most Likely Diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Myocarditis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cardiogenic Shock"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Rupture Papillary Muscle"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Acute Right Sided Heart Failure"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 214, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2143, "stem": "Patient 45 years old with right sided breast pain and bloody nipple charge, no findings on physical examination of the right breast Whats the most appropriate investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ductogram"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mammogram"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ultrasound"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CT"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2144.A 32-year-old woman with obesity (BMI 38 kg/m²) and chronic hypertension is planning pregnancy. She is concerned about the\nrisk of intrauterine fetal demise. Which of the following preconception interventions is most important for reducing this risk?\nA. Achieving optimal blood pressure control\nB. Weight reduction before conception\nC. Both are equally important\nD. Smoking cessation (or another plausible distractor)forgot\n2145.A patient has a postoperative open wound. The wound is improving, with healthy granulation tissue forming and no signs\nof infection. What is the next best management?\nA. Simple packing (wet-to-dry or saline gauze)\nB. Vacuum-assisted closure (VAC/negative-pressure wound therapy)\nC. Primary closure\nD. Antibiotics", "id": 215, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2163, "stem": "A 39-year-old woman, who is a mother of 3 children and has completed her family, she is a known case of endometrioma in the right ovary that has been removed 2 years ago. She came to the clinic complaining of mild to moderate dysmenorrhea and deep pain during intercourse along with her chronic lower abdomen pain. Pelvic ultrasonography revealed the presence of le\\ ovarian cyst measuring 6X7 cm that appeared to be an endometriotic cyst. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Removing the cyst when the size becomes more than 10 cm"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Aspira7on of the content of cyst under ultrasound guidance"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Total abdominal hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Removing the cyst laparoscopically with ablation of any endometriotic spot"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 216, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2166, "stem": "Patient with symptoms of Celiac disease . Has positive anti endomysial and anti TTG. biopsy showed vilous atrophy What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give gluten and retest for antibodies"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Repeat biopsy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Elimination of gluten for life"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 217, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2170, "stem": "A 65-year-old man with congestive heart failure and osteoarthritis moved into a nursing home 3 months ago. During the past 4 weeks, the patient has lost 3.6 kg, lost interest in all social activities, and has been crying almost every day. The mood is worse in the morning. At times, especially when the mood is impaired, the patient's short-term memory appears to be lost. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Multi-infarct dementia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Alzheimer's disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hypothyroidism"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Depression"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 218, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 2174, "stem": "Child 9 yrs has bed wetting and mother mentioned that he was dry since the age of six years. His mother noticed foul smelling urine and change in urine color. There is positive nitrate and leukocytes Culture: gram negative bacilli bacteria Wbcs > 100,000 What is the most common organism causing this condition?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "E coli"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Proteus"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Klebsiella"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Streptococcus pneumonia"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 219, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2175, "stem": "Patient presents to clinic complaining of recurrent chest pain. He has chest pain with heavy exersise but releaved with rest. resting ecg normal and he has no past medical history or comorbidities. What next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Myocardial perfusion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Echo"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Stress ECG"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Coronary Angiography"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 220, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2176, "stem": "25 years old man presented to the clinic and he seems to be looking frequently at the corner of the room when asked he said “ my mother is standing there,but others can’t see her only me”. When relatives from his family were asked they said that his mother died when he was 5 years old. What does he have or what is the cause of this?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Visual hallucination"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Auditory hallucinations"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Episode of disorganized thoughts"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Delusion"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 221, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 2179, "stem": "A 75-year-old man in the Intensive Care Unit has developed a discharging sacral wound. Examination confirms necrotic skin and subcutaneous fat. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Primary repair"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Debridement and primary closure"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Debridement and primary skin grafting"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Debridement and vacuum-assisted closure"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 222, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2180, "stem": "Patient came with typical chest pain and sweating. ECG shows sinus tachycardia with LBBB. Previous ECG is normal Started on aspirin, atorvastatin, Nitroglycerin , Beta blocker and morphine. Cardiac enzyme is pending. What is the next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Intravenous Lidocaine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Wait for cardiac enzyme"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Angiography"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Discharge home"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 223, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2181, "stem": "78 years old known case of DM2 HTN and CKD admitted to hospital due to confusion and fluctuant of consciousness taking multiple drugs his temperature is Fever 39 C urine analysis shows positive leukocyte esterase WBC 50 protein negative glucose +1 which of the following is the best next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Head CT scan"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Review medication"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lumbar Puncture (LP)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Order blood and urine culture and start Antibiotics"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 224, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2182, "stem": "Elderly women with recurrent UTIs Best clinical proven way to prevent recurrent utis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1 Hydration"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "2 cranberry juice"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "3 avoid tight clothing"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "4 urinate after intercourse"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 225, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2183, "stem": "Female RA around 35 years patient on Methotrexate, ask dr about vaccination as she afraid from influenza and pneumonia", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give live influenza vaccine and No pneumococcal"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give inactivated influenza vaccine and pneumococcal conjugated"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Don't give any as she is immunocompromised"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Let her stop methotrexate and return to take vaccine"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 226, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2184, "stem": "6 months boy delivered full term vaginally without complications Come to asses by doctor and he found him has normal development Which of the following of gross motor development present in the infant at this age", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sit without support"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Roll from prone to supine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mature grasp"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 227, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2186, "stem": "Pt pos MVA BP 90/60 no bleeding what is the next step :", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Abdominal ct"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Brain MRI"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "OBSERVATION"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "IV FLUID"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 228, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2187, "stem": "Pregnant 37wks with severe pre eclampsia The most appropriate step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Observe"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Immediate delivery"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prepare for cs"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Discharge"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 229, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2188, "stem": "20-year-old G3 P2002 is in Labor and Delivery Ward in active labor at 6 cm dilatation. The patient is pregnant at 34 weeks and is known to have twin pregnancy. What would be the characteristic that puts this patient at risk of postpartum hemorrhage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Patients age"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Patients parity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Gestational age"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Multiple gestations"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 230, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2189, "stem": "Patient have liver cirrhosis with ascites and present with umbilical mass, hernias reducible and uncomfortable he is planed for hernia repair. What is most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Do the operation immediately"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Do not do operation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Delay until the ascites control"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 231, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2190, "stem": "65 post menopause woman complains of insomnia and hot flushed, what is the initial management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Soya tablets"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Life style modifications"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hormonal replacement therapy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hysterectomy i think"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 232, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2191, "stem": "Child came complaining of generalised edema and fatigue, he had history of bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain 2 weeks ago, Lab: Retics 3 Hb 9 or 8 Plt 55 Wbc 8 Cr high What test would lead to diagnosis ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Blood"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Urine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Stool"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 233, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2193, "stem": "Patient complains of heart burn on pantoprazole (dose was not mention), he is not improving, what the next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Upper endoscope"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Increase the dose of pantoprazole"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Investigate H.pylori"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 234, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2194, "stem": "Women on OCPs came complaining of chest pain for 4 or 5 hours Trop was less than the normal range mentioned in exam What is the initial management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Anti Coagulation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thrombolytic"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IVC filter"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 235, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2201, "stem": "35-year-old male presents with dizziness and fatigue that worsen after meals. On examination, he is pale and jaundiced. He does not take any medication except omeprazole. Laboratory tests show anemia and increased bilirubin (mainly direct/dark). What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cold agglutinin anemia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Coombs-positive autoimmune hemolytic anemia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Drug-induced hemolysis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "G6PD deficiency"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 236, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2202, "stem": "19 years old woman came with amenorrhea , no secondary sexual characters low fsh , lh?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Abdominal us"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Start OCP"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Brain MRI to assess pituitary & hypothalamus"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 237, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2203, "stem": "A 34-year-old male presents to the emergency department after a high-speed motor vehicle accident. He is found to have a pelvic fracture described as an \"open-book\" injury. On examination, there is blood at the urethral meatus and a high-riding prostate on digital rectal exam. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Insert a Foley catheter"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Obtain a KUB CT"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Perform a retrograde urethrogram"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Proceed to exploratory laparotomy"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 238, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2204, "stem": "Child came with increasing vague abdominal pain and bruises in abdomen has history of falling from bicycle days ago what is next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT abdomen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "US abdomen"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reassure I think"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 239, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2207, "stem": "A 60-something-year-old man with known COPD, controlled with bronchodilators, comes for a routine follow-up. He quit smoking 12 years ago. His oxygen saturation ranges from 89% to 91%. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Azithromycin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pneumococcal vaccine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Home oxygen"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 240, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2208, "stem": "A patient presents to the emergency department with abdominal pain for 5 days and yellowish discoloration of the sclera, associated with fever and chills. Laboratory results: WBC: 19 × 10⁹/L Indirect bilirubin: 7 Direct bilirubin: 18 Total bilirubin: 25 Alkaline phosphatase (ALP): 250 What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cholangitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Acute cholecystitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Biliary colic"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hepatic mucocele"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 241, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2209, "stem": "Pediatric with seizure and Fatigue other signs, Hepatomegaly and Cataract Blood Culture: E.coli labs showed metabolic acidosis urine analysis: aminoaciduria and glycosuria What’s the diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Galactosemia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Homocystinuria"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Methylmalonic Acidemia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Maple syrup urine syndrome"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 242, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2210, "stem": "Pregnant delivered and placenta still in place + 800 cc blood loss , typpe of PPH?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1ry"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "2ry"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "3ry"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Iatrogenic"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 243, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2213, "stem": "Woman heavy smoker, will start on exercise program and she doesn't know the risk on health , what type of model?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Health belief model"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Health promotion model"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Transtheoretical"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 244, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2214, "stem": "Perforated diverticulitis + abscess collection 6.8 cm , vitally stable ( vitals provided no hypotension nor fever) ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Antibiotic"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Exploration"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Percutaneous drainage and Antibiotic"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 245, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2218, "stem": "Child with head trauma vomited then came to ED drowsy and dilated pupil , CT small epidural hematoma no midline shift ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urgent surgical evacuation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Repeat ct"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mannitol"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 246, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2223, "stem": "Uncontrolled BA + greasy foul smell stool , next investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ct chest"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Brocho lavage"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sweat chloride test"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 247, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2224, "stem": "A 14-year-old girl presents with short stature, primary amenorrhea, and absent secondary sexual characteristics. Her karyotype is 45,X. What is the most appropriate treatment at this time?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Growth hormone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Oral contraceptive pills"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Estrogen therapy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Gonadotropin replacement therapy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 248, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2226, "stem": "A 34-year-old woman who delivered 5 months ago presents with fatigue, dizziness, and failure to breastfeed her baby. Her obstetrical history reveals a vaginal delivery complicated by a retained placenta and severe postpartum hemorrhage. On evaluation, her free T4 level is low (4 mmol/L). What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give thyroxine immediately"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Order Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH) level"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give conjugated estrogen"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Correct hyponatremia with hypertonic saline"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 249, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2227, "stem": "A patient who is a known case of Crohn's disease presents with bouts of bloody diarrhea and left-sided abdominal pain. The patient is currently taking prednisone 50 mg daily. A sigmoidoscopy is performed and reveals recurrent patchy colitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Anti-TNF agent (e.g., Infliximab / Adalimumab)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ileal release steroids"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Broad-spectrum antibiotics"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "5-aminosalicylic acid (Mesalamine)"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 250, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2228, "stem": "A post-operative patient who underwent a subtotal colectomy has a nasogastric tube set to high-level continuous suction. Laboratory evaluation reveals a serum potassium level of 2.8 mmol/L (low). What is the primary cause of this patient's hypokalemia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nasogastric (nasal) suctioning"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Inadequate dietary intake"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renal potassium wasting due to aldosterone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Intraoperative fluid shif"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 251, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2229, "stem": "A 72-year-old man is brought to the clinic by his wife due to episodes where he suddenly stops speaking, develops a blank, staring look, and displays repetitive facial grimacing and lip-smacking. Each episode lasts for 1 to 2 minutes, after which he is temporarily confused. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Temporal lobe seizure"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Typical absence seizure"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Transient ischemic attack"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Frontotemporal dementia"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 252, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2231, "stem": "A 40-year-old man medically and surgically free is evaluated in the Emergency Department for several episodes of hematemesis. He has no previous similar episodes and takes no medications. There is no jaundice, splenomegaly, or other signs of chronic liver disease. Abdominal examination reveals no tenderness, guarding, or rebound. Lab results: BP: 105/65 mmHg, HR: 110/min, Temp: 36.8°C, Hb: 95 g/L (low), Platelets: 250 × 10⁹/L, WBC: 12 × 10⁹/L, LFTs: normal Which of the following is the most likely cause of his gastrointestinal bleeding?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Erosive gastritis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Peptic ulcer disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Esophageal varices"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mallory-Weiss tear"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 253, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2232, "stem": "A married couple underwent in vitro fertilization (IVF) and successfully created viable embryos. However, before the embryos could be implanted into the wife's uterus, the couple decided to get a divorce. What is the most appropriate ethical and legal action regarding the stored embryos?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Discard the embryos as they are no longer married"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Keep the embryos stored until all legal matters end"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Allow the wife to use the embryos since she has maternal rights"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Donate the embryos to an infertile married couple"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 254, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2233, "stem": "A female patient undergoes BRCA genetic screening for breast cancer risk. Three days later, her sister arrives at the clinic asking to obtain the test results on her sister's behalf. What is the most appropriate action by the physician?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refuse to disclose the results to protect patient confidentiality"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give the results to the sister as she shares a genetic risk"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refer the sister to the patient affairs department to sign a release form"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Contact the patient immediately while the sister is in the clinic to get verbal consent"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 255, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2234, "stem": "A 4-day-old infant is evaluated in the neonatal intensive care unit for episodes of apnea. On examination, a continuous machinery-like murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border, and a wide pulse pressure is noted. A chest X-ray reveals bilateral pulmonary congestion (\"wet lung\"). What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Fluid restriction and diuretics"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "NSAID (Prostaglandin inhibitor)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Urgent surgical ligation of the ductus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Prostaglandin E1 infusion"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 256, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2235, "stem": "A newborn infant weighing 2 kg (small for gestational age) is noted to have a depressed nasal bridge, hypertelorism, upward slanting palpebral fissures, and an elevated epicanthal fold. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Chromosomal analysis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Metabolic screening"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Congenital infection screening (TORCH)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Skeletal survey"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 257, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2236, "stem": "A 42-year-old woman presents with a firm, partially movable right breast mass that has been present for 3 months. Physical examination and imaging reveal an ill-defined lobulated hypoechoic mass measuring 2 × 2 cm, which is coded as BI-RADS IV. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Fine needle aspiration cytology"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Needle core biopsy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Excisional biopsy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Short-interval follow-up imaging"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 258, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2237, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus presents to the Emergency Department with a 2-day history of generalized tonic-clonic seizures and severe fatigue. Musculoskeletal and neurological examinations are otherwise normal. She is initiated on intravenous methylprednisolone. Brain MRI reveals multiple hyperintense lesions. Which of the following medications is the most appropriate additional treatment to control her disease flares?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cyclophosphamide"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Azathioprine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Phenytoin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Heparin"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 259, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2238, "stem": "A 37-week gestation baby on delivery has a heart rate below 100 beats per minute, blue extremities, a weak cry with grimace, and little flexion. What is the most accurate APGAR score?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "4"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "5"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "7"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "9"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 260, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2239, "stem": "A 28-year-old woman underwent surgical excision of a breast mass that was clinically and radiologically suspected to be a fibroadenoma. The final histopathology report reveals a benign fibroadenoma with an incidental finding of a single focus of lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS) with free surgical margins. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Simple mastectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adjuvant radiotherapy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Adjuvant chemotherapy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "High-risk screening and follow-up"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 261, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2240, "stem": "A 33-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a history of severe right upper quadrant abdominal pain and yellow sclera. On examination, she appears unwell and has marked right upper quadrant tenderness with no guarding. An abdominal ultrasound reveals multiple gallstones and a dilated common bile duct (CBD) measuring 12 mm. She is initiated on intravenous flfluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Labs White Blood Cells (WBC): 15.0 × 10⁹/L (high) Total Bilirubin: 43 µmol/L (high) Alkaline Phosphatase: 280 IU/L (high)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Emergency laparoscopic cholecystectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Percutaneous cholecystostomy tube placement"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 262, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2242, "stem": "30 yo man with one week hx of jaundice, RUQ pain and fatigue, no mention of any chronic illness or risk factors ALT 1000 AST 850 what type of ACUTE hepatitis infection this pt have? (the word Acute was written in the Stem)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hep A"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hep B"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hep C"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hep D"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 263, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2243, "stem": "Baby for 9m vaccination, which of the following would be considered a contraindication for vaccination.", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Moderate to severe illness"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Penicillin allergy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Pharmacological therapy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "2244.65 yo man Terminal lung cancer pt discussed his desire for DNR with his primary doctor, dnr order was NOT completed,\nthen pt went for abdominal emergency surgery and during the surgery he had cardiac arrest, what to do?\nA. respect pt previously mentioned desire for DNR\nB. start Cardiac resus\nC. obtain consent from family member\nD. obtain consent from ethical committee", "id": 264, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2245, "stem": "Patient with Epigastric pain improving with calcium carbonate. On exam tenderness in hypochondriac region FOBT positive Colonoscopy normal Next best step", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Esophagogastroduodenoscopy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Treat with omeprazole"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Explorative lap"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Can’t remember"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "2246.A pic of rooting relfex, when will it disappear ?\nA. 2 months\nB. 4 months\nC. 6 months\nD. 8 months\n2247.A pregnant lady, TSH is high, low Free t3 and 4. What to give now?\n1. Thyroxine\n2. Propranolol\n3. nonsense\n4. Nonsense\n2248.An 82-year-old man hospitalized for a severe urinary tract infection develops acute confusion, agitation, and a fluctuating\nlevel of consciousness. He is diagnosed with delirium. In addition to treating his underlying infection, what is the most\nappropriate next step in his supportive management?\nA. Initiate low-dose haloperidol therapy\nB. Reorient the patient frequently to time and place\nC. Apply physical restraints to prevent accidental falls\nD. Place the patient in a dark, completely isolated room\n2249.A 9 month baby, with 6 months bone age, GH is normal, IGF 1 is low\nA. Failure to thrive\nB: Constitutional low growth\nC: Decrease growth hormone\n2250.A 14-year-old boy presents to the pediatric clinic complaining of recurrent crampy abdominal pain, persistent non-bloody\ndiarrhea, and weight loss over the past 3 months. A colonoscopy is performed and reveals deep, longitudinal mucosal\nulcerations alternating with areas of completely normal-appearing mucosa, alongside multiple aphthous ulcers. What is the\nmost likely diagnosis?\nA. Ulcerative colitis\nB. Crohn's disease\nC. Celiac disease\nD. Pseudomembranous colitis\n2251.A 34-year-old healthy woman presents with recurrent symptoms, including headache, anxiety, and mastalgia, that start 8-\n10 days before menstruation and improve afterward. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\nA. Endometriosis\nB. Perimenopausal symptoms\nC. Pelvic congestion syndrome\nD. Premenstrual Tension Syndrome\n2252.A 19-year-old woman presented with crampy, lower abdominal pain beginning with the onset of menses every month, and\nlasting for 2 to 3 days. There was no pain between menses, but reported radiation of pain to the lower back and upper\nthighs, along with nausea, fatigue, and headache which has been present for several years and progressively ge\\ng worse.\nPhysical examination normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\nA. Secondary dysmenorrhea\nB. Premenstrual syndrome\nC. Primary dysmenorrhea\nD. Endometriosis\n2253.A guy came from India, with meningitis symptoms ( headache neck rigidity photosensitivity)\nCSF shows\n- low glucose\n- slightly high protein\n- lymphocytosis\nwhat could be the causitive organism?\nA. TB\nB. Viral\nC. Bacterial\nD. Fungal\n2254.Male patient in his 20s, present with sever epigastric pain for 6 hours, start suddenly. On examination there is diffuse\ntenderness, sluggish bowel sound.\nLabs: high WBCs, Amylase 300\nWhich of the following best initial diagnostic step?\nA- Erect chest X-ray\nB- Abdominal X-ray\nC- Abdominal CT\nD- Abdominal US\n2255.A 30 something or late twenties woman complains of secondary amenorrhea for 6 month , normal FSH and prolactin,\nwhat is the diagnosis ? ( question had short stem, no labs mentioned)\nA- premature ovarian failure\nB- functional insufficiency\nC- axis failure (HPO)\nD- polycystic ovarian syndrome\n2256.19 years old female with primary amenorrhea and present breast development and secondary characteristics most\nprobable diagnosis?\nA-gonadal dysgenesis\nB- lh agenesis\nC- mullerian agenesis\nD-pituitary fsh deficiency\nQ 19 y.o female with primary amenorrhea, normal physical examination with normal breast\ndevelopment, what is the cause?\nA. Müllerin syndrome\nB. Gonadal agenesis\nC. Pituitary hormone\nD. Ovarian\n2257.A 9-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric endocrine clinic by his mother due to concerns regarding his short stature. On\nphysical examination, his height and weight are both tracking steadily along the 3rd percentile, and his systemic exam is\notherwise unremarkable.\nLaboratory evaluation reveals a normal random tsh , fsh , but a low insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) level for his\nchronological age. A left wrist radiograph demonstrates a bone age of 6 years. Upon taking the family history, the physician\nnotes that the mother short.\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?\nA) Growth Hormone Deficiency\nB) Familial Short Stature\nC) Severe Nutritional Failure to Thrive\nD) constitutional growth delay", "id": 265, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2261, "stem": "Biostat question asking to calculate the rate of people affected 30 kids with gastroenteritis 150 people total", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "10%"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "15%"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "20%"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "25%"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 266, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2262, "stem": "A 39-year-old G6P5 woman at 24 weeks' gestation presents to the antenatal clinic complaining of decreased fetal movement for the last 24 hours. She is hemodynamically stable. An ultrasound is performed and confirms intrauterine fetal death (fetal demise) with no cardiac activity. What is the most appropriate management to induce labor?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oxytocin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Misoprostol"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mifepristone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Dinoprostone"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 267, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2265, "stem": "Calculate the ASA score. hypertension, not very controlled, mildly elevated, diabetes (can’t rememembr on metformin or insulin). 2 years ago had inferior stemi, not managed with a stent but he took aspirin and statin. now coming for an elective inguinal hernia repair, what's the ASA score", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "2"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "3"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "4"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 268, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2266, "stem": "A guy fell from 4 stories. has a double fracture in the third fourth and fifth ribs with lung contusion. Breath sounds present. What’s the next best step", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Adequate analgesia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Surgery"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Chest tube"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Needle decompression"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 269, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2268, "stem": "Kid with 4 episodes of tonsillitis, missed a few days from school now doing well", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tonsillectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Don’t play out in the cold"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hand hygiene"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Prophylactic abx"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 270, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2269, "stem": "56 years old. on Aspirin and some other med. Presenting with 6 or 8 hrs of painless rectal bleed with clots. No abd pain, no weight loss, no changes in bowel movement, no abdominal tenderness. No hemorrhoids or masses seen. Whaat the most likely case", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Colon cancer"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Angiodysplasia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ibd"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 271, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2273, "stem": "If you had to raise awareness to the public which one would u choose", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oral cancer"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pancreatic"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Colon cancer"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 272, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2274, "stem": "Woman with neck mass showing hypo echoic with irregular borders", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "FNA"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thyroidectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Can’t remember"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 273, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2275, "stem": "Woman pregnant 9 weeks gestation presenting with signs of hyperthyroidism but no eye protrusion what’s treatment", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Methimazole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ptu"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Thyroidectomy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Radioactive iodine"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 274, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2284, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman G2 P1 L1 presented 3 days ago to the Emergency Room at 30 weeks gestation with preterm premature rupture of membranes. She was managed expectantly and today she started to have fever and abdominal pain. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Labor"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Chorioamnionitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Placental abruption"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cervical incompetence"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 275, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2285, "stem": "Screening of IDA 9-12 months Concerned mom with her newborn ask about when is the time for screening for IDA:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "2 months"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "4 months"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "10 months"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "18 months"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 276, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2289, "stem": "Newborn delivered by vacuum, and had blood cross sutures (cephalohematoma), he had tachycardia and hypotension and Hb 7.5. What will you do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "FFP"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Immediate resuscitation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IV flfluids and blood transfusion"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 277, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2291, "stem": "I can’t remember the exact details but the description was as it is Colonoscopy shows linear ulce segmental strictures, and areas of normal mucosa. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Inflammatory bowel disease"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Crohn's disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ulcerative colitis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 278, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2293, "stem": "Female 38 wkf of gestation with twins in vertex position ctg was normal what type of deliver", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Vaginal"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Forceps"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "C section"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 279, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2294, "stem": "A 33-year-old woman diagnosed with papillary thyroid cancer and planed for total thyroidectomy, she have mitral valve prolapse with mild regurgitation, no other co-morbidity. Which of the following is most appropriate option to prevent infection?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Amoxicillin to prevent endocarditis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cefazolin to prevent wound infection"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "No indication for antibiotic prophylaxis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Administer both cefazolin and amoxicillin"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 280, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2295, "stem": "A 22-year-old primigravida at 40 weeks gestation with a breech presentation is planned for a Cesarean section. During surgery, you notice a right ovarian cyst measuring 12 cm. What will you do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cystectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Oophorectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "No intervention"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Squeeze and rupture"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 281, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2298, "stem": "78-year-old male post-op contaminated abdominal surgery. Admitted to ICU and received 6 L of crystalloid IV fluid and 1 PRBC in the last 12 hours. Broad- spectrum antibiotics were administered. CVA 10 and urine output 20 ml/hr. Vitals: BP 70/45, HR: 125, T: 38.6, O2: 93%, Labs: WBC 8. What is the most appropriate IV intervention?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Albumin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "2L crystalloid fluid"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Norepinephrine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Furosemide"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 282, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2304, "stem": "5-Year-Old Man Known To Have COPD Presented To The Emergency Department With Increasing Dyspnea, Cough, Expectoration And Fever. Initial Assessment Revealed Fully Conscious Patient With Mild Distress, Central Cyanosis And Bilateral Wheezing On Chest Auscultation. The Patient Was Started On High Flow Oxygen And Salbutamol Nebulizer But 1 Hour Later, His Level Of Consciousness Is Reduced . Lab Results: ¥ ABG HCO\"#: 36 (Normal: 22-28 mmol/L) ¥ ABG PCO$: 9 (Normal: 4.7-6.0 kPa) ¥ pH: 7.18 (Normal: 7.36-7.45) ¥ ABG PO2: 15 (Normal: 10.6-14.2 kPa) Which Of The Following Is The Best Initial Step In Management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Start Mechanical Ventilation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Reduce Oxygen Flow"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Order Brain CT Scan"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Give Naloxone"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 283, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2306, "stem": "A patient presented after MVA with paraplegia and T6 injury, he is breathing well but his BP is 70/40 What is your initial step ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Immediate intubation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Isotonic fluid"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CT"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 284, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2308, "stem": "Patient with dilated cardiomyopathy on metoprolol develops cardiogenic shock. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Stop metoprolol immediately"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give glucagon as an antidote"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Continue metoprolol and add dobutamine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Switch metoprolol to carvedilol"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 285, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2309, "stem": "A 30-year-old Primigravida at 17 weeks of gestation is known to have epilepsy and is on valproic acid, She was concerned about the possible risks to her baby. Which of the following fetal abnormalities is markedly increased?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oligohydramnios"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Polyhydramnios"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Neural tube defect"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Non-immune hydrops"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 286, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2311, "stem": "A 7-month-old child presents with vomiting and jaundice, but no hepatosplenomegaly. The child was previously healthy and tolerated breastfeeding well. Symptoms began after the introduction of fruits and fruit juices. Urine sediment test is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Fructose intolerance syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Galactosemia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Gluten insensitivity"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 287, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2316, "stem": "A 37y patient suffered from circumferential right forearm burn + weak distal pulsation +oxygen saturation 86% in right arm ,what is most appropriate next step", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IV vasodilators"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Immediate Escharotomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IV flfluids , burn dressing , limb elevation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Measure intra compartmental pressure"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 288, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2317, "stem": "Pregnant known diabetic 40weeks uneventful pregnancy, sudden fetal death, fetus was 2.6kg no obvious anomaly, placental sample showed thrombi, what's the cause of the fetal death:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Diabetes"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Placental abnormality"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Growth restriction"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 289, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2319, "stem": "A child with a history of asthma presents for evaluation. His medical history is notable for atopic dermatitis. The patient has experienced recurrent episodes of wheezing and coughing, especially at night and with exposure to triggers Which of the following provides objective evidence of asthma?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "History of atopic dermatitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Increase in FEV%after bronchodilator"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Family history of asthma"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Nighttime coughing"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 290, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2320, "stem": "A patient presented with hypercalcemia. The next step in management was asked. The options included:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Calcitonin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Bisphosphonate"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Denosumab"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "IV flfluids"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 291, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2322, "stem": "A patient presents with right-sided hemiplegia (power 0/5), left-sided facial palsy, aphasia, and right-hand dominance. He also has facial anesthesia. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Left MCA stroke"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Right MCA stroke"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lateral medullary syndrome"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "A) Left MCA stroke", "id": 292, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2324, "stem": "Adult involved in MVA with irregular breathing low bp and pulse(cushing's triad) but oriented and cannt move his limbs what to do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tracheostomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cricothyroidectomy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Oro tracheal intubation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "NasoTracheal tube"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 293, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2325, "stem": "A 25 years old woman presents with sever acne following with a dermatologist who prescribed her ISOTRETINOIN and advised her not to get pregnant. She asks when she can try to get pregnant, what is the most appropriate (counseling?) regarding her pregnancy?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The medication is safe to take during pregnancy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "She can try to get pregnant after she finishes the treatment"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Stop and try to get pregnant immediately"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "She can try to get pregnant after 3 months of stoping the medication"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 294, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2326, "stem": "A female patient on isotretinoin for Acne. wishes to conceive. What should the physician tell her about the appropriate time?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "She can conceive as soon as she stops the isotretinoin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "She can conceive while taking isotretinoin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "She can conceive after 3 months off of isotretinoin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "She can conceive after 1 month of stopping isotretinoin"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "2328.Post MVA pt was hypotensive and tachycardic, ct scan was done and showed “large internal pelvic hematoma actively\nbleeding” most appropriate management?\nA. Ex lap\nB. Blood transfusion\n2329.18 months old boy, came to clinic for review. The child is always hungry, drinks 1 litter of milk daily and\neats dirt. Poor solid food intake. On exam He is underweight & has pale conjunctiva. Which the most\nappropriate investigation?\nA. lead screening\nB. CBC with blood smear\nC. Ophthalmology consultation\nD. Ferritin level\n2330.34 year old G2P1 pregnant found out that her baby has Single umbilical artery, she has hx of a multiple\npregnancy, and hypothyroidism on thyroxine. what is the risk factors?\nA. Age\nB. Parity\nC. Multiple pregnancy\nD. Hypothyroidism", "id": 295, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2331, "stem": "7 Years old complains of chronic clear thin nasal discharge and easy nasal itching, o/e there is periorbital dark swollen and pale turbinates mucosa. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Allergic rhinitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Foreign body (it was written foreign “boy” lol)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "TB"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Rhinitis medicamentosa"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "2332.23 weeks primagravida came with minimal vaginal spotting, no pain, no contractions.\nUS showed viable baby with cervical length of 30mm, most appropriate management?\nA. Cervical cerclage\nB. conservative management\nC. Administration of corticosteroids\nD. induction? C sec? I can’t remember\n2333.A 26-Year-Old Woman Of Average Size Has Delivered A Baby Boy Within The Last 24 Hours. Her Blood\nGroup Is B-Negative, Her Husband Is O-Positive, And Her Newborn Son Is A-Positive. A 300 μg Dose Of\nAnti-D Immunoglobulin Has Been Prescribed. Which Of The Following Volume Of Fetal Blood Will Be\nProtected By The Prescribed Dose?\nA. 15 mL Of Fetal Whole Blood\nB. 30 mL Of Fetal Whole Blood\nC. 60 mL Of Fetal Whole Blood\nD. 30 mL Of D-Positive Red Cells", "id": 296, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2334, "stem": "40-50? year-old man undergoes colonoscopy, which reveals a 1.5 cm polyp in the colon Histopathology: Tubular Adenoma found and removed completely When should the next colonoscopy screening be performed?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "3-6 months"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Every 3 years"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Every 10 years"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "No screening needed"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 297, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2335, "stem": "A 10 year old came 5 days post tonsillectomy with bleeding. Which of the following is the cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Foreign body"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Infection"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Incomplete resection"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Coagulopathy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 298, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2336, "stem": "40yo female healthy but recurrent UTI, 4 times in 1 year wants to prevent it Whats the best clinical based evidence choice to prevent recurrent UTI?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hydration"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cranberyy juice"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Postcoital hygiene"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Prophylactic antibiotics"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 299, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2337, "stem": "A 5-year-old child presents with history of increasing eye puffiness for 1 week especially in the morning. He has had flu-like illness 2 week ago. On examination, he looks well, not in distress and no lower limb edema. Eye, chest and abdomen exams are normal . Blood pressure 100/70 mmHg Heart rate 88 /min Respiratory rate 18 /min Temperature 36.6 °C Oxygen saturation 95 % Test Result Normal Values Total Proteins 35 56-80 g/L Albumin 16 36-52 g/L Protein +4 Absent Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Nephritic syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Nephrotic syndrome"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hereditary angioedema"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Protein losing enteropathy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 300, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2338, "stem": "A guy with severe perineal pain and swelling, discoloration that is expanding with crepitus, what is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cellulitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Testicular torsion"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Fournier's gangrene"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 301, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2340, "stem": "16 yo is pregnant and wants to get an abortion due to socioeconomic status. The Dr is persuading her not to terminate due to personal belief. What should the physician's stance be?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give the advice in a non-bias way"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Say abortion is prohibited by religion"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Take stance of the dr and don’t abort"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 302, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2341, "stem": "A pregnant woman in labor is found to have fetal distress based on the cardiotocography (CTG) monitoring. The doctor decides that a cesarean section (CS) is necessary for the well-being of the baby. However, the mother refuses the CS and requests a second opinion. What should be the doctor's next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Respect the mother's request and arrange for a second opinion."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Consult with the father of the baby before making a decision."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Proceed with the recommended cesarean section."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Both parents' consent is needed in the emergency situation"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 303, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2342, "stem": "A Pregnant Woman In Active Labor Presents With Signs Of Obstructed Labor And Fetal Distress. She Is Counseled For An Emergency Cesarean Section (CS), But She Refuses The Procedure And Requests A Second Opinion. What Is The Most Appropriate Next Step In Managing This Situation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Proceed With The Cesarean Section Against Her Will Due To Fetal Distress"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Prioritize The Father’s Opinion Over The Mother’s Decision"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Respect Her Refusal And Request For A Second Opinion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Require Consent From Both The Mother And Father Before Proceeding"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 304, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2344, "stem": "Father has SC trait with 2 brothers who have SCD. Married a woman her family without SCD and the woman didn't test yet. What is the risk for baby?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "High risk"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "No risk"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Advice not to have child"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Low risk and nil if mother test negative"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 305, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2347, "stem": "A 14-year-old patient presented with a painless neck swelling. Examination confirmed a midline neck swelling below the hyoid bone that moves up and down with swallowing. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cystic hygroma"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thyroglossal cyst"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Midline dermoid cyst"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Submental lymphadenopathy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 306, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2348, "stem": "A 60-year-old man with chronic history of heartburn for several years, presented with occasional hematemesis. Upper I endoscopy revealed intestinal-type metaplasia at the distal esophagus. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oesophagitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mallory-weiss tear"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Barretts oesophagus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Plummer vinson syndrome"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 307, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2349, "stem": "Old male presented with left side body weakness resolve completely after 20 minutes, brain ct normal, carotid ultrasounds show stenosis > 55% What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aspirin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Apixaban"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Warfarin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Carotid endarterectomy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 308, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2350, "stem": "Senna syrup is used to treat chronic constipation, which of the following is considered a serious adverse effect / side effect ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Diarrhea"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Abdominal cramps"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hypokalemia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Synergic effect"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 309, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2351, "stem": "A 6-years old with noticed progressive jaundice and mild tremors with decreased school performance. On examination there is stigmata of few chronic liver injuries. See labs. Hb 7 Retics 6 Direct bilirubin 20 Total bilirubin 25 What is the gold standard for diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Liver biopsy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Liver HIDA scan"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bone marrow aspiration"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "24h urine copper excretion"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 310, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2353, "stem": "A 2-year-old girl brought to Primary Clinic because the mother noticed she is pale. She had history of drinking 3 glasses of cow's milk daily, very picky eater and refusing to eat meat. The mother gave history of similar presentation on 2 of her brother . Test Result Normal Values RBC 3", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "8-7.1 x 1012/L (Newborn) 4.6-4.8 x 1012/L (Child) Hb 4 165-195 g/L (Newborn) 112-165 g/L(Child) MCH 18 28-33"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pg/cell"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "MCV 62 80-95 f1"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Reticulocyte 3 0.2-1.2 %"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Platelets count 480\n150-400 x 109/L\nFerritin 9 20-200 g/L\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\nA. Sickle cell disease\nB. Alpha thalassemia trait\nC. Iron deficiency anemia\nD. Anemia of chronic disease", "id": 311, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2354, "stem": "A 2-year-old girl brought to Primary Clinic because the mother noticed she is pale. History of drinking 3 pints of cow's milk daily and is a very picky eater. The mother gave history of similar presentation on 2 of her brother . Test Result Normal Values RBC 3 4.8-7.1 x 1012/L (Newborn) 4.6-4.8 x 1012/L (Child) Hb 4 165-195 g/L (Newborn) 112-165 g/L (Child) MCH 18 28-33 pg/cell MCV 62 80-95 fl Reticulocyte 3 0.2-1.2 % Platelets count 480 150-400 x 109/L Ferritin 9 20-200 ug/L Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sickle cell disease"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Alpha thalassemia trait"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Iron deficiency anemia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Anemia of chronic disease"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 312, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2355, "stem": "A 2-Year-Old Girl Brought To Primary Clinic Because The Mother Noticed She Is Pale. She Had History Of Drinking excessive amounts Of Milk and refuse to eat and solid food, Very Picky Eater And Refusing To Eat Meat. The Mother Gave History Of Similar Presentation On 2 Of Her Brother. Lab Results: RBC: 3 Hb: 4 MCH: 18 MCV: 62 Reticulocyte: 3% Platelets Count: 480 Ferritin: 9 Which Of The Following Is The Most Likely Diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sickle Cell Disease"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Alpha Thalassemia Trait"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Iron Deficiency Anemia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Anemia Of Chronic Disease"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 313, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2356, "stem": "An 8-year-old girl presents to the Emergency Room by her mother after ingestion of large amount of acetaminophen tablets. According to the mother, she ingested full 2 packs, containing 20 tablets. 1 day ago after she had fight with her. On examination, she has right upper quadrant abdominal pain, with nausea and vomiting Blood pressure 95/50 mmg, Heart rate 135 /min, Respiratory rate 25 /min Temperature 36.6 °C Oxygen saturation 95 % Which phase of acetaminophen toxicity she is at?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Phase 1"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Phase 2"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Phase 3"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Phase 4"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 314, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2357, "stem": "10 Y/O Child with cystic fifibrosis, and the parent is asking what the proper counseling for this patient is. Which of the following is most appropriate?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oral glucose tolerance test yearly"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fasting blood glucose yearly"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "No Need to test until 18 years old"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Random blood glucose every six months"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 315, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2358, "stem": "A 2-year-old girl with a high, relapsing fever of 9-day duration associated with marked irritability. a non-purulent conjunctivitis, red cracked lips, swollen and erythematous hands and feet, and a maculopapular erythematous rash on the trunk and extremities. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Epstein bar viral infection"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Kawasaki disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Rubella infection"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Measles"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 316, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2359, "stem": "During a normal newborn exam, an absent red reflex in the eye and cardiac mummer are discovered. After interviewing the mother, she gave a history of viral infection during the first trimester. Which of the following infections can cause this?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Rubella"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Syphilis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Toxoplasmosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cytomegalovirus"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 317, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2360, "stem": "A 3-year-old girl found during health campaign in poor area to have generalized edema, sever wasting, hyperkeratosis, distended abdomen and sparse hair . Blood pressure 85/45 mmHg Heart rate 80 / min Respiratory rate 25 /min Temperature 36.0 °C Test Result Normal Values Sodium 132 134-146 mmol/L Potassium 3.2 3.5-5.1 mmol/L Magnesium 0.4 0.7-1.0 mmol/L Glucose", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "1 2.8-5 mmol/L (Newborn) 3.3 -5.5 mmol/L (Child)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Albumin 20 36-52 g/L"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A. Vitamin B12 deficiency"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "B. Vitamin D deficiency"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Which of the following contributes to the finding?\nC. Severe protein malnutrition\nD. Sever carbohydrate malnutrition", "id": 318, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2361, "stem": "A 50-year-old man with a history of heavy smoking presents for a routine health checkup. He is asymptomatic and has no known chronic medical conditions. According to preventive screening recommendations, which of the following conditions is most important to screen for in this patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Osteoporosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Colon cancer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Abdominal aortic aneurysm"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Prostate cancer screening"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 319, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2362, "stem": "A 26-year-old male presents for evaluation of a systolic murmur noted during a routine checkup. He is asymptomatic, with no history of chest pain, dyspnea, or palpitations. On auscultation, a mid-systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur is heard best at the apex. Bedside maneuvers: The murmur increases with standing or Valsalva The murmur decreases with handgrip or squatting What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Degenerative mitral regurgitation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Secondary mitral regurgitation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Functional mitral regurgitation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mitral valve prolapse (MVP)"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 320, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2364, "stem": "Child presents 6 hours after ingestion of a coin. The child is clinically stable. Radiographic imaging confirms the coin is in the stomach. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ultrasound"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Immediate endoscopy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Wait until it passes"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "CT abdomen"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 321, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2366, "stem": "2 years old boy was playing with his brother and fell downstairs had breif jircky movements at home and on exam was drwosy and lathergic What is the most appropriate ( without next)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "CT brain"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Observe"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Blood electrolytes"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Neurosurgery referral"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 322, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2367, "stem": "18 months old had kawasaki and was received IVIG, which vaccine can she take in her appointment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "MMR"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Varicella"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "DTap"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Measles"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 323, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2368, "stem": "A 67-year-old man presents with insomnia, irritability and palpitation for 3 months. He is known to have hypertension, depression and atrial fibrillation. He is on amiodarone, fluoxetine and enalopril. Clinically, he is unremarkable. Blood pressure 130/70 mmHg, Heart rate 76 /min, Oxygen saturation 95 % Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add propranolol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Measure thyroxin level and TSH"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Substitution of antidepressant drug"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Refer him for psychiatric assessment"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 324, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 2369, "stem": "33 weeks preterm baby diabetic mother, presented with grunting, respiratory distress What to do (with picture of ground glass appearance) :", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bilateral chest tube insertion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Iv antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refer to cardiology"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Intratracheal exogenous surfactant"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 325, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2371, "stem": "A 6-year-old girl developed reddish urine. Her parents state that she had been constipated for the last 2 weeks and recently became incontinent. On examination, there is abdominal tenderness, and no organomegaly . Blood pressure 115/76 mmHg Heart rate 76/min Respiratory rate 27 /min Temperature 38.9° C Test Result Normal Values WBC 9.5 4.5-10.5 x 109/L Hb 79 112-165 g/L Platelets count 325 150-400 × 109/L Urine Analysis Result Normal Values Leukocytes 25-30 0-3 per high power field Erythrocytes 30-35 0-2 per high power field Protein + Absent Blood +++ Absent Which of the following is most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urinary tract infection"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Henoch-schönlein purpura"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Autoimmune haemolytic anemia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Post streptococcal glomerulonephritis"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 326, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2372, "stem": "A 7-month-old infant presents with signs of UTI, Stable not dehydrated. Alert and feeding. Urinalysis : + Nitrite, +leukocytes. Fever: 39.1 What’s the appropriate empirical antibiotic to be started?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oral amoxicillin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Oral azithromycin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ciprofloxacin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "IV Cefepime"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 327, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2373, "stem": "A 9-year-old girl is brought to the Emergency Department with abnormal movements and a recent history of tea colored urine. Her parents described a preceding skin eruption. On examination, the child looks unwell, unresponsive and lower limb swelling is noted . Blood pressure 155/105 mmHg Heart rate 110/min Respiratory rate 25 /min Temperature 37.4° C Test Result Normal Values Hb 78 112-165 g/L Platelets count 165 150-400 x 109/L Blood urea nitrogen 13.1 1.8 to 6.4 mol/L Creatinine 101 27-88 mol/L Complement C3 0.5 0.7-1.5 g/L Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Post streptococcal glomerulonephritis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hemolytic uraemic syndrome"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Henoch-schönleinpurpura"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Acute renal failure"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 328, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2374, "stem": "Previously healthy 13-month-old girl has 2-day history of fever and increasing cough. She continued to be breastfed with no vomiting. Her immunizations are up to date. She is alert and mildly ill appearing. On examination, there is no grunting or chest retractions. There are crackles heard over the right lung base. The remainder of the examination findings are normal. She has no known allergies. Blood pressure 110/70 mmHg Heart rate 1 4 6 / m i n Respiratory rate 45/min Temperature 38.6 °C Oxygen saturation 95 % Which of the following is the most likely pathogen?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Moraxella catarrhalis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mycoplasma pneumonia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Streptococcus pneumonia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Haemophilus influenza type B"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2375.A 12-year-old boy has joint hyperextensibility and is taller than his classmates (1.78m). No systemic abnormalities\nnoted.\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\nMarfan syndrome\nB. Ehlers-Danlos syndrome\nC. Klinefelter syndrome", "id": 329, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2376, "stem": "A 37-year-old G3 P1011 at 28 weeks' gestation had vaginal spotting 2 weeks ago but no abdominal cramping or passing any clots or tissue. Examination reveals fundal height equivalent to 18 weeks' size. Ultrasound confirms lack of fetal heart activity and no fetal movement. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Fetal demise"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Missed abortion"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Incomplete abortion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Gestational trophoblastic disease"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 330, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2377, "stem": "Child with allergic rhinitis still sneezing and nasal cognition even after take antihistamine:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Intranasal corticosteroid"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Montelukast"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Oral decongestant"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Allergy immunotherapy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 331, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2378, "stem": "Boy complains of URTI, treated. 2 weeks later comes with itchy nose, and congestion on examination there is tenderness on the frontal cheeks . What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bacterial Sinusitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Allergic rhinitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Foreign body obstruction"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Nasal polyps"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 332, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2379, "stem": "A 45-year-old diabetic man presented with dull aching headache increases with straining and coughing, there is tenderness all over the eyebrows. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Viral meningitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "TB meningitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Brain abscess"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Sinusitis"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 333, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2381, "stem": "A 10-month-old boy is brought to the OPD. His mother reports he missed his 7-month and 9-month vaccinations. What is the best course of action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Start catch-up vaccination"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give all missed vaccines in a single dose"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Do not give him the missed vaccines"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Restart vaccination schedule from beginning"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 334, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2383, "stem": "A pediatric patient came to the ER complaining of abdominal pain and gaseous distention, he had 7 times foul smelling watery diarrhea. His parents report going into camping where the source of water was contaminated What is the most appropriate management", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Supportive"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Clindamycin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Metronidazole"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Loperamide"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 335, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2384, "stem": "A7-year-old boy who has recently visited his grandfather's farm, now presents to you with diarrhea, miosis, vomiting, lacrimation, salivation, excessive urination, and a garlic odor in his mouth. Which of the following should be administered?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Atropine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "N-acetylcysteine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Flumazenil"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Sodium bicarbonate"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 336, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2385, "stem": "47-year-old woman with multiple comorbidities, including obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), presents with heavy menstrual bleeding secondary to uterine fibroids. She has completed her family and prefers a non-surgical treatment. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "GnRH agonist"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Uterine artery embolization"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Counsel her that the definitive treatment is hysterectomy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Myomectomy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 337, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2386, "stem": "Acute mesenteric ischemia history of ischemic stroke 1 month ago what is the initial management ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Angiography and embolectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thrombolysis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IV hydration and heparin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Us abdomen"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 338, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2387, "stem": "A 3-year-old child presents with symptoms consistent with measles. Three of the child’s siblings also have similar symptoms.v What is the most likely mode of transmission of this infection?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Airborne"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Droplet"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Fecal-oral"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Bloodborne"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 339, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2389, "stem": "Female at 39w of gestation, Examination shows polyhydramnios, Which Condition might be associated with her?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Trisomy 21"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Renal agenesis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intrauterine Growth Retardation (IUGR)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pneumothorax"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 340, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2391, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman in her first pregnancy had an ultrasound confirming Dichorionic Diamniotic twins. What was the time interval between ovulation and cleavage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0-72 hours"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "4-8 days"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "9-12 days"}, {"letter": "D", "text": ">12 days"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 341, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2392, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman in her first pregnancy had an ultrasound confirming Monochorionic Diamniotic twins. What was the time interval between ovulation and cleavage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "0-72 hours"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "4-8 days"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "9-12 days"}, {"letter": "D", "text": ">12 days"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 342, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2394, "stem": "8 y.o child presents with recurrent chest pain, found in high status, joints are laxed, and fingers arachnodactyly Which of the following is most appropriate to rule out life threatening causes?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "ECG"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "MRI Brain"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CT Chest"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Echocardiography"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 343, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2395, "stem": "A 5-week-old infant is brought to the clinic with a 3-week history of intermittent vomiting. After vomiting, the infant becomes very hungry and wants to feed again. Physical examination shows no alterations apart from a", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "50 kg weight loss ."}, {"letter": "B", "text": ", Chloride 89 97-108 mmol/L"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "A. Gastric volvulus"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "B. Cyclic vomiting syndrome"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Test Results Normal Values Sodium 147 134-146 mol/L , Potassium 2.9 3.5-5.1 mmol/L ,Bicarbonate 32 21-28 mmol/L\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\nC. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis\nD. Intestinal malrotation with volvulus", "id": 344, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2396, "stem": "A 6 years old was brought by his parents with history of sudden abrupt loss of environmental awareness with lip smacking followed by sudden return to normal baseline. Brain Imaging= Normal, EEG 3-Hz spikes Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Complicated febrile seizure"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Absence seizure"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Infantile spasm"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cerebral palsy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 345, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2397, "stem": "Patient arrived to ER after head injury, upon Glasgow Coma Scale calculation, he can open eyes to painful stimuli, localizes the pain and says groaning sounds only, what is the calculated GCS for him?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "8"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "9"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "10"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "11"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 346, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2398, "stem": "An obese young lady presented with irregular periods. Her BMI is 35. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassure her that irregular periods are normal"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Weight reduction and lifestyle modification"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Start oral contraceptive pills (OCPs)"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 347, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2399, "stem": "Female diagnosed with pCO and she is trying to conceive what to give :", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "OCP"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Metformin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Clomiphene citrate"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 348, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2400, "stem": "66-year-old man with hypertension comes to the clinic for a follow-up. He is currently taking a combination of losartan and amlodipine, which is failing to keep his blood pressure within target. Examination is unremarkable. His blood test and renal function profile were within the normal limits. What is the most appropriate next step assuming he has no relevant contraindications?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add ramipril 1.25mg od"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add atenolol 50mg od"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add doxazosin 1mg od"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add indapamide MR 1.5mg od"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 349, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2401, "stem": "Newborn with midline face deformity diagnosed with panhypopituitarism ( diagnosis is written) Thyroid low , Cortisol low , Growth Hormone low ( written no values ) What to do ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Give hydrocortisone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give thyroxine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give recombinant growth hormone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Wait 1 month and repeat labs"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 350, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2402, "stem": "Regnant woman requires an urgent cesarean section. The physician explains the risks and benefits. Her husband agrees to the procedure, but she refuses. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Prevail upon the mother's decision"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Prevail upon the father's decision"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refer for psychiatric assessment"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Perform the cesarean section despite her refusal"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 351, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2403, "stem": "10 weeks pregnant with ruptured ectopic pregnancy and hypotensive and unconscious:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Wait family to consent"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Proceed for surgery"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Court order for consent"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 352, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2404, "stem": "A 4-Year-Old Child Presents With Shortness Of Breath And Cough Following Flu-Like Illness For 2 Days. Examination Reveals Child In Severe Distress With Increased Effort Of Respiration With Bilateral Wheeze And Prolonged Expiratory Phase. He Had Similar Attacks Before That Required Hospitalization. Blood Pressure: 100/65 mmHg Heart Rate: 124 /min Respiratory Rate: 33 /min Temperature: 36.6 °C Oxygen Saturation: 82 % Which Of The Following Is The Most Appropriate Management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Antibiotic And Systematic Steroid"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ventolin Nebulization And Antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intravenous Fluid And Ventolin Nebulization"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ventolin Nebulization And Systematic Steroid"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 353, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2406, "stem": "Pediatric patient age 2 days old post SVD presented to the ER with bleeding and hemarthrosis labs showed Prolonged PTT, Normal PT no family history of bleeding Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Factor 8 VIII disease"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Syndrome..."}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Liver disease"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 354, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2407, "stem": "Ld Patient came with Cirrhosis and massive hematemesis, became drowsy, and blood oozing out from his mouth, What is the next step of management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Blood transfusion"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Endotracheal tube"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Injection Octreotide"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Urgent Endoscopy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 355, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2408, "stem": "39 wks pregnant with IUFD what to do:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "C/S"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Discharge for spontaneous delivery"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Induction of labour with oxytocin and PGE2"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 356, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2410, "stem": "A 24 year old female G2P1, found to have a 7 week gestation ectopic pregnancy. One dose of methotrexate was given, and the following β-HCG values were measured from dose date: -Day 1: 1800 mmol -Day 2: 1200 mmol -Day 3: 400 mmol -Day 4: 70 mmol -Day 5: 70 mmol Which of the following approach would you do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Continue monitoring β-HCG and send home"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Immediate surgical management"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Laparotomy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Administer methotrexate regimen"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 357, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2411, "stem": "A 35-year-old woman presented to the clinic for preconception counseling. She is known to have hypertension and is on ACE inhibitors. Which of the following is the most appropriate for her antihypertensive medication when she gets pregnant?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Continue on ACE inhibitors as it is safe"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Continue because the benefit outweighs the risk"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cannot be stopped, so she should not get pregnant"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Switch to alternative antihypertensive due to fetal kidney teratogenicity"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 358, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2412, "stem": "A 6-year-old girl presents to the Emergency Department with a 10-hour history of feeling unwell and tired. On examination, she is pale, has a palpable liver, and her spleen is 6 cm below the costal margin. Vital signs: BP 93/55 mmHg, HR 169/min, RR 28/min, Temp 37.5°C Lab results: Hb: 52 g/L (112–165) Platelets: 102 × 10⁹/L (150–400) Sickle cell screen: Positive What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Sequestration crisis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Evans syndrome"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bacterial sepsis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Aplastic crisis"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 359, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2413, "stem": "A middle-aged man with diabetes mellitus is on metformin and aspirin. His labs show elevated creatinine and high BUN. He is planning to travel and asks about prophylaxis for traveler’s diarrhea. Which of the following is the most appropriate option?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bismuth subsalicylate"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fluoroquinolone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "No medication"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Probiotics"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "We need the exact number of Cr level and BUN, If severe CKD or ESKD then answer is A", "id": 360, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2414, "stem": "Patient in 60s diagnosed with prostate cancer presenting with back pain , urine retention and maybe lower limb weakness What is the Appropriate Next Step:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refer to physiotherapy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Outpatient MRI"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IV Dexamethasone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Oral analgesia and reassess after 24 hours"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 361, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2415, "stem": "A mother was concerned of ADHD in her child (8 years) . She described him at home as restless and cannot focus. Upon your assessment he is a calm child with normal behavior. What is the best action:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Obtain further information from parents and teachers"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Start him on trial of amphetamines"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reassure the mother that these are normal behaviors at his age"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Do neurological exam"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 362, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2416, "stem": "8 years old girl brought to primary healthcare clinic by her mother, complaining of poor concentration and hyperactivity at school. Her teacher noticed that she has difficulty staying seated at her place. The mother reported that her daughter only sleeps 5 hours per night from 1 AM to 6 AM. What’s the most appropriate step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Start Melatonin 1 Mg daily"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Neurological examination"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Structured Sleep routine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Blood test to check for Anemia"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2417.7-year-old child is reported to be hyperactive, restless, talks rapidly, and has violent outbursts. What intervention\ncan help mitigate these issues?\nA- Regular preschool examinations\nB- Decreasing time spent on video games and watching television\nC- Parenting classes for behavior management\nD- Special education resources", "id": 363, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2419, "stem": "24-year-old obese woman, no previous pregnancy, complains of irregular menstrual periods. Menarche was at age of 14 and her periods were irregular in timing, duration and flow. The last menstrual flow was scanty 3 months ago. History of weight gain since menarche along with facial acne and mild facial hirsutism . Test Result / Normal Values: Testosterone: 3.4 (0.7–2.8) Sex hormone-binding globulin: 9 (13–17) Luteinizing hormone (LH): 31 (5–22) Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH): 2.1 (5–20) Random Glucose: 6.1 (3.9–5.5) Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Turner’s syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Exogenous obesity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Premature ovarian failure"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Polycystic ovary syndrome"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 364, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2420, "stem": "A healthy 7-year-old boy presents with short stature. He has normal growth velocity but delayed bone age. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassure the parents that the child will reach normal height by puberty"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Order basic investigations (CBC, electrolytes, bone profile)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Advise more sunlight exposure"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Advise avoidance of dairy products"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 365, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2421, "stem": "A 3-month-old boy presented to the Emergency Room with history paroxysmal episodes of cough followed by whooping sounds at the end of each episode. It is associated with perioral cyanosis and post- tussive vomiting. There is no history of diarrhea. This illness has been preceded by runny nose a few days ago. History revealed he did not receive his 2-month vaccine yet. On examination, the child looks unwell and tachypneic. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pertussis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adenovirus pneumonia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mycoplasma pneumonia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Chiamydia trachomatis pneumonia"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 366, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2422, "stem": "A 3-month-old boy presented to the Emergency Room with history paroxysmal episodes of staccato cough, inspiration between each cough. It is associated with perioral cyanosis and post-tussive vomiting. There is no history of diarrhea. This illness has been preceded by conjunctivitis few days ago. On examination, the child looks unwell and tachypneic with auscultatory crackles. He has normal white blood cell count with elevated eosinophils on laboratory evaluation. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pertussis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adenovirus pneumonia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mycoplasma pneumonia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Chlamydia trachomatis pneumonia"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 367, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2423, "stem": "A 3-month-old boy presented to the Emergency Room with history paroxysmal episodes of cough associated with an inspiratory whoop and possessive emesis. It is associated with fever, conjunctival redness and diarrhea. His immunization is up to date. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pertussis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adenovirus infection"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mycoplasma pneumonia"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Chlamydia trachomatis pneumonia"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 368, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2424, "stem": "A 38-year-old woman presented with a 7-weeks of frequent, loose and bloody bowel movements that associated with campy abdominal and rectal pain. She has family history of crohn's disease. Stool analysis is negaive . Blood pressure 110/70 mmH, Heart rate 76 /min, Temperature 36.6 °C Test Result Normal Values RBC 9.2 4.7-6.1 × 1012/L (Male)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "2-5.4 x 1012/L (Female)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hb 111 130-170 g/L (Male)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "120-160 g/L (Female)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "HCT 0.41 0.42-0.52 (Male)"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "0.37-0.48 (Female)\nMCH 27 28-33 pg/cell\nMCHC 330 320-360 g/L\nMCV 77 80-95 f1\nPlatelets count 220 150-400 x 109/L\nC-reacive pepide 22 <8.2 mg/L\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in establishing diagnosis?\nA. Anorectal manometry\nB. Upper GI endoscopy\nC. Celiac disease tesing\nD. Measurement of fecal calprotecin", "id": 369, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2426, "stem": "Young male presented with vomiting of blood , he was heavy alcohol consumption stable endoscopy show esophageal varices what is the cause of varices :", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Acute pancreatitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Portal hypertension"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Essential HTN"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Budd Chiari syndrome"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 370, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2427, "stem": "A 30-year-old man with a known diagnosis of type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with polyuria and polydipsia. He is taking vitamin D supplements for a previous deficiency. Family history: relative with type 1 DM. Labs: Elevated PTH Normal calcium HbA1c: 8% Normal urine osmolality and serum osmolality Elevated C-peptide: 0.09 ng/mL (Low) Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Diabetes insipidus (DI)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Type 1 diabetes mellitus (DM1)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus (DM2)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hyperparathyroidism"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 371, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2428, "stem": "A patient is invited to participate in a clinical trial for a new drug. She is worried that she might be placed in the placebo group. The doctor reassures her by saying, Don’t worry, You Will Receive Standard Care Which ethical principle is most relevant to this situation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Beneficence By Providing Standard Care"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Non-maleficence By ....."}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Autonomy By Shared Decision Making (Smth Like This)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Justice By ...."}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 372, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2429, "stem": "5 year old child going for preschool vaccination, what is the most important prevaccination preassessment", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Egg allergy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Received recent immunoglobulin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Current fever"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lookout for previous vaccine site"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 373, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2430, "stem": "47-year-old woman with large symptomatic uterine fibroid, heavy menstrual bleeding. Completed childbearing. Significant comorbidities: uncontrolled DM and OSA. Prefers non-surgical management.", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "GnRH agonist"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "UAE"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Counsel for hysterectomy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Counsel for myomectomy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 374, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2431, "stem": "46-year-old woman undergoes a routine screening mammogram, reported as completely normal. She has no symptoms and no family history. When should she do her next mammogram?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "6 months"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "1 year"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "2 years"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "3 years"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "\"Above 50 → C. 2 years\"", "id": 375, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2432, "stem": "Female 42 wants cancer screening, smoker for 18 years, no family history of cancer:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bilateral mammogram"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Colon cancer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lung cancer"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "All of the above"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 376, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2433, "stem": "A mechanic had an accidental injection of oil dissolving in his index finger. The finger is red and swollen with mild tenderness and normal joint movement. What to give??", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Abx"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Abx and steroids"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Surgical drainage"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Elevation and ice packing"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 377, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2434, "stem": "79-year-old male patient, post cholecystectomy, developed confusion and agitation. What to do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Restraining"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Keep in quiet room"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give antipsychotic"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Routine orientation"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 378, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2436, "stem": "4 months old with poor weight gain. At birth it was 3.3 kg, then became 4 kg. His mother uses 2 spoons of milk in 90 mL water, she feeds him every 3 hours. She's 19 years old, that's her first baby. What is the most likely cause of failure to thrive?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Child neglect"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Celiac disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Inappropriate formula preparation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Decrease feeding frequency"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 379, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2439, "stem": "A 15-year-old girl is brought for evaluation of primary amenorrhea. Physical examination reveals normal breast development but absent pubic and axillary hair. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Androgen insensitivity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Müllerian agenesis (MRKH)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Gonadal failure"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Turner syndrome"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 380, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2440, "stem": "FEMALE AT 37 weeks, what is prerequisite of ECV?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Maternal fasting 6 hrs"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Early and late dating confirmation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "30 min fetal monitoring"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 381, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2441, "stem": "33-year-old nulliparous woman with cyclic pelvic pain and dyschezia underwent diagnostic laparoscopy. Most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Pelvic TB"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Adenomyosis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Endometriosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pelvic inflammatory disease"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 382, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2442, "stem": "Developed large neck swelling on the left side. On exam it is large 10 cm in size, US FNA showed clear lymphatic fluid. What is the appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Surgery"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Observation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Chemotherapy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Radiotherapy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "No Sclerotherapy", "id": 383, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2443, "stem": "A patient underwent surgery and developed an infection as a complication. There was a nurse from another department who is a family friend. She opened the patient's file to see why she got the infection, \"to check on her.\" The patient found out and filed a complaint. What's the proper action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ignore and dismiss the issue"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Train the staff on confidentiality and privacy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Inform patient and document"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Investigation and take a legal action"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 384, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2444, "stem": "A 29-year-old woman presented to the clinic unsure of her gestational age. She missed her period 3 months ago but her periods are irregular. Ultrasound: determined gestational age as 11 weeks. Which of the following was measured in the ultrasound to determine gestational age?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Femur length"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Crown-rump length"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Abdominal circumference"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Biparietal diameter and head circumference"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Second Trimester ➔ D\nThird Trimester ➔ C", "id": 385, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2445, "stem": "12-week-old child with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome with the mother concerned about increased risk for liver lesion. What tumor marker will you use?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urine oxalic level (UOL)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prostate-specific antigen (PSA)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Urine Homovanillic acid (HVA) and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA)"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "AFP is used to screen for hepatoblastoma in Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome", "id": 386, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2446, "stem": "Patient came to ER after he loss concious after he regained it he experience sever headache describing it as worse headache he ever had plain ct showed: Subarachnoid Hemorrhage and Hydrocephalus how are you going to investigate the affected artery ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "MRI with contrast"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lumpur puncture"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CT angiography"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Transcranial doppler ultrasound"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 387, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2448, "stem": "A 50-year-old woman with a history of involuntary urine leak, especially when she runs. Which of the following is the most appropriate next clinical evaluation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Q-Tip test"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cough test"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bonney test"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Uroflowmetry"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 388, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2450, "stem": "24-year-old primigravida presents with the diagnosis of right ectopic pregnancy. The ultrasound revealed a 3 cm right tubal gestational sac with 5-week crown rump length, negative fetal heart activity and minimal amount of fluid in the pouch of Douglas. Her social history reveals that she lives in a village 80 km away from the city center . Blood pressure 90/50 mmHg Heart rate 110 /min Temperature 35.6 °C BMI 36 kg/m² Human chorionic gonadotropin 3500 Which of the following would be the absolute contraindication to the use of methotrexate for this patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "The B-hCG level and ultrasound findings"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "The far distance from medical services"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "The high body mass index"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "The vital signs"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 389, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2451, "stem": "Women had a baby through a success vaginal delivery. What are you going to do to prevent infection during post partum period ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hand washing"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Switz bath"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prophylactic antibiotics"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Frequent irrigation of the perineal area with special Sanitizers"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 390, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2452, "stem": "RTA with dull percussion on exam and chest tube was inserted, Which of the following is an indication for surgical intervention?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Initial output > 500 ml"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ongoing output more than 200 ml/hr"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Combined hemothorax with pneumothorax"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Drop in Hemoglobin and hematocrit"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 391, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2455, "stem": "40-year-old patient diagnosed with essential HTN, the doctor discussed with him the role of physical activity in reducing cardiovascular risk. Which of the following is the recommended physical activity?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "10 min swimming once a week"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Weight lifting every 2 weeks"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "30 min brisk walking for 5 times a week"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "10 min cycling most days of the week"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 392, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2456, "stem": "A 6-year-old boy complains of passing Coca-cola-coloured urine for the last week, with periorbital puffiness worse in the morning and fever. Congested throat and anterior cervical lymphadenopathy on exam. Which is the next best investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urine culture"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Renal ultrasound"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renal biopsy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Urine analysis"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 393, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2457, "stem": "A kid who won't move his lower limb presents with a swollen, hot, tender knee. What to do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Blood culture"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Synovial fluid aspiration"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IV antibiotics"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2458.A 7-year-old child presents with signs of meningitis. Laboratory investigations reveal a\nblood glucose level of 4.8 mg/dL. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis shows:\nProtein: 450 mg/dL (normal range: 150–450)\nGlucose: 3.8 mg/dL (normal range: 2.8–4.8)\nPredominant lymphocytic pleocytosis\nWhich of the following is the most likely cause of meningitis in this case?\nA. Viral\nB. Bacterial\nC. Tuberculous\n2459.A 41-year-old woman Para 4 presented to the Gynecology Clinic with post-coital bleeding for the past three months.\nHer cycle is irregular, and she is not taking any contraception. Examination revealed a soft abdomen with no\npalpable masses.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\nA. Pap smear\nB. Pelvic ultrasound\nC. Endometrial biopsy\nD. Dilatation and curettage\n2460.A mother brings her infant to the well-baby clinic, where the physician decides to prescribe\nvitamin D. Which of the following is the best way to write the prescription?\nA- 400 U PO OD\nB- 400 Unit PO OD\nC- 400 Unit PO daily\nD- Four hundred unit oral daily\nHow to write Vitamin D dose ?\nA. Vit D 400 U OD\nB. Vit D 400 units OD\nC. Vit D 400 unit daily\nD. Vit D 400 U daily\n2461.During nurse handover, it is discovered that a child with peanut allergy and asthma received medications without the\nallergy being documented.\nWhich ethical principle applies?\nA- Proper documentation of patient history\nB- Use two patient identifiers\nC- Improve communication to ensure patient safety and good healthcare\nD- Patient education\n2462.A 15-year-old boy presents to the clinic for an annual physical assessment. The patient has normal growth and\ndevelopment and is able to do usual activities. His elder brother died due to a sudden cardiac death while walking to\nwork. On physical examination, there is no cardiac murmur. Pulses are equal in all extremities.\nWhich condition must be excluded before any sport is allowed?\nA. Bicuspid aortic valve\nB. Patent ductus arteriosus\nC. Ventricular septal defect\nD. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy", "id": 394, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2463, "stem": "A 20-year-old man with a history of high liver function test in the past presents, with tremors and dysarthria and dyskinesia. Slit-lamp examination reveals a greenish-brown ring at comeoscleral junction. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Antitrypsin deficiency"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hepatic hemangioma"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hemochromatosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Wilsons disease"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 395, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2465, "stem": "After accident a patient came with warm lower extremities with normal chest exam , BP was low, HR70 what kind of shock?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hypovolemic"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Neurogenic"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cardiogenic"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Septic"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 396, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2467, "stem": "A patient has recently stopped smoking and has started using nicotine replacement patches. The patient is motivated to quit smoking and is actively taking steps towards achieving that goal. Which stage of smoking cessation is the patient currently in?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Action"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Preparation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Contemplation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Precontemplation"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 397, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2469, "stem": "A 45-year-old smoker came to the clinic for his diabetes follow-up. During discussion, he acknowledged that smoking is not good for his health. He plans to quit this year. According to the Stages of Change Model, at what stage of change is this patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Precontemplation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Contemplation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Preparation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Maintenance"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 398, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2470, "stem": "A patient presents to the clinic seeking help with smoking cessation. He already bought nicotine replacement and he has desire to stop? What this stage called:", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Preparation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Action"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Contemplation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Precontemplation"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 399, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2471, "stem": "A newborn develops respiratory distress shortly after birth. Chest X-ray shows fluid in the interlobar fissures. What is the most important in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "NPO (withhold oral feeding)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Start empirical antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give normal saline"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 400, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2474, "stem": "Man had a car accident and he had “ interval consciousness” he was okay for 6 hours and get drowsy all over again", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Subarconid hemorrhage"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Subdural hemorrhage"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Extradural hemorrhage"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 401, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2475, "stem": "Man with ALL started chemotherapy and he developed fever after 8 days : I am sure it was 8 days .", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Reassures"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Broad spectrum antibiotics"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antibiotic and antifungal"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 402, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2476, "stem": "9 years old child who had been bed weeting ,” his mother said he had POLYURIA AND POLYDIPSIA “ What is the firts invX :", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urine cultural"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Behavioral changes"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Random blood glucose"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 403, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2478, "stem": "25-year-old male came recently from India, presented to ER with RUQ pain, Night sweats, malaise, fever. US showed: right lobe Liver collection, Hypoechoic CT Finding: Thick walled abscess without septations. Which of the following is the diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Amoebic liver abscess"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pyogenic liver abscess"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hydatid cyst"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "TB abscess"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 404, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2479, "stem": "Old age with 2 year history of progressive memory loss, had multiple attacks of weakness, decline in abilities and cognitive function, he was diagnosed with HIV 3 years ago, diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Vascular dementia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lewy body"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Alzheimer"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hiv related dementia"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 405, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2482, "stem": "A 60-year-old woman with a history of rheumatoid arthritis is treated with prednisolone 60 mg orally once daily for an acute exacerbation. She is instructed to continue the treatment for a short period and then gradually taper the dose. However, the patient fails to follow the tapering instructions and continues taking prednisolone 60 mg daily for several days. She is subsequently found dead at home. Which of the following is the most likely cause of death?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Acute perforated peptic ulcer"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Acute cerebral vasculitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hemorrhagic cerebrovascular accident"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Acute adrenal insufficiency"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Acute pancreatitis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2483.34 weeks gestation presents to ER with ROM.placenta was 1cm dilated.She delivers vaginally after 6hrs and with 2nd\ndegree perineal tear.Placental Delivery was delayed and was removed manually.\nwhich of these indicates the reason of giving prophylactic antibiotics ?\nA.2nd degree tear\nB.ROM for more than 6 hrs\nC.precipitous Labour\nD. Manual removal of placenta", "id": 406, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2484, "stem": "Young boy had upper respiratory tract infection three weeks ago. Now, dyspnea on exertion, and on examination tachypnea, tachycardia, gallop rhythm, bilateral crackles on the chest, on X-ray bilateral opacities. Which if them is most helpful in confirming diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Tachycardia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tachypnea"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "History"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Gallop rhythm"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 407, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2485, "stem": "17-year-old girl complained of anorexia nervosa was admitted for initiation of nutrition, the blood samples revealed : hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia What is the cause of the laboratory investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Wrong protocol of nutrition."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Wrong test"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refeeding syndrome"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Wrong kit in lab"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 408, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2486, "stem": "A ]age not recalled[ male presented to the emergency department with icterus and jaundice. Serology showed: Hepatitis A: • Anti-HA V IgM — positive • Anti-HA V IgG — positive Hepatitis B: • Anti-HBs (surface antibody) — positive • HBsAg (surface antigen) — negative • Anti-HBc (core antibody) — negative • HBeAg (e antigen) — negative What is the diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Acute hepatitis A"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Chronic hepatitis B"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Acute hepatitis A with immunization against hepatitis B"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Acute hepatitis A with previous hepatitis B infection"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 409, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2487, "stem": "Which of the following cerebral palsy types is associated with Kernicterus?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Spastic"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ataxic"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hypotonic"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Dyskinetic"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 410, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2488, "stem": "A 26-year-old woman presents at 29 weeks of gestation with prelabor premature rupture of membranes for 7 days. Examination revealed she is having lower abdominal pain, rigors, and fever with purulent, foul-smelling vaginal discharge. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Chorioamnionitis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Urinary tract infection"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Antepartum hemorrhage"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lower respiratory tract infection"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 411, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2489, "stem": "A 35-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a 5-month history of fatigue. She takes no medication. Her examination is unremarkable . Tsh 14 Normal t 4 T.cholestrol 5.3 Ldl 2.7 ?? Tg slightly high What is the most appropriate treatment for this patient's dyslipidemia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Statin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Fibrate"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Niacin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Thyroxine"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 412, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2490, "stem": "A 26-year-old pregnant woman visits the clinic for regular follow-up. She is asymptomatic. She is in her 30th week of pregnancy. Her physical examination reveals a mid-systolic ejection murmur at the right upper sternal border. The murmur radiates to carotids. No diastolic murmur is heard. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aortic stenosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mitral stenosis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tricuspid stenosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Physiologic murmur of pregnancy"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 413, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2491, "stem": "A 77-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension presents with a 6-months history of fatigue and dyspnea on exertion. He denies edema, orthopnea, chest pain and palpitations. His only medication is hydrochlorothiazide (25 mg) once daily. On examination, he has a grade 2/6 midsystolic ejection murmur, which is loudest at the right upper sternal border. An S4 is heard at the apex, there is no S3. His lungs are clear and there is no peripheral edema. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aortic regurgitation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mitral regurgitation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Aortic stenosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mitral stenosis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 414, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2492, "stem": "A 55-year-old man presents to the clinic with progressive shortness of breath on exertion for 8 months. He has no history of chest pain or other systemic manifestations. Physical examination reveals an early diastolic murmur at the left upper sternal border that is augmented when sitting forward . Chest radiography: Cardiomegaly. ECG: Left ventricular hypertrophy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aortic stenosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mitral stenosis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Aortic regurgitation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mitral regurgitation"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 415, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2493, "stem": "A 33-year-old man presented with shortness of breath, orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea. Examination revealed collapsing pulse and wide pulse pressure. Early diastolic murmur over left sternal border and pistol-shot sound heard over femoral arteries. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Coarctation of aorta"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Aortic regurgitation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cardiac tamponade"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ventricular septal defect"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 416, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2494, "stem": "A 66-year-old man presents with a diastolic murmur best heard at the left sternal edge. On examination, the apex beat is displaced outwards. Which condition typically presents with these findings?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Coarctation of the aorta"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Aortic regurgitation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Mitral regurgitation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Aortic stenosis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 417, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2495, "stem": "56-year-old man has a low-pitched diastolic rumble heard at the apex, a widened pulse pressure and a grade 3/6 diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pulmonic insufficiency"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Aortic regurgitation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Mitral stenosis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 418, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2498, "stem": "A diabetic patient presents with an ulcer with its granulated in its center over the left lateral border of the foot left dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulse are intact. Which type of diabetic foot ulcer is most likely?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Neuropathic ulcer"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ischemic ulcer"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Venous ulcer"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pressure ulcer"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 419, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2499, "stem": "A 12 months baby presented by his parents for check up , found that he didn’t receive any vaccines except at birth vaccine. Normal development. parents reported that he has recurrent URTIs and because of that they delayed the vaccinations until become healthy completely. What is the next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refer the parents to social worker"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Do blood test and immune test for baby"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reassurance and catch up all vaccines"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Told them that the delaying in vaccines is the cause of recurrent infxs"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 420, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2506, "stem": "Patients pregnant asthmatic and didn’t have an attack for 6 months (implying controlled asthma). What is most likely to be progressively increasing during pregnancy?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Respiratory rate"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tidal volume"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Respiratory reserve capacity"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2507.Pt female 20 something.\nBP: 150/(80 something)\nMedically free.\nOn examination: flank bruit.\nInvestigation to confirm her diagnosis:\nA. Abdominal CT\nB. Plain abdominal radiograph\nC. Renal US\n2508.Pediatric patient with history of trauma (or injury) presented with severe pain out of proportion to the visible area and\ncrepitation.\nFebrile.\nManagement?\nA. Surgical consultant and IV antibiotics\nB. Oral antibiotics\n2509.Patient single, sexually active. She has a problem remembering taking daily medications. She wants contraception that\nprovides privacy and long-term protection.\nA. Etonogestrel implant\nB. OCP\nC. Diaphragm\nD. Condom", "id": 421, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2516, "stem": "15 years old Turner ( 45 X ) they didn’t mention turner only the karyotype , she presented with no breast development and High FSH and LH and low estradiol . What do we give her?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Estrogen replacement therapy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Growth hormone only (they literally said only)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Observe until 16"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 422, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2532, "stem": "5-year-old child presents to the Emergency Room with severe sore throat, high-fever and increasing difficulty breathing over 8 hours. On examination, ill toxic looking child with drooling of saliva and sitting immobile with open mouth. Blood pressure 110/70 mmHg Heart rate 115 /min Respiratory rate 32 /min Temperature 38.8 °C. Oxygen saturation 95 % Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate action should be taken?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urgent referral to ENT for tonsillectomy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Complete sepsis work up, followed by antibiotics for 7 days"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Send home on oral antibiotics for 7 days and referral to ENT"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Urgent admission to ICU for intubation then sepsis work to follow"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 423, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2533, "stem": "Female presented to clinic complaining of infertility for 4 years Normal labs Pic shows Bilateral tube blockage What is the most likely diagnosis according to Xray Pic?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hydrosalpinx"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ovarian Cyst"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Adhesions"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 424, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2534, "stem": "Elderly Presents To The Emergency Department with bright blood with his stool he known for colonic polyp and hypertension On Ex he is pale and tachycardic Vitals : BP 160/90 HR : 128 What is the most appropriate initial ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Urgent colonoscopy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ct abdomen"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Oral iron ( yes I am sure)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "IV fluid and blood"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 425, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2535, "stem": "A patient with recent rectal surgery comes to you with absent pulses up to the femoral area. How will you manage such a case?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Unfractionated heparin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Enoxaparin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "IVC filter"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Thrombolysis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 426, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2536, "stem": "A17-year-old man is evaluated in the Emergency Department for polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia and an unintentional weight loss over the past month. He has abdominal pain and drowsiness for the last 21 hours. Hsi medical and family history are unremarkable. He is not of any medications. On physical examination, he looks sick with dry mucous membrane, tachypnea, and tachycardia with clear chest auscultation. Abdominal examination shows diffuse mild tenderness with normal bowel sounds. Intravenous 0.9% saline is initiated. An electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia 120/min. Chest radiograph si normal . Blood pressure 95/53 mmHg. Heart rate 119 /min Respiratory rate 20 /min Temperature 37.2 C°Oxygen saturation 95 % BMI 19 kg/m2 Sodium 130 134-146 mmol/L Potassium 4.4 3.5-5.1 mmol/L Chloride 96 97-108 mmol/L Bicarbonate 10 21-28 mmol/L Blood urea nitrogen 15 2.8 to 8.9 mmol/L Creatinine 115 44-115 mol/L Random Glucose 25 3.9-5.5 mmol/L. ABG HCO3-10 22-28 mmol/L рН 7.10 7.36-7.45. RBC 5 4.7-6.1 x 1012/L (Male) 4.2-5.4 x1012/L (Female) Hb 130 130-170 gL/ (Male) 120-160 g/L (Female) WBC 144.5-10.5×109/L. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Administer intravenous ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Initiate intravenous insulin therapy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Administer intravenous potassium chloride"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Administer intravenous sodium bicarbonate"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 427, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2537, "stem": "A patient was brought to the Emergency Department following a road traffic accident. During the initial assessment, the doctor inquired about the airway, to which the nurse responded that it was patent with signs of obstruction. The doctor then inquired about breathing, and the nurse reassured that it was okay. The doctor proceeded to ask about circulation, and the nurse responded once again. What type of communication is demonstrated in this scenario between the doctor and nurse?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Check back"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Situation, background, assessment, and recommendation (all in one answer)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Close loop communication"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Call out"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 428, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2538, "stem": "A 50-year-old man is complaining of pain affecting the wrist joints bilaterally, second and third proximal interphalangeal joints, and right knee joint for the last 4 months. His examination revealed active arthritis at both wrists and second and third proximal interphalangeal joints . Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Methotrexate"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Methotrexate and sulfasalazine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Methotrexate, hydroxychloroquine, and prednisone"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 429, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2539, "stem": "A 19-year-old G1 P0 at 42 weeks is admitted for induction of labor. Her antenatal care was uncomplicated. Examination revealed the cervix was closed and long. Prostaglandin E2 is placed vaginally. An hour later, she started to contract regularly, and the fetal heart dropped to 70 bpm and was not recovering. Her vaginal examination showed a fully dilated cervix and presenting part at +2. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Consider oxytocin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Pressure the patient"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Take her for caesarean section"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Start pushing and consider applying ventouse"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 430, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2540, "stem": "5 months old child admitted to hospital many times with pneumonia. Came now with cough and monophasic bilateral expiratory wheezing . What is the diagnosis test?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Chest x ray"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CBC and blood culture"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Spirometry"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Flexible Bronchoscopy"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 431, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2541, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman with primary infertility for the last 3 years is seen for follow-up in the clinic. Her investigations included a hormonal profile, and an ultrasound is normal. Her husband's semen analysis is also normal. She had a 3-month trial of induction of ovulation with clomiphene. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "In-vitro fertilization"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Diagnostic laparoscopy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Intrauterine insemination"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Induction of ovulation with gonadotropins"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 432, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2542, "stem": "A 70-year-old gentleman known to have liver cirrhosis with esophageal varices underwent endoscopic variceal ligation 1 month ago. Which of the following is the best long-term intervention to decrease the risk of recurrent variceal bleeding?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "B blocker"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "H2 blocker"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Proton pump inhibitor"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "No further intervention"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 433, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2544, "stem": "A diabetic male patient presented with an ulcer on the toes of the foot failed to heal. On examination: absent dorsalis pedis pulse. How would you manage the patient? (More details in the Q but I forget them)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Metatarsophalangeal amputation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Stenting"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Vacuum closure"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 434, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2546, "stem": "Pt had testicular torsion and resolved what to do", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Bilaterl orchopexy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Unilateral orchopexy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Repeat doppler us"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 435, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2547, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman with known sickle cell trait attends an antenatal booking clinic at 8 weeks' gestation. Which of the following antenatal complications is significantly more common in her case?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Chest infection"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Placental abruption"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Urinary tract infection"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Intrauterine growth restriction"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 436, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2548, "stem": "A 16-year-old male presents with right lower quadrant pain ,it is a clear case of appendicitis, and the diagnosis was mentioned He is scheduled for a laparoscopic appendectomy. How should antibiotics be administered?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Preoperative dose, then stop immediately after"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Preoperative dose and continue for three days"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Preoperative dose and continue for four days"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Preoperative dose and continue for five days"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 437, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2549, "stem": "A 26-year-old man complains of pelvic pain after a motorcycle collision. Physical and radiologic examinations confirm a pelvic fracture. Urologic workup reveals a normal urethrogram and extraperitoneal bladder injury. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for his bladder injury?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Immediate surgical exploration and repair."}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Placement of a permanent suprapubic tube"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Catheter drainage followed by definitive repair after 2 weeks"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Catheter drainage for 2 weeks followed by repeat imaging"}, {"letter": "E", "text": "Bilateral nephrostomy tubes"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 438, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2550, "stem": "A 29-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a history of irregular menstrual cycles for the last 2 years. Her last delivery 5 years ago. Currently, she is not on any contraception. Examination shows all within normal limits except breast examination revealed frank galactorrhea. Which of the following is most helpful investigation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Beta-human chorionic gonadotropin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ultrasound of the breast"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Serum prolactin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Thyroid function"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 439, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2551, "stem": "A Young male baby came to well-baby clinic, upon examination his right testis was palpable in the inguinal canal and small in size and easily moved to scrotum, the left is normal, what is the cause?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ectopic testis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Undescended testis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Testicular torsion"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Retractile testis"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 440, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2552, "stem": "A 30-year-old G3 P0020 at 17 weeks gestation is undergoing serial ultrasound assessments of cervical length due to a history of a previous second-trimester abortion. Which of the following features will indicate cervical cerclage before 24 weeks gestation?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cervical length less than 5 mm"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cervical length less than 25 mm"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cervical length less than 35 mm"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Funneling of the internal os"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 441, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2553, "stem": "A 12-year-old girl has typical symptoms of migraine- And she was diagnosed in the stem by migraine - She was treated with first-line therapy but has had no improvement, and symptoms have persisted for 3 weeks. What is the most appropriate second-line treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ibuprofen"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "TCA"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Benzodiazepine"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2554.mother brought her 4 years old child because he frequently wakes up in the middle of the\nnight setting on the bed, eyes opened but unresponsive for 2-3 min then he go back to sleep,\nin the morning, he doesn't remember what happened?\nA. nightmares\nB. night terror\nC. night walking\nD. Epilepsy", "id": 442, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2555, "stem": "A patient presents with palpitations. He denies dyspnea, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, or lower limb edema. ECG shows atrial fibrillation. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Propanalol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Digoxin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Furosemide"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Dobutamine"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 443, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2556, "stem": "Around 17 years with history of Seizure On 400 carbamazabin BID but still has seizure attack What next to mange him", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Increase dose of carbamazabin to 600 BID"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Replace carbamazabin with VA"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Add lamotrigin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Add VA"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 444, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2558, "stem": "Heart Failure With A-Fib, Complain Of Palpitation, Mx?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Carvedilol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Diltazem"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Digoxin"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 445, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2559, "stem": "A 22-year-old man presents to the emergency department with pain and tenderness over the lateral aspect of his left thigh. On examination, there are linear erythematous streaks extending along the leg. The area is warm and tender. He has no significant past medical history. What is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Penicillin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cloxacillin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tetracycline"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Erythromycine"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 446, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2560, "stem": "Patient presents to the emergency department with vomiting and diarrhea shortly after ingesting an overdose of tablets. A few hours later; the patient develops melena, hematemesis, and shortness of breath. Laboratory investigations reveal elevated liver enzymes. Which of the following is the most likely substance ingested?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ferrous sulfate"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Paracetamol"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ibuprofen"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Aspirin"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 447, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2562, "stem": "A woman complains of irregular menses with facial hair (hirsutism) but no signs of virilism. She was already counseled on lifestyle modification. What is the early something promotion (sure about \"early\" and \"promotion\" — unsure if the middle word was \"health\") and preventive measure for the underlying pathology (it was not \"initial\" and not \"most appropriate\" — this is the exact stem wording)?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Refer to endocrinology for adrenal mass"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cosmetic treatment and reassure the patient"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Screen for PCOS with testosterone and pelvic ultrasound"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Start hormone therapy without further testing"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 448, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2565, "stem": "A 28-year-old man is brought to Emergency Room with head injury after motor vehicle accident, he is making incomprehensive sounds, open his eyes to painful stimuli and flexes his limbs to painful stimuli, and his pupils react sluggishly to light. What is his Glasgow Coma Score?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "5"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "6"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "7"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "8"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "Answer C. 7 (MG)", "id": 449, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2568, "stem": "A 43-year-old female, medically free, presents to the clinic after experiencing a single episode of maroon-colored stool. She reports a history of three previous episodes of gastrointestinal bleeding from an unknown location. On examination, she is vitally stable with no abdominal symptoms and no other associated complaints.", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Abdominal X-ray"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Barium Study"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Upper GI Endoscopy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Lower GI Endoscopy"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 450, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2575, "stem": "A 40-year-old woman has just delivered her 7th child at 38 weeks with 4 kg baby boy vaginally 10 minutes ago. The placenta is still in place and she starts to bleed profusely. The estimated amount of blood loss visually is around 800 ml. Which of the following best describes this case?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Secondary post-partum hemorrhage"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Latrogenic post-partum hemorrhage"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Primary post-partum hemorrhage"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Tertiary post-partum hemorrhage"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 451, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2579, "stem": "Woman with typical chronic cholecystitis sys (pain increased following fatty meals, radiating to lt shoulder), GB US shows 2cm polyp", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lithotripsy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Observation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CT"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cholecystectomy"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 452, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2581, "stem": "11 years old girl with multiple joints affection, malar rash, photosensitivity Positive ANA & Anti DNAse + low C3 started on prednisone Which of the following is the most frequently utilized other agent to maintain remission and prevent vascular complications (exactly written like this)", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "HCQ"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "NSAIDS"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cyclophosphamide"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cyclosporine"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 453, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2582, "stem": "10 days old neonate presented with RD and lethargy, on examination: absent femoral pulse (no vitals mentioned) What of the following is the most appropriate initial management", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Surgical repair"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ventricular septoplasty"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Balloon dilatation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "PG infusion"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 454, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2583, "stem": "An 8-year-old child presents with recurrent episodes of cellulitis following minor abrasions sustained during school activities. His mother is concerned about how to prevent future episodes. What is the most appropriate preventive measure?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Limit contact sports"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Wear long-sleeved clothing"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Prophylactic antibiotics"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Clean wounds and cover abrasions with a bandage"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 455, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2586, "stem": "An infant presents with projectile vomiting, dehydration, and failure to thrive. Which of the following acid–base abnormalities is most likely?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hypokalemic metabolic acidosis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hyperchloremic metabolic alkalosis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 456, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2588, "stem": "Some months baby with bowing of the (legs ) which vitamin deficiency", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "D"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "A"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "C"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "B1"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 457, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2589, "stem": "Elderly with end stage cancer c/o severe pain. The family asked the physician to give a stronger analgesic or to increase the dose. The doctor explains that while it might help alleviate the pain, it also might hasten their father’s death. Which of the following is this referred to?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Principle of subsidiarity"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Principle of double effects"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Principle of totality"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Principle of informed choice"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 458, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2593, "stem": "Asymptomatic pregnant asked for urine culture for some job reason, What is the best time to get urine culture for asymptomatic bacteriuria?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "12 weeks"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "20 weeks"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "26 weeks"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "32 weeks"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 459, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2594, "stem": "A 15-year-old boy is brought to the physician by his parents due to a one-week history of fatigue, visible jaundice, and TREMOR in his hands. His parents note that his speech has recently DYSARTHRK Physical examination is notable for scleral icterus and a distinct greenish discoloration encircling the CORNEOSCLERAL junction Laboratory studies are obtained and reveal the following: Direct bilirubin: 1 8 mol Indirect bilirubin: 20 mol (Normal: 1–6 mol) normal < 18 mol AST 1000+ ALT 1000+ Which of the following is most likely to be found in this patient's further diagnostic workup?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "JAK2 gene mutation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Low serum ceruloplasmin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Centrilobular necrosis on liver biopsy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Positive anti-smooth muscle antibodies (anti-SMA)"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 460, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2595, "stem": "Prospective cohort study is being done to assess obesity in pediatric patients, many baseline variables being collected including diet, lifestyle, ………… (many thing) The primary outcome variable was obesity defined as greater the 95%, what is the most appropriate test to be used?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "T test"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Linear regression"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Logistic regression"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Multi varite analysis of variant"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 461, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2596, "stem": "Young man with appendicitis was treated conservatively with antibiotics. He now presents with an appendicular mass but no collection. How should this case be managed?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Interval laparoscopic appendectomy after 12 weeks"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Interval open appendectomy after 12 weeks"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "No further intervention needed"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Colonoscopy after 6 weeks"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 462, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2597, "stem": "11-Boy 4 yo with contact with chikenpox case in the daycare 2 days ago what to do ? He is now asymptomatic", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Acyclovir"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Give vaccine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Give ig"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Wait and observe"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 463, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2598, "stem": "Diabetic girl with BМІ 35 https://t.me/Alhomrani525 21-23July", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "150 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous intensity exercise spread over ≥ 3 days per week"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Harvey exercise every day"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Diet because of her joint"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Bariatric surgery and then see what to exercise"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "2599.30 years female with a family history of DM2 came for advise BМІ 35\nA. 150 min moderate exercise weekly\nB. intense exercise everyday\nC. surgery then diet", "id": 464, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2600, "stem": "Case of incomplete abortion, some tissue still in the uterus by (TVUS), how to manage?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "IM misoprostol"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Oxytocin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Evacuation of uterine cavity"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 465, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2601, "stem": "Child came from Africa (Region Not Specified, Just Africa) with his family, complaining of cyclic fever, fatigue and sweating, the diagnosis is confirmed by blood film (Diagnosis Not Mentioned, Just \"Diagnosis Confirmed\") what is the management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Metronidazole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ceftrixone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Chloroquine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Doxycycline"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 466, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2602, "stem": "Kid with chills and fever just returned from Africa. Blood culture confirms the diagnosis (diagnosis not stated). Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Chloroquine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Metronidazole"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Acyclovir"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "(This was the stem, short without many details)", "id": 467, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2603, "stem": "30 year old women with bronchial asthma came for routine check up, despite adherence to inhalaled corticosteroid complains of intermittent wheezing and coughing and at night, her husband smokes around her,Normal vitals, Most appropriate action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Add leukotriene receptor antagonists (LTRAS)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Add Long-acting beta-2 agonists (LABAs)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Increase the dose of inhaled steroid"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Implementing smoking cessation for the people in the house"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 468, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2605, "stem": "A man, previously divorced, has been diagnosed with familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP).He refuses to inform his current wife and children about his diagnosis or the need for screening, stating that he will complain if his wife finds out. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Respect patient confidentiality"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Let the social worker notify the mother"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Tell the wife because children should get screening tests"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ask Ethics committee"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 469, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2606, "stem": "A 3-year-old child presents with clinical signs of intestinal obstruction. Physical examination reveals a sausage-shaped mass in the right abdomen. The child is irritable, exhibits episodic crying with leg flexion, and may have red currant jelly stools. What is the definitive in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Enema"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Surgery"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Endoscopy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "IV antibiotics only"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 470, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2608, "stem": "Pedia with burning micturition from one week what to give?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Amoxicillin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cephalexin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 471, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2609, "stem": "A 3-year-old girl is brought to the Emergency Department with fever and dark urine. Her parents state that she had been constipated. On examination, she looks unwell and irritable with abdominal tenderness, but no palpable liver or spleen . Blood pressure 125/86 mmHg Heart rate 125 /min Respiratory rate 29/min Temperature 39.9° С Hb 81 112-160 g/L Platelets count 325 150-400 x 109/L Urinalysis Test Result Normal Values Leukocytes 16 0-3 per high power field Erythrocytes 18 0-2 per high power field Protein ++ Absent Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hypertensive nephropathy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Acute glomerulonephritis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Acute pyelonephritis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Acute cystitis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 472, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2610, "stem": "A 6-Year-Old Girl With Recurring UTI Complaining Of Fever, Dysuria, And Vomiting. The Mother Says She Had 3 Previous Similar Episodes. What Investigation Would You Do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Renal US"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "MCUG"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Renal US + MCUG (PRESENT TOGATHER IN ONE OPTION)"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 473, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2611, "stem": "Patient of stab wound measuring 2 cm penetrating injury with minimal bleeding and partially omentum exposed patient is vitally stable, CT report negative findings, Next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Observation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Close the wound"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Leave the wound open"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Laparotomy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 474, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2614, "stem": "A 62-year-old diabetic woman was admitted to the Intensive Care Unit with pneumonia and sepsis . Chest X-ray: Right lobe consolidation and pleural effusion. Which of the following most likely indicates a poor prognosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Age"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Diabetes mellitus"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "High white blood cell count"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pleural effusion on chest X-ray"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 475, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2615, "stem": "Pt with COPD came with fever and yellow sputum (signs of chest infection) PH : low Pco2 : very high O2: very low I Don’t remember the numbers Diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "COPD exacerbation with acute respiratory failure type 1"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "COPD exacerbation with acute respiratory failure type 2"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "COPD exacerbation with mixed respiratory failure"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 476, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2616, "stem": "Patient post MVA with abdominal pain", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "FAST: +ve fluid in splenic area"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Bp: 99/70"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Pulse: between 102-107"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "(Sure about the numbers)"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Next step?\nA- CT\nB- laparotomy\nC- MRI", "id": 477, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2618, "stem": "A 3-month-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his mother due to poor feeding and fast breathing. The mother reports that he easily \"tires out\" and sweats heavily across his forehead during breastfeeding, causing poor weight gain. He was born at full term with no complications and has normal facial features. On physical examination, he is tachypneic and a harsh holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border. His liver is palpated 3 cm below the right costal margin. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his symptoms?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Ventricular septal defect (VSD)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Atrial septal defect (ASD)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Coarctation of the aorta"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 478, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2619, "stem": "A patient presents with social withdrawal, abnormal behavior, auditory hallucinations, and behavioral changes. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Generalized anxiety disorder"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Major depressive disorder"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Bipolar disorder with psychotic features"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Schizophrenia / schizophrenia-spectrum psychotic disorder"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 479, "specialty": "Psychiatry"}, {"q_num": 2620, "stem": "A 30-week pregnant woman is diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy. Her left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) is 35%. Which of the following medications is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Losartan"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Enalapril"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Methyldopa"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hydralazine"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 480, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2623, "stem": "A patient with sickle cell disease presents with acute chest syndrome. While receiving a blood transfusion, he suddenly develops fever and chills. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hydroxyurea"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Stop transfusion immediately"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hydration"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "ABC (Airway, Breathing, Circulation)"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 481, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2624, "stem": "A 24-Hour-Old Neonate With Ambiguous Genitalia. 17-Hydroxyprogesterone Was Abnormally High : Test Result - Normal Values Sodium 127 134-146 mmol/L Potassium 6.8 3.5-5.1 mmol/L Bicarbonate 15 21-28 mmol/L Glucose 2.5 2.8-5 mmol/L (Newborn), 3.3-5.5 mmol/L (Child) Which Of The Following Is The Inheritance Of This Disease?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Multifactorial"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "X-linked recessive"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Autosomal dominant"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Autosomal recessive"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 482, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2625, "stem": "20 year old , medically free presented with epigastric tenderness for 1 month not related to any thing,associated sometimes with heartburn and vomiting. + history of mother having non specific abdominal pain Patient had + blood and mucous in stool asking what to give", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Omeprazole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tetracycline"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Nonallergic diet ( not relevant much)"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 483, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2628, "stem": "A woman has a 3 x 3 cm axillary mass for the past 3 months. On examination, the node is mobile and has not changed in size. What is the next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lymph-node core needle biopsy"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mammogram"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Observation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Breast biopsy"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 484, "specialty": "Surgery"}, {"q_num": 2631, "stem": "An 18-year-old patient came with reduced level of consciousness and began to seize. While in the ambulance, he was in deep sleep and regained consciousness in the ER. He has HIV with questionable treatment adherence and drinks alcohol heavily. On examination, there is tooth clenching and a temperature of 37.9°C. What is the next step in management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "LP"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Urine toxicology screen"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "CT"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 485, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2634, "stem": "Patient presents with shortness of breath and productive cough, he is on lisinopril, hydrochlorothiazide, amlodipine, warfarin, acid fast bacilli was positive, anti-TB 4 medications was initiated. What drug should be adjusted?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Warfarin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lisinopril"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Hydrochlorothiazide"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Amlodipine"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 486, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2635, "stem": "Elderly Male On Amlodipine, Beta Blocker, And Lisinopril Presents With Flu-Like Symptoms. Acid-Fast Bacilli Result Came Positive, And He Was Started On First-Line Anti-TB Regimen. Which Antihypertensive Drug Should Be Adjusted?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Amlodipine"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Beta Blocker"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Lisinopril"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Forget"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 487, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2636, "stem": "A 43-year-old man presented to the hospital with right leg swelling and pain. He has a history of pulmonary embolism 5 years ago and is currently not taking any medications. Doppler ultrasound confirmed the diagnosis of proximal Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT). Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aspirin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thrombolysis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Long-term anticoagulation"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Anticoagulation for 2 months"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 488, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2637, "stem": "A 30-year-old woman G2 P2002 at 32 weeks' gestation presents to Emergency Department complaining of lower abdominal and back pain which has increased in frequency and intensity over the last few hours. Abdominal examination shows fundal height equals to 32 cm, longitudinal lie fetus and cephalic presentation. Fetal heart was positive and cardiotocography is reactive with 2-3 uterine contractions/10 minutes. Vaginal examination shows 1 cm dilated cervix, 70% effacement, -3 station and cephalic. Which of the following is the next step to", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Continuous cardiotocography"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "B. Lecithin-sphingomyelin ratio"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "C. Repeat vaginal assessment in two hour-time"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "D. Cervical length assessment by ultrasound scanning"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "confirm the diagnosis?\n2638.A 30-year-old G3 P1010 woman presented at 24 weeks of gestation with preterm labor. She gave a history\nof preterm delivery at 34 weeks in her first pregnancy. Which of the following is the best method to\nevaluate her risk of preterm labor?\nA. Fetal fibronectin\nB. Uterine contractions\nC. Serial pelvic examination\nD. Cervical length measurement", "id": 489, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2654, "stem": "A sickle cell disease patient underwent bone marrow transplant, post transplant he developed febrile neutropenia. What is the appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Cefepime"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vancomycin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Doxycycline"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 490, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2655, "stem": "Patient underwent bone marrow transplantation due to SCD, developed febrile neutropenia and has pneumonia, she was given Ceftazidime and shows no improvement , in first week what to add?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Vancomycin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tazocine"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Amphotericin b"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 491, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2656, "stem": "A 17 year old developed febrile neutropenia and shock post chemotherapy for lymphoma. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial antibiotic?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Meropenem"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vancomycin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Cefuroxime"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Caspofungin"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 492, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2657, "stem": "A Sickle cell disease patient who underwent bone marrow transplant. He got a fever 1 day after the procedure. He was started on ceftazidime but did not improve after 72 hours. There was no pneumonia or hypotension. What to add ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Voriconazole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sulfamethoxazole"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Vancomycin"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 493, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2658, "stem": "Patient underwent bone marrow transplantation due to SCD, developed febrile neutropenia and has ]NO[ Pneumonia , she was given Ceftazidime and shows no improvement, what to add?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Vancomycin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Tazocin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 494, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2660, "stem": "A 30 or 40 -year-old man noticed easy bleed when he brush his teeth. He presented with a rash on his lower legs, and he has very severe thrombocytopenia, 5 × 10⁹/L . Also hemoglobin was below normal ( 12.5) , WBC: 7.6 On blood film, there was no schistocyte and no platelet clumping", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura ( ITP)"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Aplasic anemia"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 495, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2662, "stem": "Strongest risk factor related to endometrial hyperplasia/cancer ?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Infertility"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Obesity"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "COCP"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Menarche at young age"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 496, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2663, "stem": "Which of the following is the most suggestive of hemolysis as a cause for neonatal hyperbilirubinemia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Low reticulocyte count"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Nega9ve direct Coombs test result"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Increased conjugated bilirubin level"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Increased unconjugated bilirubin level"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 497, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2665, "stem": "A 76-year-old with prosthetic mitral valve presents with fever and a new murmur; blood cultures pending. Best empirical antibiotic regimen?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Vancomycin + ceftriaxone"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Vancomycin + gentamicin + rifampicin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Vancomycin alone"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Ceftriaxone alone"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 498, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2666, "stem": "A 65-year-old smoker with PAD on aspirin/clopidogrel/insulin has normal lipids. What should be added?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Beta-blocker"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Calcium channel blocker"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Atorvastatin"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Losartan"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 499, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2667, "stem": "65-year-old man with diabetes and hypertension is admitted with extensive anterior ST elevaon myocardial infarcon (STEMI). had successful angioplasty and stenng to le anterior descending artery. Aspirin, clopidogrel, bisoprolol and enalapril were started . Test Result Normal Values Cholesterol (HDL) 1.0 > 1.03 mmol/L Cholesterol (LDL) 3.36 <3.36 mmol/L Total Cholesterol 5.0 <5.1 mmol/L Triglycendes 2.2 <2.16 mmol/L Which of the following is recommended?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "No additional medication is indicated"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Atorvastatin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Fenofibrate"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Niacin"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 500, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2668, "stem": "A 34-year-old asymptomatic with fasting glucose 7.3 and HbA1c 6.3%. Best next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Repeat A1c in 6 weeks"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Repeat fasting glucose in 3 months"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Oral glucose tolerance test"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Random blood glucose"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 501, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2669, "stem": "55-year-old man with a family history of type 2 diabetes undergoes screening . Blood pressure 130/85 mmH Heart rate 80 /min Height 185 cm Weight 91 kg Test Result Normal Values Glucose, fasting 7.4 3.5-6.5 mmol/L Hemoglobin A1C 6.3 4.7-5.6% Which of the following should be recommended?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Random plasma glucose"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Haemoglobin A1C after 6 weeks"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Fas7ng plasma glucose after 3 months"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "2-hour 75 g oral glucose tolerance test"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 502, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2670, "stem": "An 18-year-old with new T1DM after DKA, now stable and ready for discharge. Best insulin regimen?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Twice-daily premixed insulin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Basal-bolus (glargine + aspart)"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Sliding scale only"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "NPH twice daily"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 503, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2671, "stem": "A 20-year-old woman is diagnosed of type 1 diabetes mellitus aer presenng with diabetic ketoacidosis. Her blood sugars are now stable and she is ready for discharge. Which of the following is the best insulin regime she should be offered as outpaent?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Twice daily NPH insulin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Once daily mixtard insulin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Basal-bolus insulin regimen with once-daily insulin glargine"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Rapid-acng insulin analogue before each meal with no longer acng insulin"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 504, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2672, "stem": "A 45-year-old with DKA, K+ 2.5 mmol/L, started on IV fluids and insulin. What complication should be anticipated?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Hyperglycemia rebound"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Worsening hypokalemia"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Metabolic alkalosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Hypercalcemia"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 505, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2673, "stem": "A 20-year-old with diffusely enlarged thyroid, irregular pulse, high T4/T3, low TSH. Initial symptomatic treatment?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Methimazole"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Propylthiouracil"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Beta-blocker"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Radioactive iodine"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 506, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2676, "stem": "An 18-year-old female with dizziness on standing and hyperpigmented skin creases, low morning cortisol. Best confirmatory test?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Dexamethasone suppression test"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "24-hour urinary cortisol"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "ACTH (synacthen) stimulation test"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Urinary catecholamines"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 507, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2680, "stem": "Which of the following osteoporosis treatment has FDA approval to treat malignancy induced hypercalcemia?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Denosumab"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Alendronate"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Ibandronate"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Recombinant human 1-34 PTH (teriparade)"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "Alhomrani", "id": 508, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2681, "stem": "A 27-year-old presents with sudden severe headache while bending down, described as \"worst of my life.\" CT brain is negative. Next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "MRI brain"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Lumbar puncture"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "EEG"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Echocardiography"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 509, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2682, "stem": "A 63-year-old with sudden left-sided weakness for 2 hours, CT shows no hemorrhage, BP 160/95. Most appropriate management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Aspirin"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Thrombolytic therapy"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Control blood pressure only"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Close observation"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 510, "specialty": "Uncategorized"}, {"q_num": 2683, "stem": "A 24-year-old with ascending symmetric limb weakness 2 weeks after gastroenteritis, absent reflexes. Most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Myasthenia gravis"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Guillain-Barré syndrome"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Multiple sclerosis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Transverse myelitis"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 511, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2684, "stem": "A 12-year-old girl comes to the clinic after developing progressive weakness and inability to walk. She has intermi]ent tingling of her fingers and toes. History reveals she had 3 days of diarrhea about 2 weeks ago. Physical examination confirms marked peripheral muscle weakness without atrophy or fascicula7on. The ankle and patella reflexes are absent, and a sensory examination is normal. Motor involvement is symmetric. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Guillain-Barré syndrome"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Mononeuri7s multiplex"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Myasthenia gravis"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Polio"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 512, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2685, "stem": "A 55-year-old with resting tremor, bradykinesia, and shuffling gait. Site of degeneration responsible?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Basal ganglia"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Cerebellum"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Substantia nigra"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Cerebral cortex"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 513, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2687, "stem": "A 67-year-old with hypertension develops sudden right-sided weakness, aphasia. CT shows hypodensity in left MCA territory 6 hours after onset. Best management?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Thrombolytic"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Aspirin"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Control BP and admit"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Anticoagulation"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 514, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2688, "stem": "A patient with severe asthma exacerbation cannot complete a sentence, PEFR 30% predicted. This indicates?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Mild exacerbation"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Moderate exacerbation"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Life Threatening asthma"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Well-controlled asthma"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 515, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2689, "stem": "A 51-year-old woman with asthma complaining of increasing shortness of breath and wheeze in the past few hours. She has a long-standing diagnosis of asthma but her medication history suggests a lack of compliance. Her best peak flow measurements in previous consultations have been 300 L/min. Which of the following features suggests acute severe asthma in this patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Heart rate 100/min"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Respiratory rate >20/min"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Peak flow rate 200 L/min"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Inability to complete sentences in one breath"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "", "id": 516, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2690, "stem": "A 31-year-old man with asthma complaining of increasing wheeze in the past few hours. His medication history suggests a lack of compliance. His best peak flow measurements in previous consultations have been 600 L/min. Which of the following features suggests acute severe asthma in this patient?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "A peak flow rate of 250 L/min"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Respiratory rate >20/min"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Heart rate 100/min"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "SpO2 95%"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "", "id": 517, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2691, "stem": "A 31-year-old man with asthma complaining of increasing wheeze in the past few hours. He rarely attends asthma clinic and his previous best peak flow readings has been 400 L/min. Which of the following would suggest acute severe asthma?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "02 saturation <95%"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Respiratory rate >25"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Carotid pulse > 100 bpm"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Peak flow rate <300 L/min"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 518, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2692, "stem": "A 65-year-old smoker with dyspnea, FEV1/FVC <0.7, DLCO low, minimal reversibility. Most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Asthma"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Chronic bronchitis"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Emphysema"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Pulmonary fibrosis"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "", "id": 519, "specialty": "Internal Medicine"}, {"q_num": 2693, "stem": "A child presents with a band-like headache that has been occurring for the past 2 weeks. He has been spending several hours playing electronic games for the past 3 weeks. There are no other concerning symptoms or neurological abnormalities. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Lifestyle modification"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "CT scan of the brain"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Paracetamol"}], "answer": "A", "explanation": "2694.6 year old child brought by mother for fever and diarrhea. The child look well and all examination and vitals\nunremarkable. When asked about vaccine mother said “she did not and will not vaccinate as she thinks they are unsafe\nWhat is yhr most appropriate management from the GP?\nA- report to ministry of health\nB- explain vaccine are safe and should be used\nC- refer to pediatric for management\nD- ignore vaccine and manage", "id": 520, "specialty": "Pediatrics"}, {"q_num": 2695, "stem": "You are a GP and a mother refuses vaccination for her children. What should you do?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Report to the Ministry of Health"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Explain the benefits of vaccination"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Refer her to a pediatrician"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "2696.18-month child was brought to the clinic for a regular checkup. The doctor found out that the child had not taken any of his scheduled vaccines. He asked the mother why and she replied “Vaccines will make my son retarded!”. What is the appropriate action?\nA) Refer the mother for psychiatric assessment\nB) Contact child protection services\nC) Explain the benefits of vaccines to the mother\nD) Contact the hospital ethics committee\n2697.45 year old woman presents with multiple large tender uterine fibroid. She is bleeding and something. She completed child\nbearing and would like to have a non surgical approach\nNot vitals or labs, examination mild tend diffuse abdomen\nA- Gnrh\nB- uterine embolization\nC- inform her that surgical is best and she should do total abdominal hysterectomy\nD- informer surgcal is best and she should do myomectomy\n2698.A 47-year-old woman with a large symptomatic uterine fibroid presents with heavy menstrual bleeding. She has completed\nchildbearing and has significant comorbidities, including uncontrolled diabetes mellitus and obstructive sleep apnea. She\nprefers non-surgical management. What is the most appropriate management?\nA. GnRH agonist\nB. Uterine artery embolization\nC. Inform her that surgical management is preferred and counsel her regarding hysterectomy\nD. Inform her that surgical management is preferred and counsel her regarding myomectomy", "id": 521, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2699, "stem": "19 year first time pregnant woman came with her 6 month or 1 year for failure to gain weight. birth weight is 3.3kg and he is now 4kg. she feeds him 2 table spoon in 90ml water bottle every 3 hrs. note: no mention of anything else but this. also no mention of breastfeeding the infant. what is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Neglect"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Celiac disease"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Reduced frequency in feeding"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Improper bottle preparation"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "2700.Patient had low risk and uncomplicated pregnancy comes for labor. Which of the following is the best intrapartum\nsurveillance to ensure maternal and fetal wellbeing during labor?\nA- Maternal pulse and blood pressure monitoring\nB- Uterine contractions monitoring\nC- Early identification of possible labor complications\nD- Intermittent fetal heart monitoring as it'", "id": 522, "specialty": "Ethics & Biostatistics"}, {"q_num": 2701, "stem": "46.pregnant g3 p2 at 38w admitted to L&D with controlled hypertension on medication Baby is 8th% Asking about intrapartum monitoring", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Maternal vitals monitoring"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Intermittent fetal heart monitoring"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Continuous fetal cardiotocography as a high risk pregnancy"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Early identification for labor complications"}], "answer": "C", "explanation": "2702.pregnant, 20weeks gestation, TB symptoms, PE: shows bilateral dulleness on apical lungs. Fever, neck stifness, and blood\nshowed interferon gamma assay\nvitals normal BP, 37.5C, 110 pulse\nthe question was which one of the following is the BEST NEXT TO REACH DEFINITIVE DIAGNOSIS?\nA- CXR\nB- Sputum smear\nC- Something i cant remember but far off\nD- Sputum smear cytology\n2703.A 28-year-old woman G 4 P2012 immigrant presented to the Maternal Health Clinic at 20 weeks' gestation with a cough\nand night chills for the past month. The cough is productive and produces thick sputum. She had been prescribed\ntreatment by a general practitioner for the cough and it had not responded to treatment. Patient nail beds and conjunctiva\nappeared pale and the cervical lymph nodes are palpable. Auscultation of the chest shows crepitation over the\nupper left lobe. She had received a BC vaccine as a child. A blood test is positive for interferon-gamma release assays.\nBlood pressure 110/70 mmHg\nHeart rate 84 /min\nRespiratory rate 18 /min\nTemperature 37.5° C oral\nWhich of the following is the next best step in obtaining a definitive diagnosis?\nA. Chest X-ray\nB. Sputum culture\nC. Tuberculin skin test\nD. Sputum smear cytology\n2704.A 35-year-old nulliparous woman at 24 weeks. Insulin-dependent diabetes class F with nephritis, chronic hypertension on medication. Fundus 25 cm, fetal heart baseline 160 bpm. Which complication is most likely?\nA. Stillbirth\nB. Pre-eclampsia\nC. Shoulder dystocia\nD. Large for gestational age\n2705.A 35-year-old nulliparous woman presents to the Maternal Health Clinic at 24 weeks' gestation.\nThe significant medical history includes insulin-dependent diabetes class F with nephritis. Additionally, she has chronic hypertension which is controlled with medication. The pregnancy has been uncomplicated so far. The uterine funds measured 25 cm above the symphysis and fetal heart tones show a baseline of 160 beats per minute.\nWhich of the following complications is most likely to occur?\nA. Stillbirth\nB. Pre-eclampsia\nC. Shoulder dystocia\nD. Large for gestational age infant\n2706.A 6-month-old infant presents with recurrent pneumonia, chronic diarrhea, and otitis media. Laboratory evaluation\nreveals normal T-cell counts and undetectable levels of IgG, IgM, and IgA. What is the most likely diagnosis?\nA. Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID)\nB. X-linked (Bruton) Agammaglobulinemia\n2707.Case of pedia pt with nephrotic syndrome on high dose of prednisolone 4 weeks\nago and came for preschool vaccine\nA. delay all vaccines until he is off treatment\nB. give only dtap\nC. give dtap and mmr but no varicella or OPV\nD. don’t give Dtap but give varicella\n2708.A women is planning to get pregnant ,what is the recommended dose of folic acid in (mg)?\n2709.A 35-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Room with sudden onset of shortness of breath associated with Severe\nStabbing pleuritic chest pain. . She is using oral contraceptives regularly.\nD dimer is ELEVATED .\nCTPA showed saddle embolus\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate managment? ?\nA. UFH\nB. Aspirin and clopidogrel\nC. warfarin.\nD. something but NO tPA\n2710.6 month infant came with mother Fever plus vomiting. Ill looking, lethargic. PE: revealed mass in upper right quadrant sausage shape like. what is the most appropriate management?\nA- Barium enema\nB- Surgical intervention\nC- Antibiotic\nD- Something i cant remember but not it tho", "id": 523, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2712, "stem": "8 yrs girl came with hx of fever ,sore throat, painful swallowing.. on examination there is bilateral enlargement tonsils .. she start on amoxicillin and after 3 days she divolpe maculopopular rash over trunk :", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Measles"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Scarlet fever"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Kawasaki"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Infectious mononucleosis"}], "answer": "D", "explanation": "2713.25 years Female complain of breast pain , fatigue start 5 days before menstrual and last for 2 days after :\nA. Pre menstrual syndrome\nB. Pre menstrual dysphoric\n2714.Child came with 3 days hx of headache and neck stiffness.. he denised photophobia ..\nSerum glucose: 4.4\nCSf analysis show :\nGlucose 3.8\nProminent lemphocytes\nProtien 450 (normal limit was up to 450)\nWhat is the diagnosis?\nA. TB\nB. Viral\n2715.Young female asymptomatic accidental find of 2cm subserosal fibroid .. she is concern about her future infertility :\nA. Reassure\nB. Myomectomy\n2716.i dont remember the age. baby came with persistent left eye looking medial and absent red reflex. what is the most\nappropriate next step for managment?\nA- eye patch\nB- reffer to ophtha\nC- unrelated\nD- unrelated\n2718.LONG CASE AND PATIENT NEED SURGERY but it was mainly INR 6 and already given Vitamin K?\nwhat to give?\nA- FFP\nB- Cryoprecipitate\nC- Vit K\nD- 4-PCC", "id": 524, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}, {"q_num": 2749, "stem": "34-year-old woman at 27 weeks of gestation presented with preterm labor. She is started on Beta mimetics (tocolytics). Which of the following is the most common side effect?", "options": [{"letter": "A", "text": "Oliguria"}, {"letter": "B", "text": "Palpitations"}, {"letter": "C", "text": "Abdominal pain"}, {"letter": "D", "text": "Vaginal bleeding"}], "answer": "B", "explanation": "", "id": 525, "specialty": "Obstetrics & Gynecology"}];
